Thanks — this is enough to give useful, practical guidance. I’ll summarize what you can and can’t deduct, show how the home-office deduction would work, give a quick comparison of itemizing vs. the standard deduction, list the federal forms you’ll likely need, and call out a few clarifying questions I need to finalize numbers.

Quick summary / bottom line
- Likely best to take the 2024 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly ($29,200) unless you have other itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes, more charitable gifts, etc.). Your single charitable gift of $1,400 by itself won’t beat the standard deduction.
- Your consulting income can be treated as self-employment income (Schedule C). If so, you can take a home-office deduction for the exclusive 140 sq ft office and deduct business expenses related to the consulting work. Employees cannot take the home-office deduction.
- Student loan interest is an “above-the-line” deduction (Schedule 1), but the maximum deduction per year is $2,500 (not $3,600). Eligibility phases out at higher MAGI; based on the income you gave you’re likely under the phaseout and can take up to $2,500 if that amount was paid in 2024.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (the $950) are generally not deductible for W-2 employees under current law. If those $950 are actually business expenses related to the consulting work (self-employed), they are deductible on Schedule C.
- You’ll also owe self-employment tax on net consulting profit (Schedule SE). You may need to make estimated tax payments during the year if you expect tax to be owed.

Numbers and how I got them (rough)
- Total gross income (you reported): W-2 79,400 + consulting 12,500 + interest 920 + 480 = $93,300.
- Home-office share: 140 sq ft ÷ 690 sq ft = 0.2029 (≈20.29%).
- Annual rent: $2,100 × 12 = $25,200. Allocated portion for home office (actual-expense method): 25,200 × 0.2029 ≈ $5,116.
  - Alternative (simplified) method: $5 × 140 sq ft = $700 (cap applies). The actual method gives a bigger deduction here.
- If the $950 unreimbursed expenses are business-related, and you use the actual method, an example net self-employed income would be:
  - Gross consulting 12,500 − home-office allocation 5,116 − business expenses 950 = net ≈ $6,434.
  - Self-employment tax is ~15.3% on ~92.35% of net (use Schedule SE to calculate). Example SE tax ≈ $900 (very rough). Half of SE tax is an adjustment to income.
- Student loan interest: maximum deductible on your 2024 return is $2,500 (assuming you actually paid this in 2024 and your MAGI is below the phaseout). If the $3,600 is interest paid in prior years (2016–2019), only interest paid in 2024 matters for your 2024 return.

Which deductions are allowed vs not allowed
- Allowed (assuming consulting is self-employment):
  - Home-office deduction (Form 8829 or simplified method reported on Schedule C).
  - Ordinary and necessary business expenses related to the consulting (if the $950 pertains to that).
  - Student loan interest (up to $2,500, if eligible and paid in 2024).
  - Charitable donations only if you itemize (Schedule A).
- Not allowed (for most W-2 employees):
  - Unreimbursed employee expenses (suspended through 2025).
  - Home-office deduction for W-2 employees (unless you are an eligible statutory employee — rare).

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule 1 (additional income & adjustments — student loan interest deduction and the half SE tax adjustment appear here).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report consulting income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE — to compute self-employment tax.
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you use the actual-expense method for the home office). If you use the simplified method, you don’t file Form 8829; you just compute it on Schedule C.
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (charitable gifts, SALT, mortgage interest, etc.).
- Supporting forms you should collect: W-2 from Riverside Hospital; 1099-NEC or 1099-MISC (if issued) for consulting; 1099-INTs from Goldman Sachs Marcus and TD Ameritrade; Form 1098-E from MOHELA for student loan interest (if they issued one).

State returns
- File Arizona state return (AZ Form 140). Arizona allows many of the same adjustments; check state rules or a tax pro for specifics.

Practical recommendations and next steps
1. Clarify these items for me:
   - Was the consulting income received as an independent contractor (you got 1099-NEC) or as employee reimbursements? In other words, are you self-employed for that work?
   - Is the $950 in unreimbursed expenses related to the consulting work (i.e., business expenses)? If yes, gather receipts.
   - The $3,600 student loan interest you mentioned — was that interest paid in 2024 (and do you have a 2024 Form 1098-E)? Only interest paid in the tax year is deductible on this year’s return.
   - Any other deductions you haven’t listed (mortgage interest, property taxes, state income taxes paid, larger charitable donations, medical expenses, etc.)?
2. Gather documents:
   - W-2, any 1099-NEC, 1099-INTs, 1098-E (student loan interest), receipts for charitable contributions, receipts for consulting expenses, rent/utility records (for actual-method home-office allocation).
3. Decide home-office method:
   - Based on the numbers you gave, the actual-expense allocation (≈$5,116) beats the simplified method ($700). If your net self-employed income is small or negative, the actual deduction may be limited — Form 8829 and Schedule C rules cover carryovers if the deduction exceeds net profit.
4. Consider estimated tax payments:
   - If you expect to owe more than ~$1,000 in tax after withholding and credits, you should make quarterly estimated payments to avoid penalties (estimated payment responsibility comes from self-employment income).
5. If you want, I can:
   - Estimate your federal tax liability given the clarifications above.
   - Draft a checklist of documents to gather.
   - Or point you to a CPA/tax preparer familiar with self-employment and Arizona returns.

Quick conclusion
- Likely take the standard deduction ($29,200) for federal.
- Claim student loan interest up to $2,500 (if paid in 2024 and you meet the MAGI test).
- Deduct home-office expenses on Schedule C (if consulting is self-employment); actual method looks better given your rent and office size.
- Unreimbursed W-2 employee expenses are not deductible unless they are actually business expenses from your consulting work.

