I’m not a tax professional, but I can walk through the rules and give a solid, practical estimate of how your 2024 return will look and what you should gather. For definitive answers and to make sure you meet any phaseouts or state rules, check with a CPA or tax preparer.

Quick summary / bottom line
- Most likely you should take the 2024 standard deduction for married filing jointly (larger than the itemized deductions you listed).
- You can and should report your consulting income on Schedule C and you can claim a home-office deduction for the portion of your rented home used exclusively for the consulting business — that deduction reduces your self-employment income (and self-employment tax).
- Student loan interest is an above-the-line deduction only for interest paid in the tax year and is limited (see details below).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for W-2 work) are not deductible under current law.
- Forms you’ll need: Form 1040, Schedule 1 (adjustments), Schedule C (profit/loss — consulting), Schedule SE (self-employment tax), and possibly Form 8829 if you use the regular method for the home office. Also collect 1099s and 1098-E.

Details and estimates

1) Which items are deductible and how they’re treated
- Consulting income ($12,500): Report on Schedule C. You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses against it (home-office portion, supplies, mileage, etc.).
- Home office (140 sq ft exclusive; total 690 sq ft; renting $2,100/mo):
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified: $5/sq ft × 140 sq ft = $700 deduction.
    - Regular method: prorate actual expenses. Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200. Business % = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%. Home-office portion of rent ≈ $25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,113. You can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., the same way. For renters there’s generally no depreciation to worry about. If you use the regular method you’ll file Form 8829 (or otherwise report it on Schedule C).
  - Only available if the space is used regularly and exclusively for the business and it is your principal place of business (or used to meet clients).
- Self-employment tax: Net self-employment income (Schedule C profit) is subject to self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). You pay it with Schedule SE; half of that SE tax is deductible as an adjustment on Schedule 1.
- W-2 unreimbursed expenses: These are not deductible under current law (TCJA suspension through 2025), so the $950 unreimbursed employee expenses are generally not deductible unless they were business expenses for your self-employment (in which case they belong on Schedule C).
- Student loan interest ($3,600): Only interest paid in the tax year is potentially deductible (up to $2,500 per return) and the deduction phases out at higher incomes. You must have records/Form 1098-E from MOHELA for the year(s) you paid interest. If the $3,600 represents interest paid in earlier years (2016–2019), that cannot be deducted on your 2024 return — only interest paid in 2024 is relevant.
- Charitable gift ($1,400): Deductible on Schedule A if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction, you do not get an extra deduction for this cash gift. Keep the receipt.

2) Itemize vs standard (estimate)
- 2024 standard deduction (married filing jointly): $29,200 (verify with current IRS numbers).
- Your listed potential itemized deductions right now: charitable $1,400; unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible; you didn’t list mortgage interest, large medical expenses, or SALT up to $10k, so your likely itemized total is far less than the standard deduction.
- Conclusion: Unless you have additional itemizable amounts (significant state/local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, or other deductible items), you’ll almost certainly take the standard deduction.

3) Worked example (illustrative)
Assumptions: student loan interest deduction limited to $2,500 (max), home office taken by regular method, and the $950 are business expenses for consulting (if they are, include; if not, exclude them).
- Gross income: 79,400 (W-2) + 12,500 (consulting) + 1,400 (interest) = 93,300.
- Home office (regular method ≈): $5,113 allocated to Schedule C.
- Schedule C net (ignoring other business expenses): 12,500 − 5,113 = 7,387.
- Self-employment tax base = 7,387 × 0.9235 ≈ 6,819; SE tax ≈ 6,819 × 15.3% ≈ $1,043. Half SE tax ~ $522 deductible on Schedule 1.
- Student loan interest (if paid in 2024 and you qualify) — up to $2,500 deduction on Schedule 1.
- Adjusted gross income example: 93,300 − 2,500 (student loan) − 522 (half SE tax) ≈ 90,278.
- Subtract standard deduction (≈29,200) → taxable income ≈ 61,078.
This is only illustrative; precise tax and credits depend on full details.

4) Forms you’ll typically prepare or expect
- Federal: Form 1040.
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income: student loan interest, half SE tax, etc.).
- Schedule C (profit or loss from business) for consulting income and home-office deduction (or report simplified home-office on Schedule C).
- Schedule SE for self-employment tax.
- Form 8829 if using regular method for business use of home (often used for allocation calculations).
- 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC if clients issued them (you must report income even if no 1099).
- 1099-INTs for interest (Goldman Sachs, TD Ameritrade).
- 1098-E from MOHELA for student loan interest (if interest was paid in 2024).
- Arizona state return: AZ form(s). State rules differ — check AZ Dept. of Revenue or your preparer.

5) Other items and potential opportunities
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A): you may be eligible for up to 20% of qualified business income from your consulting on top of the above — eligibility and limits depend on taxable income and type of business. This can affect whether it’s worth maximizing Schedule C deductions; ask a preparer.
- Estimated taxes: because you have self-employment income, you may owe quarterly estimated taxes in the year (or have tax due at filing). If you had enough withholding from your W-2 job, that may cover some of it.
- Keep good records: receipts for charitable gifts, 1098-E for student loan interest, records showing exclusive use of home office (floor plan, photos, logs), rent and utilities statements, and any business-related receipts.

6) Questions I need to confirm to refine calculations
- Was the $3,600 in student loan interest paid in 2024? (Only interest paid in the tax year counts.)
- Are the $950 “unreimbursed expenses” actually business expenses for the consulting (and not employee unreimbursed expenses for the W-2 job)?
- Do you have any mortgage interest, state/local taxes, large unreimbursed medical expenses, or other deductions that might push itemizing above $29,200?
- Do you have any retirement contributions (IRA, SEP-IRA), dependents, or credits we should consider?
- Do any clients issue 1099-NEC? Did you receive 1099-NEC or 1099-MISC for the consulting?

If you want, I can:
- Run a more precise, line-by-line estimate if you give answers to the clarifying questions above.
- Draft an email you can send to your tax preparer with the facts and questions.
- Create a checklist of documents to collect (1099s, 1098-E, rent statements, receipts, etc.).

