I’m not a CPA or tax preparer, but I can walk you through how federal tax rules generally apply to your situation and what to check/prepare before filing. I’ll cover (1) what’s likely deductible, (2) whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, (3) how to treat the home office, (4) required forms, and (5) next steps and questions I need to be more precise.

1) What you can (probably) deduct
- Self-employment (consulting) expenses: The $12,500 consulting is reported on Schedule C. Business expenses (your $950 unreimbursed business expenses) can be deducted on Schedule C if they are ordinary/necessary business expenses (supplies, software, phone portion, mileage, etc.). Unreimbursed employee expenses for a W-2 job are not deductible federally under current law.
- Home office: If you use 140 sq ft exclusively and regularly for the consulting business, you can take a home office deduction on Schedule C. See section 3 for methods and a quick estimate.
- Student loan interest: Only interest you actually paid during the tax year (2024) is potentially deductible as an adjustment to income (above-the-line), up to the legal limit (generally up to $2,500). You must have a 1098-E for the tax year showing interest paid. Interest paid in prior years (2016–2019) cannot be deducted on your 2024 return; there’s no carryforward.
- Charitable donations: The $1,400 gift to Northwest Harvest is deductible on Schedule A if you itemize. Keep the receipt.
- Investment interest/interest income: The $920 (Goldman Sachs Marcus) and $480 (TD Ameritrade) are taxable interest (reported on 1099-INT). If total taxable interest and dividends exceed $1,500 or you have certain foreign accounts, you may need Schedule B — your total interest here ($1,400) is under $1,500, so probably no Schedule B is required for interest alone. If TD Ameritrade amount is dividends or there are brokerage transactions, there may be 1099-DIV or 1099-B items to report (capital gains).

2) Standard deduction vs. itemize
- For married filing jointly most taxpayers find the standard deduction is larger unless they have sizable mortgage interest, large state/local taxes (SALT — capped at $10,000), big charitable gifts, or large medical expenses above the threshold.
- Your only clear itemizable amounts provided: $1,400 charitable donation (plus any state income tax or property taxes you paid, mortgage interest, etc., which you didn’t list). With just $1,400 in charity, itemizing is very unlikely to beat the standard deduction.
- Action: Add up your potential Schedule A items (charity, state income tax withheld, property taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses over the floor). If the total exceeds the standard deduction for MFJ, itemize; otherwise take the standard deduction.

3) Home office treatment (critical because it’s the biggest single deduction here)
You have two choices:

A. Simplified method
- $5 per qualifying square foot up to 300 sq ft.
- For 140 sq ft: 140 * $5 = $700 deduction (simple, no allocation of rent/utilities).

B. Regular method (recommended here because it looks larger)
- Calculate the business-use percentage = home office sq ft / total home sq ft = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.3%.
- Deduct business portion of allowable home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance portion, etc.). For renters you cannot take depreciation on the home; you can take your share of rent and utilities.
- Example using only rent: annual rent = $2,100 * 12 = $25,200. Business portion = 25,200 * 20.3% ≈ $5,116. That’s substantially larger than the simplified $700.
- Regular method requires recordkeeping (rent receipts, utility bills, proof of exclusive use). The home office deduction reduces Schedule C net profit, which also reduces self-employment tax.

4) Self-employment tax and QBI
- Your estimated Schedule C net: 12,500 gross - (home office portion ~$5,116 + $950 other expenses) ≈ $6,434 net business income.
- Self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) is assessed on ~92.35% of net earnings. Rough estimate: SE tax ≈ $909, and half of that (about $455) is an above-the-line deduction on Schedule 1.
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: you may be eligible for up to 20% of qualified business income (about 20% of your net business income after expenses), claimed via Form 8995 (or 8995-A if more complex). With a small net business amount, expect a modest QBI deduction (~$1,200–1,300 in rough estimate), which reduces taxable income (not SE tax).
- You may need to make (or have made) estimated tax payments during the year to cover self-employment tax + income tax, or increase W-2 withholding to cover the tax.

5) Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income/adjustments) — for the student loan interest adjustment (if eligible) and half SE tax adjustment
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report consulting income and business expenses, including home office
- Schedule SE — to calculate self-employment tax
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — for QBI/Section 199A deduction (if applicable)
