- Interest / brokerage: $920 (Goldman Sachs Marcus) + $480 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,400 (report as interest/dividend income as appropriate)

Key points and recommendations
1. Filing status and standard deduction
- You’re Married Filing Jointly.
- For tax year 2024 the standard deduction for MFJ is $29,200 (IRS inflation adjustments).  
- With only $1,400 in charitable contributions and no mortgage interest or large state/local taxes listed, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction rather than itemizing Schedule A. (Home‑office deductions do NOT force you to itemize — they reduce business income on Schedule C whether you itemize or not.)

2. Self‑employment (consulting) income
- Report the $12,500 on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C — this includes the home‑office deduction (see next) and any business‑related receipts (supplies, advertising, software, part of phone/internet if used for business, etc.).
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll owe Social Security/Medicare (Schedule SE) on net self‑employment income (after business expenses). You can deduct half of self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040.

3. Home office deduction — what you can do and recommended method
- You stated an exclusive office of 140 sq ft and total home 690 sq ft. Office percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%.
- You pay $2,100/month (assume rent) → annual rent = $25,200.
- Regular method (recommended here): home office portion of rent = 25,200 × 20.29% = $5,112. That $5,112 is a business expense on Schedule C (and you’d prepare Form 8829 to support it).
- Simplified method: $5 × 140 sq ft = $700.
- Because your rent is high, the regular method ($5,112) will give you a much bigger deduction than the simplified method ($700). Use Form 8829 if using the regular method.
- Note: The home office deduction reduces your net Schedule C income, which reduces both income tax and self‑employment tax.

4. Student loan interest
- The student loan interest deduction is capped at $2,500 per year (adjustment to income on Schedule 1), subject to MAGI phase‑outs. You paid $3,600 to MOHELA (2016–2019 loans) — the maximum deductible is $2,500 (you cannot deduct the full $3,600).
- Given your approximate AGI (rough estimate below), you will likely be under the phaseout for MFJ and entitled to the full $2,500, but verify with exact MAGI. (If your MAGI exceeds the phaseout threshold you may get a partial or no deduction.)

5. Charitable contributions
