Thanks — this is enough to give a clear, practical run-through. I’ll (1) explain what’s deductible, (2) show whether you’ll likely itemize or take the standard deduction, (3) explain the home-office options and how they affect your self-employment numbers, and (4) list the federal forms you’ll typically need (plus a quick note on the Arizona return). I’ll also flag a few clarifying questions at the end.

Short upfront conclusion
- With the numbers you gave and no other large deductible items (mortgage interest, big medical expenses, large state & local taxes, or bigger charitable gifts), you will almost certainly take the 2024 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly (2024 MFJ standard deduction = $29,200).
- Your consulting income should be reported on Schedule C and you can claim a home-office deduction for the 140 sq ft exclusive space. That deduction is taken on Schedule C (either simplified or regular method), not on Schedule A.
- Student loan interest is only deductible for interest paid in the tax year. If the $3,600 was paid in 2016–2019, it is NOT deductible on your 2024 return. If you actually paid $3,600 in 2024, the maximum student-loan-interest deduction is $2,500 (and it phases out with higher MAGI).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (the $950) are NOT deductible for most employees on the federal return under current law (through 2025). If those were business expenses for your consulting work, they belong on Schedule C instead.

Quick numbers and comparison (approximate)
Inputs you gave:
- W-2 (Riverside Hospital): $79,400
- Consulting income (self-employed, 6 clients): $12,500 (I’ll treat this as gross self-employment income)
- Interest: Goldman Sachs Marcus $920 + TD Ameritrade $480 = $1,400
- Rent: $2,100/month for 690 sq ft (annual rent $25,200)
- Home office: 140 sq ft exclusive
- Charitable cash gifts: $1,400 (with receipt)
- Student loan interest: $3,600 (clarify year paid — see note below)
- Unreimbursed expenses: $950 (likely nondeductible unless self-employed)

Total gross income = 79,400 + 12,500 + 1,400 = $93,300.

Home-office methods — impact on Schedule C and resulting tax effects
You can choose:
A) Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft
- 140 sq ft × $5 = $700 deduction (reported directly on Schedule C)

B) Regular (actual-expense) method: allocate a percentage of real expenses to business use; for rent-based allocation:
- Business-use percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%
- Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200
- Rent portion for office ≈ $25,200 × 0.2029 ≈ $5,115 (you could also allocate portions of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if you have records — they would increase the deduction)

Comparing the two (rounded, rough illustration):
- Simplified method: Schedule C net income ≈ 12,500 − 700 = 11,800
  - Self-employment tax (approx): ~$1,668, half deductible ≈ $834
  - Potential QBI (≈20% of qualified business income after adjustments): roughly $2,190 (this is an approximate; see QBI notes below)
- Regular method (rent allocation): Schedule C net income ≈ 12,500 − 5,115 = 7,385
  - Self-employment tax (approx): ~$1,043, half deductible ≈ $522
  - Potential QBI (≈20%): roughly $1,373

Which method is better?
- Regular method gives a larger direct home-office deduction, but because QBI is 20% of business income (and simplified leaves more business income), the overall taxable-income result can be similar or the simplified method can even be slightly more favorable in some cases.
- If you have no other home-related expenses to allocate (utilities, repairs, insurance), the simplified method is easier and often preferable for small home-office areas. If you have significant eligible home expenses to allocate, calculate both ways; if the regular method yields a bigger deduction overall, use it.
