1) How to treat each item (what’s deductible and where)
- Self‑employment income and business expenses
  - Report on Schedule C (Form 1040). All consulting income must be reported even if you didn’t get 1099s.
  - Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C. The $950 unreimbursed expenses (if they are business expenses) are deductible on Schedule C.
- Home‑office
  - You can take a home‑office deduction because you said the 140 sq ft area is used exclusively for the business.
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified method: $5.00 × 140 sq ft = $700 (direct deduction on Schedule C).
    - Regular method: prorate actual home expenses by the business percentage = 140 / 690 = 20.29%. Rent portion alone = $25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,114 (plus proportional share of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs if applicable). Regular method almost certainly gives a larger deduction here. Regular method uses Form 8829 (or the Schedule C equivalent if you’re a renter; Form 8829 is for homeowners but renters still prorate rent on Schedule C — your tax software/CPA will show the exact flow).
  - Home‑office reduces your net Schedule C profit, which also lowers self‑employment tax and taxable income.
- Self‑employment tax
  - You’ll owe self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net Schedule C profit; calculate on Schedule SE. You get to deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
  - You may be eligible for a 20% QBI deduction (Form 8995) on qualified net business income. With your income level it’s likely allowed, but the exact amount depends on final net profit and taxable income.
- Interest income
  - Report bank/broker interest on Form 1040. Total $1,400 is below the Schedule B $1,500 reporting threshold, so Schedule B usually not required (but report amounts shown on 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV).
- Student loan interest
  - The student loan interest deduction is an adjustment to income (Schedule 1) and is limited to $2,500 per return (not $3,600). Also you can only deduct the interest actually paid during the tax year (and you must meet the MAGI phaseout rules — with your income you should be eligible).
  - Clarify: Was the $3,600 amount interest you paid in 2024? If yes, the deductible amount is capped at $2,500 (and you’d need the 1098‑E or loan statements).
- Charitable contribution ($1,400)
  - Deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you cannot also deduct this on federal return (no special universal cash charity deduction applies for 2024).
- Itemized vs standard deduction
  - 2024 standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly) = $29,200.
  - Likely you should take the standard deduction because the only obvious potential itemized deduction you listed is $1,400 charitable contributions; unless you have large state & local taxes, mortgage interest, or other big medical expenses, itemizing is unlikely to exceed $29,200.
  - Note: the home office deduction is NOT an itemized deduction — it reduces your business income on Schedule C whether or not you itemize.

