- Unreimbursed employee expenses for W‑2 work are not deductible (suspended under current law). But if the $950 is an expense of your consulting business, it belongs on Schedule C instead.

More detail and calculations you can use
1) Income to report
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital: $79,400 (reported on Form 1040, W‑2 attached).  
- Consulting: $12,500 — report on Schedule C (or on partnership/K‑1 if arranged differently). If you received 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, include those.  
- Interest: $920 (Goldman Sachs Marcus) + $480 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,400 interest — report on Form 1040 (Schedule B only required if interest + dividends > $1,500).

2) Home office
- Because you have consulting income, you can use the home office deduction if the space is used regularly and exclusively for the business and is your principal place of business. Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × 140 sq ft = $700 deduction.
  - Actual expense method: allocate a percent of actual home expenses to the business. Your allocation = 140 / 690 = ~20.3%. You pay $2,100/month = $25,200/year in rent. 20.3% of rent ≈ $5,117 — that would be deductible as a business expense (plus an allocable share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if you have them). If you use actual expenses you normally complete Form 8829 (for business use of home) and transfer the result to Schedule C. The simplified method is easier and has no Form 8829.
- Using the home office reduces Schedule C net profit (reduces income tax and SE tax).

3) Unreimbursed expenses ($950)
- If these are expenses related to your consulting (supplies, travel, software, phone, etc.), include them on Schedule C as ordinary business expenses. If they are unreimbursed expenses tied to your W‑2 employment, they are not deductible on federal returns under current law.

4) Student loan interest ($3,600)
- Only interest actually paid in the tax year is deductible and the deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment (claimed on Schedule 1). The maximum allowable student loan interest deduction is subject to a statutory cap (commonly $2,500) and income phase‑outs for married filing jointly. You’ll need Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer to substantiate interest paid in the tax year. Clarify whether the $3,600 was paid in 2024 or earlier years.

