Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can help. Below is a clear, practical rundown for your 2024 return given what you told me, plus a few follow-up questions so I can give a tighter estimate if you want.

1) Income summary (from what you gave)
- W-2 (Riverside Hospital): $79,400
- Consulting (self‑employment): $12,500
- Interest (Goldman Sachs Marcus): $920
- TD Ameritrade: $480
Total gross income = $93,300

2) What you can (generally) deduct
- Self‑employment/business expenses (Schedule C): deductible against the $12,500 consulting income. That includes:
  - Home office (see details below)
  - Supplies, software, internet/business phone portion, advertising, contractor costs, and your $950 if those are business/unreimbursed consulting expenses (not employee unreimbursed expenses).
- Self‑employment tax deduction: you may deduct half of your SE tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (calculated on Schedule SE). Rough estimate: on $12,500 gross you’d owe ~ $1,765 SE tax and be able to deduct ~ $883 (half) — exact numbers depend on net profit after business expenses.
- Student loan interest: only interest you actually paid in 2024 can be claimed on the 2024 return, and the maximum yearly deduction is $2,500 (it’s an above‑the‑line deduction reported on Schedule 1). Interest paid in earlier years (2016–2019) cannot be claimed now. Also subject to MAGI phaseouts — if your modified AGI is over the phaseout range the deduction phases out/ends.
- Charitable gift: $1,400 to Northwest Harvest is deductible if you itemize (cash gift to a qualified charity). Keep the receipt.
- State & local taxes (SALT): state income tax or state sales tax and property tax are deductible on Schedule A if you itemize, but combined SALT deduction is capped at $10,000.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: generally NOT deductible for most employees under current law (suspended through 2025). If the $950 are expenses for your consulting (self‑employed) work, they are deductible on Schedule C instead.

3) Home office — how to treat it
- You meet the basic facts test (exclusive use of 140 sq ft for business). Only self‑employed taxpayers can take the home office deduction (employees generally cannot).
- Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × square feet (max 300 sq ft). For 140 sq ft = $700 deduction. Easy, fewer records, and cannot create a business loss beyond your net profit (i.e., limited to your business profit).
  - Regular/actual method: pro‑rata share of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, insurance, depreciation, etc.). Your rent: $2,100 × 12 = $25,200 × (140/690 ≈ 20.29%) ≈ $5,111 of rent allocable to the office alone (plus a proportionate share of utilities, etc.). That may give a larger deduction, but the total home office deduction is generally limited to net profit from that business (and requires Form 8829 to calculate).
- Recommendation: if you have no other business expenses and your consulting net is only $12,500, the simplified method ($700) is safe and avoids complexity; consider actual method only if you have substantial other business expenses and want to claim depreciation or larger allocable costs.

4) Itemize vs standard deduction
- For 2024 the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is $29,200.
- Rough comparison using items you listed:
  - Charitable: $1,400
  - State tax: unknown (but capped with other SALT at $10,000)
  - Mortgage interest / property tax: you didn’t list any
If your only itemized amounts are the $1,400 charity (+ modest SALT), your itemized total will very likely be well below $29,200. Therefore you will almost certainly take the standard deduction unless you have significant additional deductible amounts (large state tax or mortgage interest or big medical expenses exceeding the floor, etc.).

5) Forms you’ll generally need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 — for adjustments to income (e.g., student loan interest deduction if you qualify).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $12,500 consulting income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE — to calculate self‑employment tax and the deductible half of SE tax.
- Form 8829 — only if you use the regular (actual) home office method.
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (unlikely based on the numbers you gave).
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — for Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (20% of qualified business income) if applicable; many small self‑employed taxpayers use Form 8995 (simpler) but eligibility depends on taxable income and type of business.
- Supporting forms you should receive/keep: W‑2 from Riverside Hospital, 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC from consulting clients (if issued), 1099‑INT (Goldman Sachs), 1099‑DIV or 1099‑B (TD Ameritrade), and 1098‑E (student loan interest) if you paid interest in 2024.

State return
- Arizona individual income tax return (Form 140 for full‑year residents). Arizona rules differ from federal in places — check state treatment of student loan interest and other items.

6) Actions and recommendations
- Clarify these points so I (or a tax preparer) can give a precise bottom‑line estimate:
  - How much student loan interest did you actually pay in 2024? (You should receive a 1098‑E from MOHELA for 2024.)
  - Are the $950 unreimbursed expenses for your consulting business (self‑employment) or are they employee expenses from your W‑2 job?
  - Did you pay any state income tax or property tax in 2024? Any mortgage interest?
  - Do you receive 1099s from any of the six consulting clients?
- If you want a quick estimate right now I can run a rough taxable-income calculation using assumptions (e.g., student loan interest = 0 or $2,500; take simplified home office) — tell me which assumptions to use.
- Keep receipts/records: charity receipt, MOHELA 1098‑E, documentation for business expenses and home office calculations.

7) Caveats
- This is general guidance based on current federal rules as of mid‑2024. Complexities can arise (QBI phaseouts, limits on losses, state differences). For significant ambiguity or if your situation might trigger limits (e.g., trying to create a Schedule C loss), I recommend consulting a CPA or enrolled agent.

If you want, I can:
- Run a quick numerical tax estimate under plausible assumptions (you tell me whether to assume student loan interest paid in 2024, whether the $950 are business expenses, and whether you want simplified home office), or
- Draft a checklist of documents to gather for your preparer.

Which would you like next?

