I can help — here’s a clear, practical summary of what’s deductible, how to treat the home office, whether itemizing likely helps, and which forms you’ll need. I’ll note assumptions where needed and ask a couple quick clarifying questions at the end.

High-level summary
- Report all income: W-2 wages ($79,400), consulting income ($12,500 — report on Schedule C), and interest income ($920 + $480 = $1,400 — report on Form 1040; you’ll receive 1099-INTs).
- Self-employment (consulting) income is subject to income tax and self-employment (SE) tax (~15.3% on net earnings). You’ll file Schedule C and Schedule SE.
- Home office deduction is available only for the self-employed portion (not for a W-2 employee), and only if the space is used regularly and exclusively for business.
- Student loan interest is an “above-the-line” adjustment (subject to limits), so you can often take it even if you take the standard deduction.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for a W-2 job) are not deductible for most taxpayers under current law. If the $950 is business-related (consulting) and unreimbursed, it should be claimed on Schedule C.
- Charitable gift ($1,400) is deductible only if you itemize. If total itemized deductions are less than the standard deduction, take the standard deduction and you cannot deduct the $1,400 on Schedule A.

Home office: how to treat it (two options)
1. Simplified method
- $5.00 per square foot up to 300 sq ft.
- For 140 sq ft: 140 × $5 = $700 deduction.
- Easy, no detailed allocation of rent/utilities, no Form 8829.

2. Regular method (actual-expense allocation)
- Business-use percentage = home-office area / total home area = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%.
- You can deduct the business portion of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. Example using only rent: $2,100 × 12 = $25,200 annual rent × 20.29% ≈ $5,111 allowed for the office portion (plus proportional utilities if you have them).
- You report business-use-of-home on Form 8829 (and attach to Schedule C). Note: home-office deduction cannot exceed net profit from the business; any excess may carry forward.

Which method to choose?
- Regular method typically gives a bigger deduction here (example: ~$5,100 vs $700) if your net consulting profit before the home-office allocation is large enough to absorb it.
- But if your consulting net income is small (or becomes small after other business expenses), the home-office deduction may be limited under the regular method; the simplified method is simpler and has no carryover rules.
- I can run a quick comparison if you want me to use your exact expense numbers.

Student loan interest
- The maximum federal student loan interest deduction is $2,500 (subject to MAGI phaseouts). You reported $3,600 — you can only deduct up to the allowed maximum for the year you actually paid interest, and only if you meet the income limits. Check whether you received Form 1098-E from MOHELA showing interest paid in the tax year.
- If your modified AGI is well under the phaseout range (for MFJ) you should be able to claim up to $2,500.

Charitable donation
- $1,400 to a qualified charity with a receipt: deductible on Schedule A only if you itemize. Keep the receipt (acknowledgment for donations $250+ is required).

Unreimbursed expenses ($950)
- If these are job-related expenses for your W-2 employment: not deductible currently (suspended).
- If they are ordinary and necessary expenses for your consulting business, report them on Schedule C as business expenses.

Self-employment tax & QBI
- Net self-employment earnings are subject to SE tax. You’ll owe SE tax (calculated on Schedule SE), and half of the SE tax is an above-the-line deduction on Form 1040.
- You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction under Section 199A; with $12,500 gross consulting income QBI might be modest, but it depends on net profit and other factors. Small businesses often use Form 8995 to claim QBI.

Standard deduction vs itemize (which is likely best for you)
- For most married filing jointly taxpayers with no mortgage interest and modest charitable giving, the standard deduction is larger than their Schedule A total. With only $1,400 charitable contributions (and assuming no large medical expenses, mortgage interest, or big state/local taxes beyond the SALT cap), you will most likely take the standard deduction.
- Independent of that decision, student loan interest (up to the allowed amount) and half of the SE tax reduce AGI even if you take the standard deduction.

Forms you will likely need (Federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital
- 1099-NEC / records for consulting income (Schedule C)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report consulting income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular home-office method
