2) Student loan interest
- The maximum student loan interest deduction per year is $2,500 (not $3,600). Also it only applies to interest you actually paid in the tax year being filed and phases out at higher MAGI levels. With MAGI in the low‑to‑mid $90k range for MFJ, you’re well under the phaseout threshold, so you should be eligible for the full allowed amount (up to $2,500) — but not the full $3,600 you listed. I need to know whether the $3,600 was actually paid in the filing year; if so, you can deduct up to $2,500 (the excess isn’t deductible).

3) Charitable contribution
- The $1,400 cash gift to Northwest Harvest is an itemized deduction (Schedule A) if you itemize. Keep the receipt — good documentation.

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($950)
- If these are business expenses for your consulting work, they belong on Schedule C and reduce your Schedule C net profit (fully deductible).  
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses tied to your W‑2 job, they are not deductible under current law (suspended miscellaneous itemized deductions).

5) Home office
- Because you are self‑employed (consulting), you can take a home office deduction for the exclusive 140 sq ft area. Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 140 sq ft × $5 = $700.
  - Regular (actual) method: the business percentage × allowable home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.). From what you provided:
    - Business percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%.  
    - If you only count rent ($25,200/yr), the home‑office share of rent ≈ $25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,114. (If you also have proportionate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., that would increase the deduction under actual method.)
  - Typically the actual method will give you a far larger deduction here (≈$5,100 vs. $700), assuming you have no other home expenses to complicate depreciation rules. To use the actual method you generally complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and attach it to Schedule C. You can use the simplified method without Form 8829 (just enter the computed amount on Schedule C).
  - Important limitation: the home office deduction for a sole proprietor cannot create a net Schedule C loss that you cannot offset elsewhere (there are loss limitation rules). But with $12,500 gross and likely a few hundred of other business expenses, a $5k office deduction will usually just lower your net profit rather than produce a disallowed loss.

