- Itemizable items you listed: cash charitable = $1,400. (Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line adjustment, not an itemized deduction; self‑employment tax deduction likewise above‑the‑line.) You didn’t list mortgage interest, property taxes, large state taxes, or big medical expenses. So your itemized total looks like about $1,400 (unless you have other itemizable amounts).
- The standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly for tax year 2024 is substantially larger than that (inflation‑adjusted; roughly in the high $20k range). So in your situation you will almost certainly take the standard deduction rather than itemize.
- Net effect: you’ll take the standard deduction on Schedule A (i.e., you won’t use Schedule A except to report if you did itemize), and you’ll still claim above‑the‑line deductions (student loan interest up to allowed amount, half of SE tax).

9) Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
