Important: this is general guidance, not tax advice. For final filing or if you have unusual facts, check with a CPA or tax preparer.

1) Summary of income you listed
- W-2 wages: $79,400 (Riverside Hospital) — report on Form 1040 (W-2).
- Consulting: $12,500 — this looks like self‑employment income; report on Schedule C (profit/loss). If you were an employee and this was unreimbursed employee expense, different rules apply — see questions below.
- Interest: $920 (Goldman Sachs Marcus) + $480 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,400 — taxable interest, reported on Form 1040 (and Schedule B only if total interest/dividends > $1,500).
  
2) Charitable gifts
- $1,400 to Northwest Harvest (you have receipt) — deductible as an itemized charitable contribution on Schedule A if you itemize. Keep the receipt.
- Note: cash charitable limits are high (percentage of AGI), so $1,400 is fine.

3) Student loan interest ($3,600 stated)
- The student loan interest deduction is an above-the-line adjustment (Claimed on Schedule 1 of Form 1040), not an itemized deduction.
- The maximum deduction allowed per year is $2,500 (subject to MAGI phaseouts). So you cannot deduct the full $3,600; at most $2,500 for the tax year, assuming you meet income limits and the interest was paid in the tax year.
- You should receive Form 1098-E from your servicer if > $600 interest was paid; keep records. Clarify whether the $3,600 is interest paid in 2024 (and not old years).

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($950)
- If these are unreimbursed employee business expenses (you are an employee of the hospital), miscellaneous employee expenses are not deductible for tax years through 2025 (suspended by TCJA); so they would not be deductible on Schedule A.
- If the $950 are business expenses related to your consulting (self‑employment), you can deduct them on Schedule C (and they reduce self‑employment tax and income tax). Keep receipts and records.

5) Home office (140 sq ft exclusive use; apartment 690 sq ft; rent $2,100/mo)
- You can claim a home office deduction only if you use the space regularly and exclusively for business and your consulting qualifies as self‑employment (Schedule C). If you are an employee and work from home for your employer, you generally cannot take this deduction.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft × 140 sq ft = $700 (easy).
  b) Actual expenses method: allocate your actual indirect expenses (rent, utilities, insurance, etc.) by the business-use percentage. Your business-use percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%.
     - Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200. Home-office portion of rent ≈ $25,200 × 0.2029 ≈ $5,113.
     - You could also allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. Direct expenses for the office (furniture, repairs) are fully deductible for the business portion.
     - If you use the actual method and are a sole proprietor, report the deduction on Form 8829 (and Schedule C). The actual method will likely give you a far larger deduction than the simplified method given your rent.
- Recommendation: If the consulting is self‑employment, you’ll likely want to use the actual method (and file Form 8829) because the rent allocation (~$5,100) is much larger than the simplified $700. If you have additional home-related expenses (utilities) to allocate, actual method becomes even more favorable.

6) Self-employment tax and related adjustments
- Net self‑employment earnings (Schedule C) are subject to self‑employment tax (~15.3% on net earnings after the 0.9235 adjustment). You also get to deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an above‑the‑line deduction on Schedule 1.
- Business expenses (home office, that $950 if business-related, supplies, phone, mileage, etc.) reduce your Schedule C net income.

7) Standard deduction vs. itemizing (for 2024)
- 2024 standard deduction (IRS inflation‑adjusted amounts): Married filing jointly = $29,200 (check current IRS tables when you file).
- Your itemizable items that you listed: charitable $1,400. Student loan interest (up to $2,500) is an above-the-line adjustment (reduces AGI) — it does NOT count toward Schedule A.
- Unless you have other significant itemizable items (mortgage interest, large state and local taxes up to $10,000 SALT cap, big medical expenses, etc.), your itemized total (around $1,400) is far less than the standard deduction of $29,200, so you’ll almost certainly take the standard deduction.
- Bottom line: based on what you provided, take the standard deduction (MFJ) and claim student loan interest on Schedule 1 (up to $2,500) and claim business-related deductions on Schedule C (including home office and the $950 if it’s business expenses).

8) Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual return).
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for student loan interest adjustment, and half of self-employment tax deduction flows here.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $12,500 consulting income and associated expenses.
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to calculate self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual expense method for the home office; if using simplified method, you do not file 8829.
- Form 8995 or 8995-A — for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (20% of qualified business income) if you’re eligible (small-business income may qualify; eligibility depends on taxable income and type of business).
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (unlikely given the numbers you shared).
- Schedule B — only if interest/dividend totals exceed $1,500 (your total interest is $1,400 so likely not required).
- Keep Form W-2 (hospital), Form 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC for consulting (if provided), Form 1099-INT (for $920 and $480), and Form 1098-E (student loan interest) if issued.

9) Arizona state return
- You’ll also file an Arizona individual return (Form 140 or the correct AZ version) — Arizona rules differ in some respects from federal rules; state itemized deductions/conformity may differ. If you itemize federally, check state treatment; if you take the standard deduction federally, check the state standard deduction rules. Consider a preparer familiar with AZ state rules.

10) Other practical points and next steps
- Gather documents: W-2, 1099-NEC/1099-MISC, 1099-INT, 1098-E, receipts for charity, receipts for business expenses, records of rent payments and square footage (floor plan/photos are useful), any records of estimated tax payments you may have made.
- Estimated tax payments: since you have self‑employment income, you may need to make quarterly estimated tax payments (to avoid penalties). For the current filing year, consider whether you should pay estimated taxes for future quarters.
- Consider QBI: your $12,500 business income (after deductions) may be eligible for the 20% QBI deduction; that reduces taxable income and can be significant.
