I can walk through this and give clear, practical guidance — but first a couple quick clarifying questions so my recommendations are accurate:

1. Are the $12,500 consulting payments self‑employment income (you received 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC or you consider yourself an independent contractor)? Or were they wages reported on a W‑2?  
2. The $3,600 student‑loan interest — did you pay that interest in 2024 (tax year you’re filing) or are those amounts paid in earlier years (2016–2019 as you wrote)? Only interest paid in the tax year is potentially deductible. Do you have a 1098‑E for 2024 from MOHELA?  
3. Are the $950 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses related to the consulting activity (e.g., tools, supplies, travel) or are they unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job? (Unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible on federal returns through at least 2025.)  
4. Any mortgage interest, real estate taxes, or large state income taxes you paid in 2024? Any other charitable gifts or deductions to add?

Assuming basic facts from what you gave, here’s how each item generally works and what I recommend:

Income
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital: $79,400 — reported on Form 1040, Box 1.  
- Consulting: $12,500 — if self‑employment (1099 or you ran it as independent contractor), report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). If it was wages, it would be on a W‑2 and handled on Form 1040.  
- Interest: $920 (Goldman Sachs Marcus) + $480 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,400 — taxable interest to include on Schedule B (if required) and Form 1040.

Self‑employment rules (if consulting is self‑employment)
- Report income and ordinary business expenses on Schedule C.  
- Pay self‑employment tax on net profit (Schedule SE). Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% on 92.35% of net earnings (roughly 14.13% of net self‑employment income). Half of the self‑employment tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).  
- Business expenses (including the home‑office portion of rent/utilities if you qualify) reduce net self‑employment income and thus reduce income tax and SE tax.

Home office
- You say the office is exclusive and 140 sq ft of a 690 sq ft home. That fraction = 140/690 ≈ 20.29%.  
- Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 per sq ft × 140 sq ft = $700 deduction (no Form 8829). Simple, no audit paperwork beyond basic support.  
  - Regular method: allocate actual expenses by business percentage. For rent: $2,100/month × 12 = $25,200/year × 20.29% ≈ $5,113 that could be allocated to the home‑office deduction (plus your allocated portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.). Use Form 8829 to calculate and report this; the result flows to Schedule C.
- Important: home‑office deduction only available to self‑employed persons (and certain non‑employee circumstances); it is not allowed for W‑2 employees under current law. Also, the home‑office deduction cannot be used to create or increase a business loss for some situations — excess may be carried forward. If your consulting is self‑employment, the regular method will likely give a bigger deduction than simplified, but it’s more paperwork and you must have enough net business income to use it.

Student loan interest
- The student loan interest deduction is an “above‑the‑line” deduction (reported on Schedule 1) up to $2,500 for qualifying interest paid during the tax year. It phases out at higher MAGI (limits vary year-to-year). You can’t deduct interest paid in earlier years on a current year return. You need Form 1098‑E from the loan servicer to support the deduction.
- If you truly paid $3,600 in 2024 (and meet the MAGI limits), you’d be limited to $2,500 deductible on your 2024 return. If those payments were 2016–2019, they’re not deductible on your 2024 return.

Charitable contribution
- $1,400 to Northwest Harvest is deductible if you itemize and the charity is a qualified organization. Keep the receipt. Charitable gifts only reduce taxable income if you itemize on Schedule A. Note: the standard deduction often exceeds small itemized totals.

Unreimbursed expenses ($950)
- If these are business expenses related to consulting, they’re deductible on Schedule C. If they’re unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are not deductible on federal returns (suspended under current law).

Itemize or take standard deduction?
- For Married Filing Jointly you compare total itemized deductions (Schedule A) vs the standard deduction. (I can run the math once you confirm the tax year’s standard deduction for 2024 — IRS inflation adjustments apply each year.)  
- Based on the items you listed (charitable $1,400, no mortgage interest or property tax info provided), you are very likely better off taking the standard deduction unless you have additional itemizable amounts (significant mortgage interest, real estate taxes, or large state/local taxes up to the SALT limit of $10,000). So my preliminary recommendation: most likely take the standard deduction, but confirm other itemizable amounts.

Forms you will likely need
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 (main return)  
  - Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — student loan interest if deductible, and other adjustments  
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — if consulting is self‑employment  
  - Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — if you have self‑employment income  
  - Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular home‑office method (not needed for simplified)  
  - Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize  
  - Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — if interest totals meet thresholds or you want to list bank/broker interest  
  - Supporting forms: 1099‑NEC (if applicable), 1099‑INT (interest), 1098‑E (student loan interest)
- State (Arizona):
  - Arizona Form 140 (or the current AZ individual tax form) — state rules differ; AZ may have different adjustments and deduction rules. Include W‑2 and 1099s as required by AZ.

Estimated taxes / payment considerations
- Because you have self‑employment income, you may owe self‑employment tax and possibly quarterly estimated tax payments to avoid underpayment penalties. If you expect similar consulting income next year, plan for estimated payments.

Quick numeric estimates (rough)
- Total gross income you reported: 79,400 + 12,500 + 1,400 = $93,300.  
- Home office regular rent allocation ≈ $5,113 (20.29% of $25,200). Simplified = $700.  
- Rough self‑employment tax on $12,500 (if no expenses) ≈ $1,760 — half (~$880) is an above‑the‑line deduction. Exact amount depends on net business income after business expenses.

Recommended next steps
1. Answer the clarifying questions above (self‑employment status, year of student loan interest payments, nature of the $950 expenses, any mortgage/property tax/state tax amounts).  
2. Gather documents: W‑2; any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC; 1099‑INTs from banks/brokers; 1098‑E for student loan interest (2024); receipts for charitable giving; rent receipts/lease and utility bills (for home office); records for any other deductible expenses.  
3. If consulting is self‑employment, decide whether to use simplified or regular home‑office method; I can compute the exact numbers for both and show how they affect your taxable income and SE tax.  
