- Because you have consulting (self‑employment), you can claim a home‑office deduction if the space is used regularly and exclusively for the business.
- You said 140 sq ft exclusive / home is 690 sq ft. Business percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.3%.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 140 * $5 = $700 deduction on Schedule C.
  - Regular (actual expenses) method: allocate a share of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. Example just using rent: $2,100/mo × 12 = $25,200 annual rent × 20.3% ≈ $5,119 allocable to the home office (plus allocable utilities if you pay those). For your numbers, the regular method will almost certainly give a larger deduction than the simplified method.
- Note: Home office deduction is only allowed for self‑employed income. If the deduction exceeds your net business profit, the excess can be carried forward (it’s limited to profit from that business).
- If you use the regular method, use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home). If you use simplified, just enter the simplified amount on Schedule C.

