Below I assume the consulting is self-employment, the $950 are business expenses, and the student loan interest is eligible for the 2024 deduction (I’ll show where I used those assumptions).

1) What you can deduct (high level)
- Self-employment (consulting) business expenses: deductible on Schedule C. This includes the $950 you listed if those are business expenses.
- Home office deduction (if you meet the rules): deductible on Schedule C (either simplified or actual method). You said the office is used exclusively and is 140 sq ft of a 690 sq ft rental — that is ~20.29% of the home.
- Half of your self-employment tax: an above-the-line adjustment to income (reduces AGI).
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 as an adjustment to income (subject to MAGI phase-out). If your MAGI is below the phaseout threshold you can take up to $2,500.
- Charitable contributions: deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). You listed $1,400 to Northwest Harvest (keep the receipt).
- Interest from Goldman/TDA: reported as interest income (Form 1040 / possibly Schedule B if interest > $1,500).
- Note: unreimbursed employee expenses for a W-2 job are not deductible for 2018–2025 (unless you are in certain categories like performing artists, some government-related jobs, etc.). If the $950 are employee unreimbursed expenses, they are not deductible.

2) Home office — how to treat it and which method to use
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 140 sq ft that is 140 * $5 = $700. Easy and often used by small side businesses.
  b) Actual-expense method: allocate direct/indirect home expenses by your business-use percentage (your business % = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%). For a renter, rent and a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, internet, etc., can be allocated. Example: rent $2,100/month = $25,200/year. 20.29% of that = ~$5,112. That is much larger than the simplified $700 — but actual requires tracking and use of Form 8829 (and the home-office deduction cannot exceed net profit from the business; any excess may be carried or limited).
- Which to pick? If you only have rent and no big additional home expenses tracked, simplified is easiest; if you have substantial rent or utilities and want the bigger deduction, use actual method (Form 8829). Keep invoices/records.

3) Standard deduction vs. itemize (quick comparison)
- For tax year 2024, the standard deduction for married filing jointly is $29,200.
- Your likely itemizable deductions (as given) are small: charitable $1,400. You did not list mortgage interest, property taxes or large medical expenses. So the standard deduction will almost certainly be larger than itemizing.
- Therefore you will likely take the standard deduction (unless you have additional deductible items you didn’t list).

