Thanks — good detail. Below I’ll (1) summarize what looks deductible, (2) show how to treat the home office (two methods, with numbers), (3) recommend whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, (4) list the federal (and brief state) forms you’ll likely need, and (5) give next steps and a couple quick questions.

Quick summary of your situation
- Filing status: Married filing jointly (Arizona resident).
- Income: W-2 $79,400; self-employment/consulting $12,500; interest $920 (Marcus) + $480 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,400 interest.
- Expenses/deductions you listed: home office 140 sq ft exclusive in a 690 sq ft rental (rent $2,100/mo), $3,600 student loan interest (years 2016–2019 — you should have Form 1098-E if recent interest applies), $1,400 charitable gift (Northwest Harvest, you have receipt), $950 unreimbursed expenses (need to confirm whether these are business expenses for the consulting work).

What you can (generally) deduct
- Consulting income: Report on Schedule C (Form 1040). You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (e.g., supplies, advertising, business portion of phone/internet if applicable, and home office if it meets the rules).
- Self-employment tax: You’ll owe self-employment tax on net self-employment earnings (Schedule SE). Half of the self-employment tax is an “above-the-line” deduction on Form 1040 (reported on Schedule 1).
- Home office: If the 140 sq ft is used regularly and exclusively for the consulting business, you can take the home-office deduction (see below for two methods).
- Student loan interest: Up to $2,500 of student loan interest is deductible as an adjustment to income (above-the-line), subject to MAGI phaseouts. You paid $3,600 but the maximum federal deduction is $2,500 (and it phases out at higher incomes).
- Charitable gift: $1,400 charitable cash contribution — deductible only if you itemize (on Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you won’t separately deduct this on federal return.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: If the $950 are unreimbursed expenses related to a W-2 job, they are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025. If they were business expenses for your consulting (self-employment), they are deductible on Schedule C.

Home office — two methods (with numbers)
Your numbers: rent = $2,100/mo = $25,200/year; office = 140 sq ft; total home = 690 sq ft → office percentage = 140/690 = 20.29%.

1) Actual (regular) method — use Form 8829
- Rent portion attributable to the office: $25,200 * 20.29% ≈ $5,112.
- You can also allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., to the office (if you have them) which increases the deduction.
- Example effect (if your only Schedule C expenses were home-office portion + $950 business expenses):
  - Schedule C net profit = $12,500 − $5,112 − $950 = $6,438.
  - Self-employment tax ≈ 15.3% applied to 92.35% of net profit → roughly $909 SE tax.
  - Half SE tax deductible (~$455) reduces AGI.
- Actual method gives the larger deduction in your case (because rent portion is significant).

2) Simplified method
- $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft: 140 * $5 = $700.
- Simpler recordkeeping; you do not use Form 8829.
- Example effect with same $950 business expenses:
  - Schedule C net profit = $12,500 − $700 − $950 = $10,850.
  - Self-employment tax ≈ $1,532; half deductible ≈ $766.
- This yields higher taxable income than the actual method in your case, so actual method likely preferable given your rent.

Recommendation on home office: Because your rent is high and the office is ~20% of the space, the actual method (Form 8829) will almost certainly produce a larger deduction than the simplified method. Keep clear documentation of exclusive regular use, rent receipts, and any utility bills.

Itemize vs. standard deduction
- Your known potential itemized deductions: $1,400 charitable cash + possible state income tax paid (SALT) up to the $10k limit, and any mortgage interest/property tax if applicable (you didn’t list any). Student loan interest is an above-the-line deduction (not an itemized deduction).
- For married filing jointly, the federal standard deduction is substantially larger than $1,400 (for reference, 2023 MFJ was $27,700; 2024 is higher). With only $1,400 of charitable contributions and no mortgage interest or large medical expenses listed, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction on your federal return.
- State (Arizona): check Arizona rules — some states handle deductions differently and you may have to decide itemize vs standard for state separately. Typically most people with only modest itemizable expenses take the standard deduction on both federal and state returns, but confirm for AZ or ask your preparer.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — student loan interest deduction and the adjustment for half of self-employment tax are reported here.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $12,500 consulting income and business expenses (including home office).
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — computes SE tax you owe.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual method for home office.
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize (likely you will not).
- Form 1098-E — student loan interest statement (use to substantiate the $3,600 paid; you’ll likely be limited to $2,500).
- 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC (if any of your consulting clients issued these) and 1099-INTs from banks/brokers for interest (keep these for reporting).
- If eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A), you may need Form 8995 (or 8995-A) to claim it — this depends on taxable income and the type of business; with modest self-employment income you may be able to claim up to 20% of qualified business income.

Arizona state forms
- AZ Form 140 (Arizona Individual Income Tax Return) or the correct resident return form for the tax year. Check Arizona Department of Revenue instructions or tax software for state-specific adjustments and whether you itemize differently at the state level.

Other items to watch
- Student loan interest limit: Maximum federal deduction is $2,500 and is subject to income phaseouts. You’ll need to calculate MAGI (after adjustments) to confirm whether you can take the full $2,500. Given your numbers, you’ll likely be under the phaseout threshold, but the final amount depends on the net profit and SE adjustments.
- Schedule B: You generally only need Schedule B if interest/dividend exceeds $1,500 or you have certain foreign accounts. Your combined interest is $1,400 — under the threshold — but include the 1099-INT amounts on the 1040.
- Estimated taxes: With self-employment income, you should consider making quarterly estimated tax payments in future years (Form 1040-ES) to cover income tax + self-employment tax.
- QBI deduction: You might be eligible to take a 20% qualified business income deduction on (part of) your net self-employment income. It’s somewhat technical (subject to taxable-income thresholds and other rules). With relatively low net self-employment income you may be able to claim it — ask if you want this checked.

What I recommend you do next
1. Confirm whether the $950 “unreimbursed expenses” were for your consulting business (if they were for your W-2 job, they’re not deductible).
2. Gather documents: W-2, any 1099-NEC or records for consulting, 1099-INTs, Form 1098-E (student loan interest), rent receipts/lease and utility bills for home-office substantiation, charity receipt ($1,400).
3. Decide method for the home office. Based on the quick math, actual method (Form 8829) will be more favorable here because of your high rent.
4. If you’d like, I can run through a more detailed worksheet with the exact AGI, SE tax, and estimated federal tax using whichever home-office method you prefer — just tell me whether the $950 is business-related and whether you have any other deductible items (mortgage interest, property taxes, large medical expenses, IRA contributions, or estimated payments made).

Would you like me to (A) prepare a simple worksheet with exact AGI and estimated federal tax using both home-office methods, or (B) draft a checklist/email you can send to a tax preparer with all the numbers and documents?

