I can help — below is a plain-language summary of what’s deductible, how to treat the home office, which forms you’ll need, and a short worked example using the numbers you gave. I’ll also list the follow-ups I need from you to give exact amounts (or I can draft an email to your tax preparer with these numbers).

High-level summary
- Most of the items you listed flow through as follows:
  - W-2 wages ($79,400) and interest income ($920 + $480) are ordinary income on Form 1040.
  - Consulting income ($12,500) is self-employment income and will be reported on Schedule C. Business expenses related to that consulting reduce Schedule C profit.
  - Self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare ~15.3%) is computed on Schedule SE; half of that SE tax is an adjustment to income on Form 1040.
  - Home office (if exclusive and regular business use) is a Schedule C business expense — you can use the simplified method or the actual-expense method (see below).
  - Student loan interest is an “above-the-line” adjustment (up to $2,500 max in most cases) but it phases out at higher incomes — you must have actually paid the interest in the tax year to claim it.
  - Charitable cash gifts (your $1,400) are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A).
  - Unreimbursed employee expenses are NOT deductible on the federal return for most employees (suspended since 2018). If the $950 is business expense related to your consulting (self-employment), it is deductible on Schedule C; if it’s for your W-2 job and unreimbursed by your employer, it’s not deductible federally.

Home-office: how to treat it
- Qualification: must be used regularly and exclusively for business. You said 140 sq ft exclusive — that sounds eligible if it truly is exclusive.
- Two methods:
  1. Simplified: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 140 sq ft = 140 * $5 = $700 deduction on Schedule C.
  2. Actual-expense method: allocate a share of actual home expenses to the office (percentage = office sq ft / total sq ft). Based on your numbers:
     - Office % = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%
     - Annual rent = $2,100 * 12 = $25,200
     - Rent portion for office ≈ $25,200 * 20.29% ≈ $5,113
     - You can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, internet (the business portion), etc. (Note: depreciation rules differ if you own; as a renter you generally allocate rent and utilities but not depreciation.)
  - Pick the method that gives the larger deduction (actual seems larger here). The home-office deduction reduces your Schedule C profit (and thus income and SE tax).

Student loan interest
- The maximum student loan interest deduction is generally $2,500 per year (an above-the-line deduction), subject to income phase-outs. You reported $3,600 — you can only deduct up to the allowable maximum for the tax year in which you paid the interest and if you meet the AGI limits. Confirm whether $3,600 was paid in the same tax year you’re filing.

Charitable contribution
- $1,400 cash gift to a qualified charity is deductible if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep the receipt — that’s required.

Unreimbursed expenses ($950)
- If these are business expenses related to your consulting, include them on Schedule C.
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job, they are generally nondeductible for federal returns (but some states still allow them — check Arizona rules).

Estimated taxes and self-employment tax
- Because you have self-employment income, you’ll likely owe self-employment tax (Schedule SE), and you may need to make quarterly estimated tax payments to avoid penalties (Form 1040-ES).
- Approximate SE tax: SE tax is applied to about 92.35% of your net self-employment profit. Half of the SE tax is deductible on Form 1040.

Forms you’ll likely need
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 (individual return)
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $12,500 consulting and related expenses (home office, the $950 if business-related, etc.)
  - Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — to compute Social Security/Medicare tax on the consulting profit
  - Schedule 1 (or line items on 1040) for adjustments (e.g., student loan interest deduction, deduction for half of SE tax)
  - Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) only if you choose to itemize (you must compare the total itemized amount to the standard deduction)
  - Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) only if total interest/dividend > $1,500 or if certain conditions apply (based on amounts you gave, this likely isn’t required)
  - 1099-NEC/1099-MISC you may receive from clients; 1099-INT from banks; 1099-B or 1099-DIV from brokerages if applicable
- State:
  - Arizona individual income tax form (Arizona filing is required if you’re an AZ resident with taxable income). (Check current Arizona form numbers / instructions or ask your preparer.)

Rough worked example (using your numbers and assuming the $950 and home office are business expenses):
- Gross income: 79,400 (W-2) + 12,500 (consulting) + 920 + 480 (interest) = $93,300
- Home-office (actual method on rent only): ≈ $5,113
- Business expenses total: $5,113 + $950 = $6,063
- Net Schedule C profit ≈ 12,500 − 6,063 = $6,437
- Net earnings subject to SE (approx): 6,437 * 92.35% ≈ $5,945
- SE tax ≈ 15.3% * $5,945 ≈ $910 (approx). Half of that (≈ $455) is an adjustment to income on Form 1040.
- Student loan interest: at most $2,500 deduction (if you paid that amount in the tax year and meet AGI rules).
- Itemizing vs standard: your only federal itemized items listed are $1,400 charitable and possibly state/local taxes (which are capped at $10,000). Given the typical standard deduction for married filing jointly is much larger than $1,400, you will most likely take the standard deduction — unless you have substantial mortgage interest, property taxes, large state taxes, large medical expenses, or other itemizable amounts you haven’t listed.

Important clarifying questions I need to finalize calculations
1. Is the $3,600 student-loan interest amount the amount you actually paid in the tax year you’re filing (i.e., in 2024), or is that the total interest charged across earlier years? (You can only deduct interest actually paid in the tax year.)
2. Are the $950 unreimbursed expenses related to your consulting/self-employed work, or are they unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job?
3. Do you own the home or rent? (You said you pay $2,100/month, so I assumed you rent.)
4. Do you have any other deductions not listed (mortgage interest, property tax, state income tax withheld/paid, large medical expenses, other charitable donations)?
