Quick summary / recommendation
- If your only itemizable deduction listed is the $1,400 charitable gift (and you have no large mortgage interest, big property taxes, or other deductible items), you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly. (For 2024 the MFJ standard deduction is in the high-$20k range — check the exact IRS amount when you file; it’s likely around $29k.)
- Student loan interest (up to $2,500 maximum) is an above-the-line deduction (reduces AGI) if the interest was paid in 2024 and you’re under the phaseout. With your income level, you’re likely eligible for the full student-loan-interest deduction (subject to my clarification above).
- Your consulting income looks like self-employment income. If so, you can take business expenses (including a home-office deduction) on Schedule C and pay self-employment tax (Schedule SE). Unreimbursed employee expenses (if these $950 are employee expenses for a W-2 job) are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025. If those $950 are business expenses for your consulting, they are deductible on Schedule C.
- Home office: if your consulting is self-employment, you can use either the simplified method ($5/sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 140 sq ft × $5 = $700) or actual-expense method (pro-rate rent, utilities, etc.). With rent $2,100/month, actual-expense method will almost certainly give a larger deduction: 140/690 ≈ 20.29% of rent. That’s about $5,115 of rent allocated to the business (plus a share of utilities, insurance, repairs if you include them).
- Keep all receipts and the 1098‑E (student loan interest), 1099s (consulting if issued), 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV (interest), and your W‑2. Those documents are needed to prepare the tax return.

Detailed treatment of each item
1) Income
- W-2 wages from Riverside Hospital: $79,400 — reported on Form 1040 from the W-2.
- Consulting: $12,500 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). You will also owe self-employment tax on the net profit (Schedule SE).
- Interest: $920 (Goldman Sachs Marcus) and $480 (TD Ameritrade) — include as taxable interest on Form 1040 (reported on 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV as applicable).

2) Home office (140 sq ft exclusive; home = 690 sq ft; rent $2,100/mo)
- If the consulting is self-employment, you can deduct the business portion of rent/utilities. Business-use percentage = 140/690 = 20.29%.
- Actual-expense method example (rent only): annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200; home-office share of rent ≈ $25,200 × 0.2029 ≈ $5,115. Add business-share of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs if applicable.
- Simplified method: 140 sq ft × $5 = $700 (often smaller).
- The home-office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit, which reduces both income tax and self-employment tax.

3) Unreimbursed expenses $950
- If these are business expenses for your consulting (supplies, software, phone used for consulting), deduct on Schedule C.
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses (related to your W-2 job), they are not deductible for 2018–2025 on federal returns.

