- Student loan interest: only up to $2,500 may be deductible as an adjustment to income (subject to MAGI phaseouts) — so you probably cannot deduct the full $3,600 you listed; expect up to $2,500 unless your MAGI phases you out.  
- Interest income ($920 + $480 = $1,400) is taxable and reported on your Form 1040 (Schedule B only if total interest > $1,500).  
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job are not deductible (suspended under current law). If the $950 were business expenses for your consulting, they are deductible on Schedule C.

What you can deduct (and where)
- Consulting business (self‑employed) — Schedule C:
  - Gross consulting income: $12,500.
  - Deductible business expenses: if the $950 is for the consulting business (e.g., supplies, software, travel related to clients), include it on Schedule C.
  - Home‑office expenses: you can deduct a business portion of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., attributable to the exclusive home office used for the consulting business (see below for calculation methods).
  - Self‑employment tax: you’ll pay SE tax (Schedule SE). You may deduct one‑half of SE tax as an adjustment on Form 1040.

- Home office:
  - You said 140 sq ft exclusive out of a 690 sq ft rental → business percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.3%.
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified: $5 per sq ft × 140 sq ft = $700 (easy, no Form 8829).
    - Actual (usually better here): allocate actual allowable home expenses by the business percentage. Example with the rent you gave:
      - Rent $2,100/mo → $25,200/year × 20.29% ≈ $5,115 — that alone is bigger than the simplified method. You may also allocate part of utilities, renters insurance, repairs, etc. (Note: renters cannot depreciate the home; depreciation applies only to owners.)
    - If you use the actual method and you have other allocable expenses, file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home). If you use simplified, you report the deduction directly on Schedule C.
  - Important: home office deduction is only allowed for self‑employed/independent contractor activity (your consulting). It is not available for W‑2 employees under present law.

- Student loan interest:
  - Maximum deduction is $2,500 (above‑the‑line / Schedule 1). The deduction phases out based on MAGI for married filing jointly — you’re probably well under the phaseout given your W‑2 + consulting + interest (~$93k gross), so expect to be able to take the $2,500 cap if you actually paid $3,600 in 2024. Clarify whether the $3,600 is interest paid in 2024 (only interest paid in the tax year counts).
