Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- For 2024 the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is substantially higher than $1,400 of charitable giving alone. With the information given (charity $1,400 and no mortgage interest or large state/local taxes), you will almost certainly take the standard deduction.  
- Note: the home‑office deduction and the student loan interest deduction are independent of the decision to itemize — home office is a business expense on Schedule C, and student loan interest is an above‑the‑line adjustment.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
- W‑2: Riverside Hospital (report wages $79,400).
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — student loan interest deduction and half of SE tax adjustment flow here.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report consulting income $12,500 and business expenses (home office deduction if using simplified or actual method appears here as deduction column; if using actual method, Form 8829 supports it).
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — use if you choose the actual method for home office.
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax on your net Schedule C profit; half of SE tax is deductible on Schedule 1.
- Form 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC — you may receive these from clients; include any 1099s in Schedule C income (and include any income even if you didn’t receive a 1099).
- Form 1040 line (Interest) — report interest; Schedule B only if total interest > $1,500.
- State return: Arizona individual income tax return — AZ rules differ; state treatment of some deductions may vary.

Estimated tax & withholding
