I can help — nice and clear. Short answer up front: given the numbers you gave, you’ll almost certainly take the standard deduction (instead of itemizing). Your consulting income should be reported on Schedule C (or similar) and that’s where the home-office deduction belongs (not on Schedule A). You’ll also need Schedule SE for self‑employment tax. Below I explain why, show quick estimates, list the forms you’ll likely need, and ask a few clarifying questions so I can refine the numbers.

1) Quick summary of how each item is treated
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital ($79,400): report on Form 1040 (W‑2 income).
- Consulting income ($12,500 from 6 clients): report as self‑employment income on Schedule C (profit/loss). If you got 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC for some clients, include those too.
- Interest income ($920 + $480): report on Form 1040 (from 1099‑INTs).
- Home office (140 sq ft exclusive; home = 690 sq ft; rent $2,100/mo = $25,200/yr):
  - If the consulting is self‑employment, you can take a home‑office deduction on Schedule C.
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified: $5/sq ft × 140 sq ft = $700.
    - Actual: allocate business percentage = 140/690 ≈ 20.29% of allowable home expenses. For rent only, that’s ≈ $25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,110 for the year (plus a prorated share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if you have them). The actual method looks much larger here; use FORM 8829 with Schedule C to calculate actual method, or use the simplified method to avoid Form 8829.
  - Note: home‑office deduction is available only for self‑employed or in rare employee situations (not for typical W‑2 unreimbursed employee expenses).
- Student loan interest ($3,600 reported): the student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment (up to $2,500 typically) and phases out at higher MAGI. Based on the incomes you gave, your MAGI looks like it will be below the phaseout threshold, so you’d generally be limited to a maximum deduction of $2,500 (not $3,600). Also the deduction only covers interest paid in the tax year — confirm that the $3,600 is interest paid in 2024 (not an aggregate for prior years).
- Charitable ($1,400 to Northwest Harvest): deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction you do not deduct this separately (there was a one‑year special above‑the‑line for 2020 only; that does not apply now).
- Unreimbursed expenses ($950): if these are business expenses for your consulting (supplies, mileage, phone, etc.) they belong on Schedule C and are deductible. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 job, they generally are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (TCJA suspension), except for a few narrow employee categories.

2) Quick estimated math (illustrative)
- Total gross income = 79,400 + 12,500 + 920 + 480 = $93,300.
- Student loan interest adjustment (max likely allowed) = $2,500 → AGI reduction.
- Self‑employment net (example using actual home‑office & business expense):
  - Consulting 12,500 − home‑office (≈5,110) − other business expenses (e.g., the $950 if it’s business related) = net ≈ $6,440.
  - Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% of net ≈ $909 (approx).
  - Half of SE tax is deductible (~$454), further reducing AGI.
- Itemized deductions you provided: charitable $1,400 (plus any state/local tax or property taxes you paid). For married filing jointly the standard deduction (for recent years) has been large (e.g., $27,700 for 2023) — unless you have sizeable mortgage interest, large state taxes, or big charitable gifts, itemizing will usually be lower than the standard deduction. Based on what you’ve listed, standard deduction is almost certainly better.

3) Forms you will likely need
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income) for student loan interest deduction and other adjustments (or as directed on Form 1040 for the tax year).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report consulting income and business expenses (including home office).
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you choose the actual home‑office method. If you use the simplified method you do NOT use Form 8829.
