Top-line conclusion
- You’ll almost certainly use the standard deduction on your federal return unless you have substantial additional deductible items (large state/local taxes, mortgage interest, big medical expenses, or many years of unreimbursed employee expenses that are actually business expenses). With only $1,400 of charitable contributions shown and no mortgage interest listed, itemizing will very likely be lower than the 2024 married-filing-jointly standard deduction.
- Your consulting income is self-employment income — report it on Schedule C and Schedule SE. That allows you to deduct the home-office portion of rent and the consulting-related expenses (the $950, if those are business expenses), and it also creates a deduction for half the self-employment tax.
- Student loan interest: the statutory maximum federal deduction is $2,500 (subject to MAGI phaseouts). You reported $3,600 interest — we need to confirm which year(s) that interest was paid and your 2024 MAGI to know how much you can actually deduct.

Details and calculations

1) Standard vs. itemize (federal)
- 2024 standard deduction (married filing jointly): $29,200 (IRS — please verify current number when filing).
- Your documented itemizable amount so far: charitable gifts $1,400. Unless you have substantial state/local taxes (SALT), mortgage interest, large medical expenses, or other deductible items, itemized total will be well below $29,200.
- Recommendation: take the standard deduction unless you can document additional itemized deductions that push you above $29,200.

2) How to treat the home office
- You are self-employed for the consulting income, so you may use the home-office deduction on Schedule C.
- Square footage method / calculation:
  - Home office = 140 sq ft exclusive use
  - Total home = 690 sq ft
  - Home-office percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%
  - Rent: $2,100/month → $25,200/year
  - Deductible portion of rent for home office (actual-expense method) = 25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,115
- You can elect either:
  - Actual-expense method (use the percentage of actual home expenses; generally gives the bigger deduction here because of high rent), or
  - Simplified method (standard $5/sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 140 × $5 = $700).
- Because you said the home office is exclusive and you have rent, the actual-expense method (≈$5,115) will likely give a larger deduction. Keep documentation (lease, proof of rent paid, utilities, and proof the space is used exclusively and regularly for business).
- Record the home-office deduction on Schedule C (it reduces self-employment net profit). If you use actual expenses you will show the business-use percentage and the associated amounts on Schedule C.

3) Self-employment tax and other self-employed items
- Report consulting income and business expenses on Schedule C.
- File Schedule SE to compute self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net profit. You can deduct one-half of the self-employment tax as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).
- You may be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction (Form 8995 or 8995-A) depending on taxable income and whether your consulting is not a specified service business at high income levels. If your taxable income is modest, you likely qualify for some or all of the QBI deduction — we can estimate that once we know net profit and other items.
- Make sure you track and deduct ordinary business expenses (the $950 unreimbursed — if these are business expenses for the consulting, they go on Schedule C; if they are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W-2 job, those are not deductible under current law).

4) Student loan interest ($3,600)
- The federal student loan interest deduction maximum is $2,500 per return (subject to income limits).
- The deduction is an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1) if you meet MAGI limits and other eligibility rules.
- Clarification needed: Are you saying $3,600 is the interest paid in 2024 (or do you mean interest accrued/paid in earlier years — 2016–2019?)? Only interest actually paid during the 2024 tax year is potentially deductible on your 2024 return.
- Also we’ll need your 2024 MAGI to determine phaseout eligibility. For MFJ, phaseouts begin and end at set thresholds (these thresholds change for inflation each year). If your MAGI is above the upper limit, you cannot take the deduction.

5) Charitable contribution $1,400
- Deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. Otherwise, it does not reduce your federal taxable income (unless special legislation applies; as of 2024 there is no general above-the-line charitable deduction for non-itemizers).
- Keep the receipt you mentioned.

6) Interest income reporting
- Combine interest: $920 + $480 = $1,400 total interest.
- You report interest on Form 1040. Schedule B is required only if you have over $1,500 of taxable interest/dividends or certain other conditions; your total interest is under $1,500, so Schedule B likely not required.

7) Unreimbursed expenses $950
- Important: are these unreimbursed employee expenses (for your W-2 job) or expenses related to your consulting business?
  - If they are consulting business expenses, report them on Schedule C (deductible).
  - If they are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 work, they are not deductible on federal returns through at least 2025 (suspended by TCJA), except for certain categories (e.g., certain performing artists, reservists, etc.). So clarify this.

Essential federal forms you’ll likely need
- Form 1040 (individual income tax return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income) — report adjustments such as half of SE tax, student loan interest (if eligible)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report consulting income and business expenses (including home office)
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — compute SE tax
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize (probably not)
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — if you claim the QBI deduction (might apply)
- 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC (if you received any from clients) — attach/keep info for Schedule C
- 1099-INT (from Marcus, brokerage statements) or 1099-B if securities sold (TD Ameritrade)
- 1098-E (student loan interest statement) from MOHELA (if interest was paid in 2024)
- Form 1040-ES — for estimated tax payments (if you expect similar self-employment income in 2025, you may need to make estimated payments during the year to avoid penalties)

Arizona state filing
- Arizona resident return: Form 140 (Arizona Resident Personal Income Tax Return) or other AZ forms if part-year/nonresident — check Arizona Dept. of Revenue instructions.
- Arizona has its own rules for standard vs. itemized deductions and may conform to federal AGI with certain differences — I can check Arizona specifics if you want.
