Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — here’s a clear, practical breakdown of what you can likely deduct, how to treat the home office and the consulting income, whether to itemize, and which federal & state forms you’ll generally need. I’ll also flag a few clarifying questions at the end so I can make the recommendations exact.

1) How your income should be reported
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital ($79,400): reported on Form 1040 (W-2 wages).
- Consulting $12,500 from 6 clients: treated as self‑employment income. Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). Even if you didn’t receive 1099s, you still report all income.
- Interest: $920 (Goldman Sachs Marcus) + $480 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,400 total — report as interest income (Form 1040). If total interest/ordinary dividends exceed $1,500, you’d use Schedule B; your amount is under that threshold so Schedule B likely not required.

2) Self‑employment tax and related adjustments
- Self‑employment income on Schedule C is subject to self‑employment (SE) tax (~15.3%) via Schedule SE.
- You can deduct one-half of your SE tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040. Also, business expenses on Schedule C reduce both income tax and SE tax.

3) Home office deduction (you said 140 sq ft exclusive; home 690 sq ft; rent $2,100/month)
- You qualify for a home office deduction if the space is used exclusively and regularly for your consulting business and it is your principal place of business.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For 140 sq ft: 140 × $5 = $700 deduction.
  b) Actual (regular) method: allocate a percentage of your allowable home expenses to the business. Your business percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%. If you use actual and only count rent as an allocable expense, annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200 → rent portion = $25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,114. You can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc. Direct expenses to the office are fully deductible.
- Form to use: For the actual method you generally file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) with Schedule C; the simplified method does not use Form 8829.
- Note: Home office deduction cannot exceed net profit from that business. If your Schedule C net is positive, you can take it.

4) Student loan interest ($3,600 listed)
- The student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line deduction (reported on Schedule 1 to Form 1040) with a maximum of $2,500 per year. IMPORTANT: only interest actually paid during the tax year can be deducted. If the $3,600 represents interest paid in 2016–2019, that does not make it deductible on your 2024 return. If you paid $3,600 in 2024, you can only deduct up to $2,500 (and subject to income phaseouts). Given your income (~$93k total before adjustments), you are likely under the phaseout for married filing jointly, but I can check exact limits if you want.
- You should have Form 1098-E from your loan servicer if you paid ≥ $600 in interest in 2024; that helps verify the amount.

5) Charitable contributions ($1,400 to Northwest Harvest)
- Charitable donations to qualified organizations are deductible if you itemize. You have a receipt — keep it.
- For cash donations, you can deduct the full amount if you itemize (subject to overall AGI limitations for very large gifts, which likely don’t apply here).

6) Unreimbursed expenses ($950)
- If these are unreimbursed employee business expenses for your W-2 job: since the 2018 TCJA suspension, miscellaneous itemized deductions for unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible for most taxpayers through 2025. So those would NOT be deductible on Schedule A.
- If the $950 are business expenses related to the consulting (self‑employment) work, they belong on Schedule C and will reduce your Schedule C net income (and SE tax).

7) Standard deduction vs. itemize
- You should compare your total itemized deductions (charitable $1,400 + state & local taxes paid, mortgage interest if any, property taxes, large medical expenses above the floor, etc.) to the standard deduction for married filing jointly.
- If your total itemized deductions exceed the standard deduction, itemize on Schedule A; otherwise take the standard deduction.
- Based on the items you listed (only $1,400 charitable + maybe state income tax withheld), it’s likely the standard deduction will be larger than your itemized total — BUT the home office deduction (if you use actual or simplified) is an above-the-line deduction via Schedule C and does not require itemizing (it reduces your adjusted gross income by reducing Schedule C profit). So the home office deduction can provide benefit even if you take the standard deduction.

8) Rough example calculations (illustrative)
- Gross income: W-2 79,400 + consulting 12,500 + interest 1,400 = $93,300.
- If you take actual home office allocation on rent only: home office ≈ $5,114.
- If the $950 are business expenses, Schedule C net = 12,500 − 5,114 − 950 = $6,436.
- SE tax roughly: 0.9235 × 6,436 ≈ 5,944 × 0.153 ≈ $909 (approx). One-half SE tax deductible ≈ $455.
- Your adjusted gross income (approx) = 93,300 − 5,114 (home office already subtracted in Schedule C) − 455 (half SE tax) − any student loan interest deduction (<= $2,500 if paid in 2024 and you qualify) → then subtract standard deduction or itemized.
- These are estimates — final numbers depend on exact amounts of utilities, any other deductions, withholding, spouse income, etc.

9) Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (if you have adjustments like student loan interest; some of these fields are on Schedule 1)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) for the consulting income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the actual method for the home office (simplified method does not require 8829)
- Form 1099-NEC(s)/1099-MISC if received (you still report income even without 1099s)
- Possibly Schedule B (if interest/dividend > $1,500) — probably not needed here

Arizona state filing
- AZ Form 140 (Arizona resident return) or the correct AZ individual form for the tax year. Arizona allows its own rules for deductions and adjustments; you’ll also report your federal-adjusted income and make state-specific adjustments. Check AZ rules for any differences in home office treatment or student loan interest.

10) Practical next steps / checklist
- Confirm how much student loan interest was paid in 2024 (and get Form 1098-E from MOHELA).
- Confirm whether the $950 are business-related or unreimbursed employee expenses.
- Gather 2024 rent paid (monthly × 12), utilities, renter’s insurance, and any receipts for repairs/office items if you plan to use the actual home office method.
- Collect charity receipt(s) for Northwest Harvest.
- Gather any 1099s and W-2 and 1098-E.
- Decide whether you want to use simplified home office method ($700) or actual method (about $5,100 from rent alone in the example).
- If you expect to owe additional tax because of self-employment income, consider estimated tax payments or increasing withholding to avoid penalties.

11) Caveats and recommendation
- This is general guidance. For exact calculations (and to confirm student loan interest phaseout thresholds, state-specific nuances, or complex situations), I can either:
  a) walk through a more detailed worksheet with your exact 2024 payment numbers and other itemizable amounts and compute estimated tax & final taxable income, or
  b) you can consult a CPA/tax pro for final filing if you prefer.

Quick clarifying questions so I can refine the numbers for you:
1. Is the $3,600 student loan interest amount what you actually paid in 2024 or is it interest paid in earlier years (2016–2019)?
2. Are the $950 unreimbursed expenses business-related for your consulting work, or are they employee expenses from your W-2 job?
3. Do you have any other deductible items (mortgage interest, property tax, large state income tax paid, large medical expenses, or other charitable donations) that would change the itemizing calculation?
4. Do you want me to run a detailed estimate using the actual-home-office calculation (using only rent) vs simplified method and show what your federal tax & self‑employment tax would look like?

Tell me answers to those and I’ll compute a concrete estimate and list exactly which boxes/lines and forms to fill.

