- The $950 “unreimbursed expenses”: were those expenses for your consulting work (self-employment) or were they unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 job? (After 2017, unreimbursed employee expenses generally aren’t deductible on the federal return.)

Assuming the common case (student loan interest paid in 2024 and the $950 are business expenses), here’s how things look and what to do.

1) What’s taxable / what must be reported
- W-2 wages: report $79,400 on Form 1040 (from Riverside Hospital W‑2).
- Interest income: report $920 (Goldman Sachs Marcus) + $480 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,400 (1099‑INTs).
- Consulting income: report $12,500 on Schedule C (even if you didn’t receive 1099s).
- Self-employment tax: you’ll owe SE tax on your net self-employment earnings (Schedule SE). You also get to deduct half of self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040.

2) Home office treatment
- Qualification: The home office deduction is only for self-employed people (Schedule C). It requires regular and exclusive use and that the space is your principal place of business. You said the 140 sq ft is exclusive — that sounds like it may qualify for the consulting work, assuming that is your principal place for that work.
- Methods:
  - Actual method: allocate expenses by percentage of home used for business. Your allocation = 140 ÷ 690 ≈ 20.29%. Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200. Home-office portion of rent ≈ $25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,112. You can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., by the same percentage. Use Form 8829 to calculate and report these (it flows to Schedule C).
  - Simplified method: $5 × 140 ft = $700 (no Form 8829). In your numbers, the actual method will likely yield a larger deduction because of the high rent.
- Effect: Home-office deduction is a business expense that reduces your Schedule C net profit (and thus income subject to income tax and SE tax).

3) Student-loan interest
- If you paid student-loan interest in 2024, you may be able to deduct up to $2,500 as an above-the-line deduction (reported on Form 1040 / Schedule 1). That deduction phases out based on MAGI for married filing jointly (phaseout limits can change year to year), so whether you get the full amount depends on your AGI/MAGI. You should have Form 1098‑E from MOHELA showing the 2024 interest paid. If the $3,600 was paid in earlier years (2016–2019), that is not deductible on the 2024 return.

4) Unreimbursed employee expenses
- If the $950 are unreimbursed expenses for a W‑2 job: for federal purposes (post‑TCJA) those are generally not deductible.
- If the $950 are business expenses for the consulting (self-employed) work: include them on Schedule C as ordinary and necessary business expenses.

5) Whether to itemize or take the standard deduction
- 2024 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly: $29,200.
- Typical itemized deductions include: charitable contributions, mortgage interest, state/local taxes (SALT, capped at $10,000), medical expenses (above threshold), etc.
- From the items you listed, the only clear itemized deduction is charitable gifts of $1,400. That alone is far below the $29,200 standard deduction. The home office and business expenses are not itemized deductions — they reduce self-employment income instead.
- Conclusion: Based on the information given, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction for federal purposes. (If you have significant mortgage interest, property taxes, or other itemizable items you haven’t listed, re-check totals — itemizing only makes sense if your itemized total > $29,200.)

6) Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (if any adjustments to income like student loan interest or educator expenses).
- Schedule C — Profit or Loss from Business (to report the $12,500 consulting income and business expenses).
- Schedule SE — Self-Employment Tax (to compute SE tax and self-employment tax deduction).
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you use the actual method for the home office; not needed for the simplified method).
- Schedule A — Itemized Deductions only if you choose to itemize (unlikely here).
- Keep Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) and any 1099‑INT / 1099‑NEC you received as support.
- State: Arizona return (Form 140 for resident MFJ) — state forms and rules differ; Arizona may require its own schedules. Check AZ rules for deductible items and conformity with federal adjustments.

7) Other practical points and estimates
