- Home: 140 sq ft exclusive home office in a 690 sq ft rental; rent $2,100/month → $25,200/year
- Deductions/other: Student loan interest paid $3,600 (MOHELA); charitable cash gift $1,400 to Northwest Harvest (have receipt); $950 unreimbursed expenses (need to know whether related to consulting or to W‑2 job)

What to report (federal)
- Form 1040: Report all income on your 1040.
- W‑2 wages: report the $79,400 on Form 1040 (wages).
- Self‑employment income: report $12,500 on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). Even if you didn’t receive 1099s, you must report the income.
- Interest/dividends: report $1,400 (from 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV depending on what you received). If total interest + dividends < $1,500, Schedule B is generally not required.
- If you have capital transactions at TD Ameritrade, those get reported on Schedule D/Form 8949 — only relevant if you had sales/gains/losses.

Self‑employment tax and related adjustments
- Because of the consulting income you’ll owe self‑employment tax on your net Schedule C profit (Social Security + Medicare). Use Schedule SE to compute this.
- You get to deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).

Home‑office treatment (two options)
You can choose either the simplified method or the regular (actual) method for a self‑employed business home office. The home-office deduction only applies to your self‑employment activity (Schedule C).

1) Simplified method
- $5.00 per square foot up to 300 sq ft.
- Your office 140 sq ft → 140 × $5 = $700 deductible on Schedule C.
- Simpler recordkeeping; no Form 8829 required.

2) Regular (actual) method
- Business percentage = office sq ft / total sq ft = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%.
- Deduct the business share of allowable home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.) on Schedule C. With your rent only: $25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,112. (If you have utilities or other home expenses, include the business portion of those too.)
- To claim actual expenses, complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and carry the result to Schedule C.

Which method is better for you?
- Based on the rent numbers you provided, the actual method gives a much bigger deduction (~$5,112 vs $700). That will reduce your Schedule C net profit and self‑employment tax. So the actual method will likely be more beneficial unless you want simplicity.
- Note: If you use actual method you must keep records (rent, utility bills, etc.). If you ever sell a home (not applicable here for renter), special rules apply—another reason some people prefer simplified method.

Unreimbursed expenses ($950)
- If these $950 are business expenses for your consulting (supplies, travel, marketing, home‑office supplies, subscription software, etc.), include them on Schedule C — they lower your net profit.
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job (post‑TCJA), they are generally NOT deductible for federal returns through at least 2025.

Student loan interest ($3,600)
- The student loan interest deduction is an “adjustment to income” (reported on Schedule 1). Maximum deduction is generally $2,500 (check current law/phaseouts).
- There are income phaseouts based on Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI). For married filing jointly, the deduction is reduced or eliminated if your MAGI is above the IRS phaseout thresholds for 2024. You’ll need to compute MAGI to see if you get the full $2,500, a partial amount, or nothing. (Keep your Form 1098‑E from MOHELA which shows interest paid.)

Charitable gift ($1,400)
- Charitable cash donations are deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. If you take the standard deduction, you cannot deduct the $1,400.
- With only $1,400 in charitable and likely little/no mortgage interest or big state/local taxes (you’re renting), you will very likely be better off taking the standard deduction.

Standard deduction vs. itemize
- With the small itemizable amount you’ve listed (charity $1,400, maybe some state/local taxes but no mortgage interest), it’s almost certain the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly will exceed your itemized total.
- Recommendation: Take the standard deduction unless you have additional itemizable items (large mortgage interest, large medical expenses above 7.5% of AGI, larger charitable giving, large state & local taxes up to the SALT cap, etc.)

Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
