- QBI (approx) = 20% × $6,438 ≈ $1,288 (if eligible).
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 deductible (subject to MAGI phaseout).
- Standard deduction (MFJ) — likely larger than your itemized total, so you’ll probably take the standard deduction.

Bottom line: after the business deductions (including home office actual method), and the student loan interest (up to $2,500 subject to phaseouts), you will probably:
- Take the standard deduction,
- Report self‑employment income on Schedule C and pay SE tax on the Schedule C net,
- Claim half of SE tax as an adjustment to income,
- Possibly claim a QBI deduction of roughly 20% of your Schedule C net,
- Not itemize charitable donation unless you have other sizable itemizable items.

Forms you’ll typically need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments — student loan interest and other adjustments)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for the $12,500 consulting income
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the regular (actual) home‑office method
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A (QBI deduction), if applicable
- Schedule B only if interest/dividend > $1,500 or if other conditions apply
- Keep Forms 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC (if you received them), 1099‑INT, 1099‑DIV, and 1098‑E (student loan interest statement)

State return (Arizona)
- File Arizona resident return (Form 140 or current AZ resident form). Arizona rules differ from federal in places; use your federal AGI as the starting point but watch for state adjustments (and check whether student loan interest or QBI have any state effects).

Documentation to keep
- Form 1098‑E from MOHELA showing interest paid.
- Donation receipt from Northwest Harvest.
- Records of consulting income (invoices, bank deposits, 1099s).
- Receipts for the $950 unreimbursed expenses and documentation tying them to the consulting business (if they’re business expenses).
- Rent receipts/lease and utility bills (if using actual home‑office method), showing amounts and dates and proof of square footage.
- Brokerage statements and 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV/1099‑B from TD Ameritrade and Goldman Sachs.

Other considerations / next steps
- If you prefer simplicity and lower recordkeeping, use the simplified home‑office method ($700) — but with your rent numbers the actual method likely gives a substantially larger tax benefit.
