  - Charitable gifts: $1,400 (to Northwest Harvest) — you have receipt
  - Unreimbursed expenses: $950
  - Home office: 140 sq ft exclusive; home is 690 sq ft; rent $2,100/month

2) Key clarifying questions (please answer so I can be specific)
- Was the $12,500 consulting income earned as an independent contractor (you received 1099-NEC) or as an employee (on a W-2)? This determines whether home office and unreimbursed expenses are deductible.
- The student loan interest: did you pay $3,600 in interest during tax year 2024 (payments made in 2024)? Or is that the total interest paid in earlier years (2016–2019)? Only interest paid in the tax year is deductible on that year’s return.
- Do you have other potential itemizable items (mortgage interest, property tax, state income tax or large medical expenses)? If so, list approximate amounts.

3) Which deductions you can take (based on common rules)
- Student loan interest
  - If you paid student loan interest in 2024, you can claim the student loan interest deduction (an above-the-line adjustment) up to $2,500 (subject to income phaseouts). With your combined income (~$93k before adjustments), you should be under the phaseout threshold for MFJ in recent years — so you’d likely get the full allowed amount (but the deduction is capped at $2,500 even if you paid $3,600).
  - You should receive Form 1098‑E from MOHELA showing interest paid in the year you actually paid it.
- Charitable contributions
  - Only deductible if you itemize on Schedule A. There is no general federal cash charitable deduction for non‑itemizers for tax years after the temporary COVID relief years unless special legislation occurs. So if you take the standard deduction, the $1,400 gift is not deductible on your federal return.
  - Keep the receipt (you have it) and ensure Northwest Harvest is a qualifying 501(c)(3).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses
  - If these $950 are unreimbursed employee expenses (you were an employee and these were work expenses), they are generally NOT deductible on federal returns for tax years since the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act (suspended miscellaneous itemized deductions) unless you’re in a special category (e.g., certain performing artists, qualified reservists, etc.).
  - If these are business expenses for your consulting (i.e., you are self‑employed), they are deductible on Schedule C.
- Home office
  - Deductible only if you are self‑employed (use of part of your home as your principal place of business for your trade). Not deductible if you are an employee.
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified method: $5 × square footage of office (up to 300 sq ft). For 140 sq ft → $700 deduction.
    - Actual expense method: use business percentage of home to allocate rent, utilities, etc. Your business-use percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%. Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200. Rent portion allocable to home office ≈ $25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,114 (you could also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, internet, etc.). Note: the home-office deduction cannot exceed your net self‑employment income from that business; any excess may carry forward (rules apply).
  - If you use the actual method and are self‑employed, you typically use Form 8829 (expenses for business use of your home) when filing Schedule C. If you use the simplified method, you just claim it on Schedule C (no Form 8829).
- Interest income
  - Your $1,400 interest is taxable. Schedule B is required only if combined interest/dividends exceed $1,500 or you have certain foreign accounts. At $1,400 you probably do not need Schedule B, but you still report the 1099‑INT amounts on Form 1040.

4) Itemize vs standard deduction (likely outcome)
- For married filing jointly, the standard deduction is substantially larger than $1,400 (standard deduction for MFJ in recent years is in the tens of thousands). Based only on the items you listed (charity $1,400, no mortgage interest, no big state or property taxes), you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction on your federal return.
- However, student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction (reduces AGI) and is available whether or not you itemize (subject to limits).
- If you are self‑employed and can claim the home office and other business expenses that reduce your taxable income significantly, that reduces your taxable income regardless of whether you itemize. But home office and business expense deductions are Schedule C items, not Schedule A.

5) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments to income (student loan interest deduction is reported here).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — if your consulting was self‑employment/1099 income.
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax (if self‑employed).
- Form 8829 — to calculate actual home office expenses if you use the actual expense method (for self‑employed). If you use simplified method, you don’t file Form 8829.
- Schedule A — Itemized Deductions, only if you choose to itemize (for charitable donations, state & local taxes, mortgage interest, etc.).
- Schedule B — for interest and ordinary dividends only if total interest + dividends > $1,500 or other conditions apply.
- Form 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC / 1099‑INT — gather these informational forms from payers (and your W‑2 from Riverside Hospital).
- State tax return (Arizona) — AZ filing has its own rules; Arizona generally conforms to federal rules in many ways but check for differences (e.g., state standard deduction vs itemizing). You’ll need your Form 140 (AZ) or appropriate AZ form.

6) Other tax consequences to watch for
- Self‑employment tax: If your consulting is self‑employment, net profit is subject to self‑employment tax (~15.3%), with half of that deductible as an adjustment.
- Estimated tax payments: If you are self‑employed and have tax due (after withholding), consider making quarterly estimated payments to avoid penalties.
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: If you’re self‑employed, you may be eligible for up to a 20% deduction on qualified business income (subject to limits). This can affect your taxable income — discuss with a preparer if applicable.

