Summary answer (short)
- Likely take the standard deduction (you only list $1,400 of charitable gifts and you rent, so itemizing is unlikely to beat the standard deduction).
- You can claim a student loan interest adjustment (up to $2,500) only for interest you actually paid in the tax year — not for interest paid in earlier years; that reduces your AGI even if you take the standard deduction.
- Your consulting income is reported on Schedule C and is subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). You can claim a home office deduction for the consulting work; based on the numbers you provided, the actual method (allocating a share of rent/expenses) will likely give a larger deduction than the simplified method.
- Unreimbursed expenses are deductible only if they are ordinary/necessary business expenses for your consulting (not nondeductible employee unreimbursed expenses for your W‑2 job).
- Required forms: Form 1040, Schedule 1 (adjustments), Schedule C (profit/loss), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Form 8829 (or the simplified option on Schedule C) for home office if using actual; Schedule A only if you decide to itemize; Schedule B only if your taxable interest or dividends exceed the threshold or other triggers apply.

Details and calculations

1) Income & reporting
- W‑2 wages (Riverside Hospital): $79,400 — reported on Form 1040 from your W‑2.
- Consulting income: $12,500 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). If net profit > $400, you must file Schedule SE and pay self‑employment tax.
- Interest: $920 (Goldman Sachs) + $480 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,400 — report as taxable interest on Form 1040. You only need Schedule B if total interest/dividends > $1,500 or other triggers (foreign accounts, etc.), so probably not required here.

2) Deductions and adjustments
- Standard deduction vs. itemizing:
  - You listed $1,400 in charitable contributions. Unless you have substantial other itemizable deductions (state/local tax, mortgage interest, large unreimbursed medical expenses >7.5% of AGI, or large property taxes), your itemized total is almost certainly less than the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly.
  - So you will most likely take the standard deduction. (If you want, tell me other possible itemizable items so I can compare exactly.)
- Student loan interest:
  - The student loan interest “deduction” is an above‑the‑line adjustment (reported on Schedule 1) up to $2,500 for interest actually paid in the tax year and subject to income phaseouts. It reduces your AGI even if you don’t itemize.
  - You mentioned $3,600 (MOHELA, 2016–2019) — I need to confirm: did you actually pay $3,600 in interest in tax year 2024? Or is that an earlier-period amount? Only interest paid in 2024 can be deducted on your 2024 return.
- Unreimbursed expenses ($950):
  - If those are business expenses related to your consulting (e.g., supplies, travel for clients, phone or software used for consulting), they belong on Schedule C as ordinary and necessary business expenses and will reduce your consulting net income.
  - If those are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, under current law (post‑TCJA) they are generally not deductible for most employees.

3) Home office (you said: 140 sq ft exclusive; home = 690 sq ft; rent $2,100/mo)
- Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 per sq ft × 140 sq ft = $700 (max 300 sq ft). Easy to use.
  - Actual method: allocate actual home expenses by the business-use percentage. Your business-use percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%.
    - Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200
    - Rent portion allocable to business ≈ $25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,112
    - If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, internet, etc., multiply those annual costs by 20.29% and add them — the actual method will usually yield a much larger deduction than the simplified method when rent and utilities are sizable.
- How to claim:
  - If self‑employed (consulting reported on Schedule C) you claim the home office deduction on Schedule C. For the actual method you file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home); for the simplified method you claim it directly on Schedule C.
  - The home office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit (so it reduces both income tax and self‑employment tax).
  - Note: the home office must be used regularly and exclusively for business to qualify.

4) Self‑employment tax
- Because you have $12,500 in consulting income, you’ll likely owe self‑employment (SE) tax on net profit from the consulting after allowable business deductions. SE tax is computed on Schedule SE. You may also deduct half of your SE tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1.

5) Forms you’ll need (primary)
- Form 1040 — main return
- Schedule 1 — additional income and adjustments (student loan interest adjustment, half SE tax, etc.)
- Schedule C — report consulting income and business expenses
- Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 — business use of home (if using actual method). If you use the simplified method, you don’t file Form 8829 but note the simplified calculation on Schedule C.
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (charitable contributions and other itemized deductions)
- Schedule B — only if total interest/dividends > $1,500 or other triggers
- Keep Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statements) and any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC you receive for consulting, plus receipts for charitable contributions and business expenses.

6) Documentation to have ready
- 1098‑E from MOHELA (student loan interest paid in 2024)
- Receipts or bank records for charitable donations ($1,400)
- Records of consulting income (1099s if any) and receipts for business expenses (including the $950 if business‑related)
- Rent receipts/lease and utility bills (to substantiate home office actual method)
- Records of interest income (1099‑INT statements from Goldman Sachs and TD Ameritrade)

7) A few cautions and tips
- Confirm whether the $3,600 student interest is interest you actually paid in 2024; otherwise it cannot be deducted on your 2024 return.
- If the $950 unreimbursed is actually business expense for consulting, include it on Schedule C; if it’s unreimbursed employee expense for your W‑2 job, it’s likely nondeductible.
- If you take the actual home‑office method, make sure the office area is used exclusively and regularly for the consulting business (the IRS requires exclusive use).
- If you expect to owe tax because of the consulting income, consider estimated quarterly payments (Form 1040‑ES) to avoid underpayment penalties.

Quick example (to illustrate effect of home office)
- Business‑use %: 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%
- Annual rent: $25,200 → business portion ≈ $5,112 (actual method)
- Simplified home office: $700
- If you have no other business expenses, actual method makes more sense here because $5,112 > $700. That lowers your net Schedule C income and reduces both income tax and SE tax.

What I need from you to finalize this precisely
