I can help — below is a clear, practical breakdown of what you can and can’t deduct, whether itemizing likely helps, how to treat the home office, which federal/state forms you’ll need, and next steps. I’m not a CPA — this is general guidance. For final filing or complex situations please confirm with a tax professional or tax software.

Quick summary recommendation
- Most likely you should take the standard deduction for 2024 for married filing jointly (it’s substantially larger than the itemizable items you listed).  
- Treat the $12,500 consulting as self‑employment income (report on Schedule C) if you were an independent contractor (no W‑2). That lets you deduct a home‑office share and business expenses on Schedule C and triggers self‑employment tax (Schedule SE).  
- Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line adjustment (subject to a maximum and income phaseouts) — not an itemized deduction.  
- Charitable gift ($1,400) is an itemized deduction (Schedule A) but by itself is unlikely to exceed the standard deduction.

What to report and how
1. W-2 wages (Riverside Hospital $79,400)
- Report on Form 1040, Line for wages (attach W‑2). No special treatment beyond withholding/credits.

2. Consulting income ($12,500)
- Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) if you were an independent contractor. If you were an employee and these were unreimbursed employee expenses, those generally are not deductible (2018–2025 miscellaneous itemized deductions suspended).  
- On Schedule C you can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses (supplies, mileage, portion of phone/internet used for business, etc.). The $950 “unreimbursed expenses” — if they were business expenses for your consulting — belong on Schedule C, not as an itemized deduction.

3. Home office (140 sq ft exclusive; home 690 sq ft; rent $2,100/month)
- You can only take a home‑office deduction for the consulting business if the space is used regularly and exclusively for that business. You cannot take it for the W‑2 hospital job.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 / sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For 140 sq ft → 140 × $5 = $700 deductible on Schedule C.
  b) Actual method: business percentage = 140 / 690 = ~20.29%. Deduct that percentage of allowable home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.). With rent alone: 20.29% × ($2,100 × 12 = $25,200) ≈ $5,114. Add business portion of utilities, internet, etc., if applicable.
- Note: home office deduction reduces Schedule C net profit and thus reduces both income tax and self‑employment tax. However the home‑office deduction cannot exceed net profit from the business in the year (excess may carry forward in certain cases).

4. Interest income ($920 from Marcus + $480 from TD Ameritrade = $1,400)
- Report as taxable interest on Form 1040. Schedule B is generally required only if interest/dividends exceed $1,500 or other conditions apply — your total is $1,400, so Schedule B likely not required. Keep 1099‑INT statements.

5. Student loan interest ($3,600)
- Student loan interest deduction (up to $2,500 max in most years) is an adjustment to income (Schedule 1) — not an itemized deduction. You cannot deduct more than the allowed maximum (historically $2,500), and the deduction phases out based on your modified adjusted gross income (MAGI) for married filing jointly (there are income limits). If your MAGI is over the phaseout threshold you may not be eligible for the full $2,500 (or any). Confirm exact 2024 phaseout thresholds for MFJ; if your combined income (after adjustments) is below the phaseout you can claim up to $2,500.

6. Charitable donation ($1,400 to Northwest Harvest)
- Deductible on Schedule A if you itemize. You must have the receipt — keep it. With only $1,400 in charity and little/no other itemized deductions (no mortgage interest, limited SALT if you rent), you’ll almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction.

7. Unreimbursed expenses ($950)
- If these are business expenses for consulting, they belong on Schedule C (deductible). If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job, they are not deductible (suspended under current law).

Other items to consider
- Self‑Employment tax: If you report $12,500 net (less business expenses) on Schedule C, you’ll owe self‑employment tax (~15.3% on net SE income). You report this on Schedule SE; half of the self‑employment tax is an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- QBI (Qualified Business Income) deduction: You may be eligible for up to 20% of qualified business income from the consulting on Form 8995 (simple) or 8995‑A. Whether you get the full 20% depends on taxable income and the nature of the business.
- Estimated tax payments: If self‑employment tax and income tax are sizable and not sufficiently covered by withholding, you should consider making quarterly estimated payments to avoid penalties.

Itemize vs Standard deduction
- Standard deduction for married filing jointly in 2024 is substantially larger than the $1,400 charitable donation plus likely small state/local taxes you have. Given what you listed, the standard deduction will almost certainly be better than itemizing.
- The home office and business expenses reduce your Schedule C net profit whether you itemize or not — they are not Schedule A itemized deductions. Student loan interest reduces AGI (Schedule 1), also independent of itemizing.

Which federal forms you will likely need
- Form 1040 — main return
- Schedule 1 — adjustments to income (student loan interest, half SE tax if applicable)
- Schedule C — report consulting income and business expenses (including home office)
- Schedule SE — calculate self‑employment tax
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (likely not)
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — if you claim the QBI (20% pass‑through deduction)
- 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC (if issued to you) and 1099‑INT statements for interest

Arizona state filing
- You’ll file Arizona resident return (Form 140 for AZ residents) and start with your federal AGI. Arizona follows many federal rules but has state‑specific adjustments — check AZ instructions. Arizona also allows either the standard deduction or itemized deductions (different amounts than federal). If you rent, you won’t have mortgage interest/property taxes for SALT unless you paid other deductible state taxes. Consider state withholding/estimated payments if needed.

What I need from you to give a more precise estimate or prepare calculations
- Confirm whether the $12,500 consulting was received as an independent contractor (1099‑NEC) or was W‑2 related. (If 1099, use Schedule C.)  
- Confirm whether the $950 unreimbursed expenses were business expenses for consulting (supplies, travel, internet) or employee expenses for the hospital job.  
- Confirm that the $3,600 student loan interest was interest you actually paid in tax year 2024 (and that you weren’t claimed as a dependent).  
- Any other deductible items you didn’t mention (mortgage interest, property tax, state income tax paid, large medical expenses, or large unreimbursed business expenses)?  
- Utility/internet/renter’s insurance amounts if you want the actual home‑office calculation (or I can use rent only).

Rough example comparison (illustrative)
- If you claim simplified home office $700 and deduct $950 business expenses on Schedule C: business expenses = $1,650 → net self‑employment income ≈ $10,850. Income tax impact plus SE tax would follow.  
- If you use actual home office based on rent only (~$5,114) + $950 business expenses = $6,064 → net ≈ $6,436. That larger business deduction reduces your taxable income and SE tax versus the simplified method — but actual method requires tracking home expenses and cannot exceed business income.

Next steps I can do for you
- If you want, I can run a rough tax estimate (federal and Arizona) using the numbers you gave — I’ll need answers to the clarifying questions above (especially whether the $950 and home office tie to consulting).  
- I can also draft a checklist of documents to gather for filing (W‑2, 1099s, 1099‑INT, student loan 1098‑E, receipts for charity, business receipts, rent and utility records for the home office, etc.).  
- Or I can draft a short email you can send to a tax preparer with these facts to get a professional estimate.

Which of these would you like next? If you want the rough tax estimate, answer the clarifying questions and confirm whether you want the simplified or actual home‑office calculation (I can compute both).

