Quick summary (short answer)
- Your consulting income is self‑employment income: report it on Schedule C and pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE).
- Home‑office is deductible for your consulting work only (if the space is used exclusively and regularly). You can choose the simplified ($5/sq ft) or the regular (pro rata of actual home expenses, which uses Form 8829) method.
- Student loan interest: maximum deduction is $2,500 (above‑the‑line) but it is subject to MAGI phaseouts — check your AGI/MAGI to see whether you can take the full $2,500.
- Charitable gift ($1,400) is deductible only if you itemize. With the numbers you gave, standard deduction for married filing jointly will almost certainly be larger than your likely itemized total, so you’ll likely take the standard deduction.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for a W‑2 job) are NOT deductible under current law (suspended through 2025). If the $950 is for your consulting business, it’s deductible on Schedule C.
- Forms you’ll use: Form 1040, Schedule C, Schedule SE, (maybe Form 8829 if you use the regular home‑office method), possibly Schedule B for interest if total interest/dividends > $1,500. Also file your Arizona return (AZ resident form).

