1) What’s deductible and where it’s reported
- W-2 wages (Riverside Hospital $79,400): reported on Form 1040 (W-2).
- Consulting income ($12,500): report on Schedule C (profit or loss from business). All income from clients is taxable even if you don’t get 1099s.
- Interest income ($920 + $480 = $1,400): report as interest income on Form 1040. If total interest/dividends > $1,500 or other triggers, you’d use Schedule B; at $1,400 you likely won’t need Schedule B.
- Student loan interest ($3,600 paid): the student loan interest deduction (above-the-line) is limited to $2,500 per year (subject to income phase-outs). You’ll claim it on Schedule 1 (adjustments to income) on Form 1040 — but you can only claim up to the allowed amount after MAGI rules.
- Charitable donation ($1,400 to Northwest Harvest): deductible on Schedule A (itemized deductions) if you itemize. Keep the receipt.
- Unreimbursed expenses ($950): whether deductible depends on what they are:
  - If they’re ordinary business expenses for your consulting work (supplies, advertising, travel for clients, phone portion used for business, etc.), claim them on Schedule C.
  - If they’re unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W-2 job, those are not deductible for federal taxes (suspended under current law for most employees).
- Self-employment tax: Social Security/Medicare taxes on net self-employment income are reported on Schedule SE. You pay SE tax (roughly 15.3% on net SE income) and get to deduct one-half of the SE tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1.

2) Home office — two methods and which to pick
You have a 140 sq ft exclusive home office in a 690 sq ft home. That’s 140/690 = ~20.29% of home area.

- Simplified method:
  - IRS flat $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft.
  - 140 sq ft × $5 = $700 deduction.
  - No Form 8829 required; easier to claim.

- Actual (regular) method:
  - Allocate actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc.) by the business percentage (≈20.29%) and report via Form 8829 with Schedule C.
  - Example: rent $2,100/mo = $25,200/yr × 20.29% ≈ $5,114 just for the rent portion. Add allocated utilities, internet, renter’s insurance, etc., and you may get a much larger deduction than the simplified amount.
  - Limitation: home office deduction (actual method) cannot exceed your net profit from that business for the year. Excess may be carried forward (rules differ depending on situation). Also, deductible home-office expenses reduce self-employment net income and therefore reduce SE tax.

Which to pick? If your allocable portion of rent + utilities is significantly more than $700, actual method will usually give a bigger deduction — assuming your consulting net income is high enough that the deduction isn’t fully limited. If you want simplicity or if you expect modest benefit, use simplified.

3) Quick example calculations (estimates)
Assumptions: consulting income $12,500; business expenses (supplied) $950. I’ll show two scenarios—simplified and actual home-office—roughly to show impact.

- Scenario A — simplified home-office:
  - Consulting gross: $12,500
  - Business expenses (other): $950
  - Home-office simplified: $700 (Schedule C expense)
  - Net self-employment income ≈ 12,500 − 950 − 700 = $10,850
  - SE tax base = 92.35% × $10,850 ≈ $10,021 → SE tax ≈ $1,532
  - Deductible half of SE tax ≈ $766 (above-the-line)

- Scenario B — actual home-office (rent allocation only, no utilities included for simplicity):
  - Rent allocation ≈ $5,114 (20.29% of $25,200)
  - Business expenses (other): $950
  - Net self-employment income ≈ 12,500 − 950 − 5,114 = $6,436
  - SE tax base = 0.9235 × $6,436 ≈ $5,944 → SE tax ≈ $908
  - Deductible half ≈ $454

Note: In Scenario B your home-office deduction is much larger and reduces your net self-employment income (and your SE tax). Which scenario gives the best bottom-line federal tax outcome depends on how those deductions change your AGI, any phaseouts (student loan interest), and your overall tax bracket.

4) Standard deduction vs itemizing
- For Married Filing Jointly (tax year 2024) the standard deduction is about $29,200.
- Your known potential itemized deductions right now: charitable $1,400 + any state/local taxes you paid (SALT capped at $10,000) + mortgage interest (if any) + property taxes (if any). From the numbers you gave, unless you have significant state/local taxes or mortgage interest or additional charitable gifts, your itemized total will almost certainly be less than the MFJ standard deduction — so you’ll likely take the standard deduction.
- Student loan interest (up to $2,500) is an adjustment to income (taken whether or not you itemize) — subject to income limits.

5) Forms you’ll likely need
- Form 1040 (federal return).
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income — student loan interest, half SE tax deduction, etc.).
- Schedule C (profit/loss from business — your consulting income and expenses).
- Schedule SE (self-employment tax).
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual home-office method.
- Schedule A (itemized deductions) — only if you decide to itemize.
- 1099-NEC or 1099-MISC (if issued by clients), but you must report self-employment income even if you don’t receive 1099s.
- 1099-INT (bank interest) and any 1099-B, 1099-DIV from brokerages as applicable.
- Arizona state return (AZ Form 140 or whatever the current AZ form is for the year) — state filing will follow its own rules.

6) Recordkeeping and documentation to gather
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital.
- Records/1099s for consulting income (if any), and a record of all consulting payments received.
- 1099-INTs (Marcus, TD Ameritrade) or year-end statements showing interest.
- Student loan interest statement (Form 1098-E) from MOHELA showing $3,600 paid.
- Charity receipt for $1,400 (you have this).
- Copies of rent payments, landlord statement, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs — to support the actual home-office method.
- A simple floor plan or note showing home size and office size (140 sq ft vs 690 sq ft).
- Receipts/records for the $950 in expenses and any other business expenses.

7) Next steps / Recommendations
- Confirm whether the $950 unreimbursed expenses are business-related (if yes, include on Schedule C).
- Gather rent and utility numbers so you can compare actual vs simplified home-office methods accurately.
- Unless you have sizable state/local taxes or other itemizable items, you’ll probably take the standard deduction (MFJ ~ $29,200 for 2024).
- Use the actual home-office method if your allocated rent+utilities materially exceeds $700 and will not be limited by net profit; otherwise use the simplified method for ease.
- Because student loan interest you paid is $3,600, you’ll likely be limited to the $2,500 student loan interest deduction (subject to income phaseout; based on the incomes you reported, you’ll likely be under the phaseout threshold).
- Consider estimated tax payments if you expect significant tax due from self-employment income (to avoid underpayment penalties).

8) Want me to run numbers for you?
If you want, I can estimate your federal tax liability and show the comparison (simplified vs actual home-office, and itemize vs standard). I’ll need:
- Confirmation that the $950 is for the consulting business (or a breakdown).
