I’m not a CPA, but here’s a clear, practical walkthrough of how your 2024 federal (and brief AZ) filing will likely work, what you can deduct, and which forms you’ll need. I’ll flag places where I need a quick clarification from you.

Quick summary / likely outcome
- Report W-2 wages: $79,400.
- Report consulting as self-employment on Schedule C: $12,500 gross.
- Report interest income: $920 (Goldman) + $480 (TD) = $1,400 (report on Form 1040; Schedule B not required because total < $1,500).
- Home office: you can take a home-office deduction for the self-employment work. Because your home office is sizable (140 sq ft of 690 sq ft ≈ 20.29%), the regular method (apportioning rent) will almost certainly give a larger deduction than the simplified method. I show an example below.
- Student loan interest: only the interest you actually paid in the tax year is eligible and the deduction is capped (see below).
- Charitable gift $1,400: deductible only if you itemize.
- Likely better to take the standard deduction (for 2024 married filing jointly the standard deduction is $29,200) unless you have substantial additional itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, high state/local taxes, large medical expenses, etc.). Based on the numbers you gave, standard deduction is almost certainly larger than itemized total.

Important clarifying questions
1. The $3,600 student loan interest — was that interest paid in 2024 or is that a total from 2016–2019? (Only interest actually paid in the tax year you’re filing for is deductible.)
2. The $950 “unreimbursed expenses” — are these expenses for your consulting/self-employment activity (i.e., business expenses you paid yourself)? Or are they unreimbursed employee expenses (for a W-2 job)? (Only self-employment business expenses go on Schedule C; unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible for 2018–2025.)
3. Do you have any mortgage interest, property tax, or sizable state income tax payments that would change the itemize vs standard analysis?
4. Did any clients issue you 1099-NEC/1099-MISC for the consulting income? (You must report all income even if no 1099 was issued.)

Worked example with your numbers (illustrative)
1) Income (total)
- W-2 wages: $79,400
- Consulting (Schedule C gross): $12,500
- Interest: $1,400
Total gross income = $93,300

2) Business expenses and home office
- Home size: 140 sq ft exclusive use / 690 total = 140/690 ≈ 0.2029 (≈20.29%).
- Rent: $2,100/month → $25,200/year. Home-office share of rent = 0.2029 × $25,200 ≈ $5,116.
- Other business expenses you listed: $950 (assumed business expense).
If both apply to Schedule C, total business expenses ≈ $5,116 + $950 = $6,066.
Net profit (Schedule C) ≈ $12,500 − $6,066 = $6,434.

(Alternative simplified home-office method = $5 × 140 sq ft = $700. The regular method using rent gives a higher deduction here.)

3) Self-employment tax and adjustment
- Net earnings subject to SE tax ≈ net profit × 92.35% ≈ $6,434 × 0.9235 ≈ $5,940.
- Self-employment tax ≈ 15.3% × $5,940 ≈ $908.
- Half of SE tax (≈ $454) is an above-the-line deduction on Form 1040.

4) Student loan interest
- Maximum student loan interest adjustment is $2,500/year (if you qualify by MAGI). You reported $3,600 — only the amount paid in 2024 (and up to the $2,500 cap, subject to phaseouts) is deductible. Based on the income shown, you’re well below MAGI phaseout thresholds, so you’d likely be eligible for the deduction (up to $2,500), but confirm the actual 2024 payment amount.

5) Adjusted Gross Income (rough)
- Start: $93,300 total income
- Less Schedule C expenses yield “business net” already included above, so total income after business expenses ≈ $87,234 (which is wages + interest + net SE profit).
- Less half SE tax (≈ $454) and student loan interest (assume up to $2,500 if paid in 2024) → approximate AGI ≈ $84,280 (this is illustrative).

6) Standard vs. itemize
- Standard deduction (MFJ, 2024): $29,200.
- Your known itemized items: charitable $1,400. Without large state/local tax, mortgage interest, or other itemizable items, itemizing will be far less than $29,200. So standard deduction is almost certainly the better choice.

Other items and potential benefit: QBI
- You may be eligible for the qualified business income (QBI) deduction (20% of qualified business income) on Schedule C income, claimed on Form 8995 (or 8995-A). Because your taxable income is well below the applicable thresholds, you would likely qualify for the simple 20% deduction on your qualifying self-employment income net of deductions — but it’s computed after accounting for your Schedule C net. With relatively small net profit (~$6,400) the QBI benefit would be small but still worth calculating. This is reportable on Form 1040 via Form 8995.

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule 1 (if you claim adjustments such as student loan interest or half SE tax; Schedule 1 flows to 1040).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report consulting income and business expenses, and to claim the home-office deduction as part of business expenses.
- Schedule SE — to calculate self-employment tax.
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — for the QBI deduction (if applicable).
- Form 1099-INT(s) — include interest statements you receive (Goldman, TD). If you got 1099-NEC for consulting, include income but all earned income must be reported even without a 1099.
- If student loan servicer provided Form 1098-E for interest paid in 2024, use that to support the deduction.
