I’m not a CPA or tax attorney, but here’s a practical, IRS-rule-based review of your situation for tax year 2024 and what I recommend you check/do next.

Short answer
- For federal taxes you will almost certainly take the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly (2024 standard deduction = $29,200) — your only certain itemizable deduction you listed is $1,400 in charitable contributions, which is far below the standard deduction.
- You should report your consulting income on Schedule C (and pay self‑employment tax via Schedule SE). The home office deduction can be taken only against your consulting (self‑employment) income — it does not apply to W‑2 wages — and you have two methods (regular or simplified). The regular method (allocating rent) will likely give a larger home‑office deduction than the simplified method, but it requires more substantiation.
- Student loan interest: you paid $3,600 but the maximum federal student‑loan interest deduction is $2,500 (and it phases out at higher MAGI). So you can only claim up to $2,500 if your MAGI is under the phaseout range.
- Unreimbursed W‑2 employee expenses are not deductible for federal returns (suspended under current law).

Detailed breakdown and numbers

Income to report
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital: $79,400 → report on Form 1040 (W‑2).
- Consulting: $12,500 → report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). If you have business expenses related to that consulting, they reduce your Schedule C net profit.
- Interest/dividends: $920 (Goldman Sachs Marcus) + $480 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,400 → report as interest/dividend income. (If total interest/dividends exceed Schedule B thresholds you’d complete Schedule B — your total is below the usual $1,500 threshold, so Schedule B likely isn’t required.)

Home office (you said 140 sq ft exclusive; total 690 sq ft; rent $2,100/month)
- You can only take a home office deduction if the space is used regularly and exclusively for the consulting business or another qualified business activity.
- Two methods:
  1. Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 140 sq ft × $5 = $700 (annual deduction). Simpler recordkeeping.
  2. Regular method: allocate actual home expenses. Example using your rent:
     - Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200
     - Business percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%
     - Rent portion deductible as home office = $25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,112 (plus a share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if applicable).
  - Regular method typically yields a much larger deduction here (≈$5,112 + portion of utilities) but requires Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and supporting records.
  - The home office deduction is taken on Schedule C (not on Schedule A) and can only reduce net profit from the business (subject to passive activity/at‑risk rules if those apply).
  - If the home office deduction creates or increases a Schedule C loss, that loss generally offsets other income (including W‑2) unless disallowed by other rules — so keep good records to justify the business nature.

Student loan interest ($3,600 paid to MOHELA for loans 2016–2019)
- The student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment up to $2,500 per return (not an itemized deduction).
- You paid $3,600, but the deductible maximum is $2,500. Also the deduction phases out based on MAGI for MFJ filers — you may be reduced or ineligible if your MAGI is above the phaseout range. Confirm your MAGI against the phaseout limits or have a preparer compute this.
- You’ll receive/should have Form 1098‑E showing the interest paid; use that to support the deduction.

Charitable contributions ($1,400)
- Deductible if you itemize (Schedule A) and if paid to qualifying charities (you have the receipt — good).
- Because the 2024 MFJ standard deduction is $29,200, $1,400 in charity alone won’t make itemizing beneficial unless you have other itemizable deductions (state taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, etc.).

Unreimbursed expenses ($950)
- If these are unreimbursed W‑2 employee expenses, they aren’t deductible for federal tax purposes (suspended through 2025 by TCJA).
- If these are business expenses for your consulting (self‑employed) work, they belong on Schedule C and are deductible against that business income.

Self‑employment tax and deductions
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment (SE) tax (~15.3% on ~92.35% of net earnings).
- You can deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040.
- Also check for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction (Section 199A) — with modest consulting income you may qualify for a QBI deduction up to 20% of qualified business income (subject to rules and taxable income limits).

Likely whether to itemize or take the standard deduction
- Based on what you’ve listed (charity $1,400 and no mortgage interest shown), you will almost certainly take the standard deduction ($29,200 for MFJ in 2024).
- The home office deduction is not part of Schedule A itemizing; it’s a Schedule C deduction and thus will reduce your adjusted gross income regardless of whether you itemize or take the standard deduction.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 — additional income and adjustments (student loan interest adjustment may appear here or as direct adjustment depending on form layout).
- Schedule C — profit/loss from consulting business.
- Schedule SE — self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — if you use the regular method for the home office deduction (to calculate allowable home office expense allocation).
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (probably not in your case).
- Schedule B — only if your interest/dividend totals exceed the reporting threshold (your total is under typical threshold).
- Form 8995/8995‑A — for Qualified Business Income deduction if applicable.
- Keep all 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC (if applicable), 1099‑INTs, W‑2, and Form 1098‑E (student loan interest).

State (Arizona)
- Arizona conforms to many federal rules but has its own adjustments and standard deduction rules; the state return may have differences for things like deductions and credits. Confirm with an Arizona tax resource or preparer for state-specific treatments (especially about unreimbursed employee expenses or charitable treatment).

Recordkeeping & substantiation
- Keep W‑2 and any 1099s (clients may issue 1099‑NEC for consulting).
- Keep receipts for charitable contributions.
- Keep Form 1098‑E for student loan interest.
- For home office (regular method) keep floor plan, photos, and records of utilities, rent payments, and how you calculated the business percentage and exclusivity.
- Keep records of any business expenses (receipts, invoices, mileage logs).

Practical next steps
1. Gather all documents: W‑2, any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV, 1098‑E, receipts for charitable contributions, documentation for unreimbursed expenses (identify whether they’re employee or business).
2. Decide home office method: simplified ($700) vs regular (≈$5,112 + utilities by allocation). If you want larger deduction and can substantiate, use regular method and prepare Form 8829.
3. Compute Schedule C net income and then compute self‑employment tax (Schedule SE) and the half‑SE tax adjustment.
