Thanks — I can help. Below is a clear, practical summary of what you can (and can’t) deduct, a rough calculation of the home‑office options, which filing choice is likely best for you, what forms you’ll need, and a few follow‑up questions so I can give a more precise estimate.

Key facts I used from your message
- Filing status: Married filing jointly (MFJ), Phoenix, AZ
- W-2 wages: $79,400 (Riverside Hospital)
- Self‑employment/consulting income: $12,500 (6 clients)
- Interest / investment income: $920 (Goldman Sachs Marcus) + $480 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,400
- Home: you pay $2,100/month for the 690 sq ft dwelling (rental)
- Home office: 140 sq ft exclusive use
- Charitable cash donation: $1,400 (receipt)
- Student loan interest: you listed $3,600 (needs clarification — see Qs)
- “Unreimbursed expenses”: $950 (need to know whether these are business expenses or employee unreimbursed)

What you can deduct (federal)
- Self‑employment business expenses (Schedule C)
  - Your consulting income ($12,500) should be reported on Schedule C (if you were an independent contractor).
  - Ordinary and necessary business expenses related to consulting are deductible on Schedule C (e.g., supplies, advertising, business portion of phone/internet, mileage, etc.).
  - If the $950 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for the consulting activity, include them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job, they are NOT deductible for federal returns (suspended through 2025 by TCJA).
- Home office deduction (only available for self‑employed use)
  - You meet the basic test if the 140 sq ft area is used exclusively and regularly for the consulting business.
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 140 sq ft × $5 = $700.
    - Actual method: business % × allowable home expenses. Business % = 140 ÷ 690 ≈ 20.29%.
      - Since you rent and pay $2,100/month → annual rent = $25,200. Business portion ≈ 20.29% × $25,200 ≈ $5,112.
      - Because you rent (no mortgage interest/depreciation to worry about), the actual method will likely produce a larger deduction (~$5,112) than the simplified ($700).
      - If you use the actual method, you report the home office deduction on Schedule C and may use Form 8829 to compute it (Form 8829 is normally used if you claim actual expenses — many tax software packages incorporate it).
- Self‑employment tax deduction
  - You will owe self‑employment (SE) tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net Schedule C profit; half of the SE tax is an “above‑the‑line” deduction on Form 1040 (reduces AGI).
- Student loan interest
  - Up to $2,500 of qualified student loan interest paid in the tax year may be deductible as an adjustment to income (Form 1040 adjustment). This deduction is phased out at higher MAGI and not available for married filing separately. You’ll need Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer showing interest paid for the tax year.
  - Your note “$3,600 (MOHELA, 2016–2019)” needs clarification — only interest paid in the tax year can be deducted. Past years’ interest is not deductible now unless you actually paid it in the tax year in question.
- Charitable donation ($1,400)
  - Charitable contributions are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Cash contributions with receipts are fine. For most MFJ taxpayers with modest deductions, standard deduction is often larger than available itemized deductions.
- State tax (Arizona)
  - State income tax you paid will be reported on your federal Schedule A if you itemize (subject to SALT $10,000 cap). You must also file Arizona Form 140 (or AZ e-file) for state taxes.

Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- For most taxpayers in settings like yours (no mortgage interest or large property taxes, only $1,400 in charitable gifts), the standard deduction for married filing jointly is likely larger than your itemized deductions — so you’ll probably take the standard deduction. (Note: exact standard deduction increases each year; check IRS amounts for the tax year you’re filing.)
- Important: the home‑office deduction is separate and available whether you itemize or not — it is taken on Schedule C and therefore does not affect the standard‑vs‑itemize decision.
- Quick check: if you have no other big itemized items (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes up to the SALT cap, big medical expenses above the floor, or sizable charitable gifts), take the standard deduction.

Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main form)
- Schedule 1 (if you have adjustments to income like student loan interest or educator expenses)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report $12,500 consulting income and business expenses
- Schedule SE — to calculate self‑employment tax and the deductible half of SE tax
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual method for the home office; if you use simplified, you report the square‑foot amount on Schedule C without 8829
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize (to claim the $1,400 charitable gift and any state taxes, etc.)
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) — you should get from MOHELA if interest was paid in the tax year
- Form 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC (if clients issued them) — used to reconcile your Schedule C income (even if you don’t receive 1099s you must report all income)
- Forms 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV — to report the $920 and $480 interest/dividends (if > $1,500 interest you’d also file Schedule B)
- Arizona state return (AZ Form 140) and any required state schedules

Rough example (illustrative only)
- Business gross: $12,500
- Home office actual deduction (rent portion): ≈ $5,112
- Other business expenses: if the $950 is business, +$950
- Estimated net Schedule C profit ≈ 12,500 − 5,112 − 950 ≈ $6,438
- Self‑employment tax (very approximate): SE tax ≈ 15.3% × (0.9235 × 6,438) ≈ $900–$1,000 (you also get half of that as an adjustment)
- Result: your Schedule C deduction (home office + other expenses) meaningfully reduces your self‑employment taxable income. Federal taxable income will then be W‑2 79,400 + net SE ~6,438 + interest 1,400 = ~87,238 before standard deduction, adjustments, etc. This is only illustrative — I can run exact numbers if you want.

Recordkeeping / practical tips
- Get Form 1098‑E from MOHELA for student loan interest paid in the tax year.
- Gather 1099s (1099‑NEC, 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV). Even if you didn’t receive a 1099, you still must report the income.
- Keep receipts for the $1,400 charitable donation and for any business expenses and home office (photos, floor plan, lease showing square footage help if ever asked).
- If you expect similar self‑employment income in the coming year, consider making estimated tax payments (or increase withholding) to cover income and SE tax.

Questions I need to finish this for you or produce a tighter tax estimate
1. Are you renting (yes — monthly rent $2,100) or do you own with mortgage interest? (Your message suggests renting.)
2. Are the $950 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for the consulting activity or unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job?
3. The student loan interest: did you actually pay $3,600 in student loan interest during the tax year you are filing? Or is $3,600 a total from prior years (2016–2019)? Do you have a 1098‑E showing interest paid in the tax year?
4. Did you receive any 1099‑NEC forms from clients, or any other 1099s not listed?
5. Do you want me to run an estimated tax calculation (approximate federal tax, including SE tax and effect of home office) using these numbers?

If you confirm the answers to those Qs I’ll:
