High-level summary (quick answer)
- You will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction for 2024 unless you have significant additional deductible items (mortgage interest, sizable state/local taxes up to the SALT cap, large medical expenses, or much larger charitable giving). With only $1,400 of cash charity shown, itemizing likely won’t beat the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly.
- Your consulting income is treated as self-employment income. Report it on Schedule C; you’ll owe self-employment tax on the net profit and income tax on taxable profit.
- You can deduct a home-office portion of your rent as a business expense on Schedule C (either simplified or actual method). That will reduce your self-employment taxable income.
- Student loan interest: only interest paid in the tax year is deductible (up to $2,500, subject to AGI phaseouts). If the $3,600 figure is interest paid in 2024, you can only deduct up to $2,500 (and check phaseout thresholds). If that $3,600 covers multiple past years (2016–2019), those earlier amounts are not deductible on your 2024 return.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for a W-2 job at Riverside Hospital) are not deductible for most taxpayers under current federal law. If the $950 are business expenses for the consulting work, they are deductible on Schedule C.

Details and calculations (estimate)
1) Income to report
- W-2 wages: $79,400 (Form W-2)
- Consulting: $12,500 (Schedule C — report all self-employment income even if you didn’t receive 1099s)
- Interest / investment: $920 (Goldman Sachs Marcus) and $480 (TD Ameritrade) — include these (reported on 1099-INT / 1099-DIV as appropriate).

2) Home-office treatment
- Space: 140 sq ft exclusive use, home = 690 sq ft → percentage = 140/690 ≈ 20.29%.
- Rent: $2,100/mo → $25,200/year. If you use the actual method, deductible rent portion = 25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,115 (deductible on Schedule C as a business expense).
- Simplified method: $5 × 140 sq ft = $700 (no Form 8829 needed). If you want a larger deduction and you have supporting records, actual method will likely give a bigger deduction here.
- If you use the actual method and have utilities or other shared home expenses, you may allocate the business portion as well. For renters you do not depreciate the structure.

3) Self-employment tax
- Net profit from consulting = 12,500 − home-office allocation − other business expenses (e.g., the $950 if it’s for consulting).
- You’ll pay self-employment tax (Schedule SE) on 92.35% of net profit. Half of the self-employment tax is an above-the-line deduction (reduces AGI).

4) Student loan interest
- Deduction is an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1) — not an itemized deduction.
- Maximum deduction generally $2,500/year (subject to MAGI phaseouts for MFJ). If the $3,600 is interest paid in 2024 you can usually deduct up to $2,500 (if your MAGI is below the phaseout range). If that $3,600 is interest paid across earlier years, that does not apply to your 2024 return — only interest paid in 2024 is deductible for 2024.

5) Unreimbursed expenses ($950)
- If these are W-2 unreimbursed employee expenses: not deductible on your federal return (TCJA suspension through 2025).
- If they are business expenses related to the consulting work: deduct on Schedule C.

6) Charitable giving ($1,400)
- Deductible if you itemize (supported by receipts). If you take the standard deduction, you cannot claim these on Schedule A.

Itemize vs. standard deduction
- For 2024 married filing jointly, the standard deduction is significant (inflation-adjusted). With only $1,400 of charitable donations and no mortgage interest or large SALT paid shown, itemizing is unlikely to exceed the standard deduction.
- Important: student loan interest and half of self-employment tax reduce AGI regardless of your choice to itemize; they are not part of Schedule A itemized deductions.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
