Thanks — this is a good list. Below I’ll (1) summarize which items look deductible, (2) compare itemizing vs the standard deduction, (3) explain how to treat the home office (with two calculation options), (4) list the federal (and state) forms you’ll likely need, and (5) ask a few quick clarifying questions so I can tighten the recommendations.

1) Items you listed — treatment and notes
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital ($79,400): report on Form 1040 (W‑2 income).
- Consulting income ($12,500, 6 clients): report as self‑employment income on Schedule C (Form 1040). Even if you didn’t get 1099s, you must report it.
- Interest: $920 (Goldman Sachs Marcus) + $480 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,400 taxable interest. Report on Form 1040 (you’ll get 1099‑INTs).
- Home office (140 sq ft exclusive; rental $2,100/month; total home 690 sq ft): deductible for self‑employed (see below for calculation options).
- Student loan interest: you listed $3,600. The student loan interest deduction maximum is $2,500 per year (above‑the‑line deduction) subject to income phaseouts for married filing jointly. You can deduct up to $2,500 if you meet the MAGI limits and the interest was actually paid in the tax year (and you have Form 1098‑E from MOHELA).
- Charitable gift $1,400 to Northwest Harvest: deductible only if you itemize (keep the receipt — cash donations $250+ require a written acknowledgement).
- $950 “unreimbursed expenses”: important — are these business expenses related to your consulting work (e.g., supplies, travel) or unreimbursed employee expenses for wages? If they are business expenses for your consulting, they’re deductible on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses (W‑2 job), those are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law.

2) Itemize vs Standard Deduction (short answer)
- For 2024 the standard deduction for married filing jointly is $29,200. (If you want, I can re‑confirm the most current IRS figure.)
- Your clearly itemizable amounts shown so far: charitable $1,400 (plus any state & local taxes and mortgage interest if applicable). Unless you have large state income/property taxes, mortgage interest, or other deductible amounts, your itemized total will be well below $29,200.
- Conclusion: it’s almost certainly better to take the standard deduction for 2024 based on the numbers you gave.

3) Home office — how to treat and quick calculations
As a self‑employed consultant you may claim a home office deduction if the area is used regularly and exclusively as your principal place of business.

Two methods:
A. Simplified method
- $5.00 per square foot up to 300 sq ft.
- For 140 sq ft: 140 × $5 = $700 deduction (no Form 8829 required; enter on Schedule C).

B. Actual (regular) method — likely larger in your case because you rent
- Percentage of home used for business = business area / total area = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%.
- Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200. Rent portion attributable to the office ≈ 25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,112.
- You can also include your proportionate share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., which would increase the deduction. Use IRS Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to calculate actual home office deduction, then transfer to Schedule C.
- Because your rent portion alone (~$5,100) is much larger than the simplified-method $700, the actual method will likely give a significantly bigger deduction — unless other limitations apply in your situation.

Note: home‑office deductions reduce Schedule C net profit and therefore reduce self‑employment tax and income tax. If the business shows a net loss, that loss can usually offset other income, subject to general loss/at‑risk rules.

4) Other tax impacts you should expect
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll owe SE (Social Security and Medicare) tax on net self‑employment earnings and you’ll report it on Schedule SE. You can deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: you may be eligible for up to a 20% deduction of qualified business income from your consulting if you otherwise meet the requirements — it’s claimed on Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) depending on income levels. With relatively small net consulting income, you may get a small QBI deduction.
- Student loan interest: reported as an adjustment on Schedule 1 (up to $2,500 subject to MAGI phaseouts). With the W‑2 and net business income you likely fall below the phaseout but I’d confirm estimated AGI.
- Estimated tax payments: with self‑employment income you may need to make quarterly estimated payments to cover income tax and SE tax unless your W‑2 withholding covers it.
- State tax (Arizona): you’ll file an Arizona resident return (AZ Form 140). Arizona tax rules mostly follow federal for many items, but there are differences — I can detail those if you want.

5) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — student loan interest deduction and other adjustments appear here
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report consulting revenue and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual method for the home office
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A (QBI deduction) — if you claim the 20% deduction
- Form 1099‑INTs (interest) and Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) — you’ll use these to support amounts reported
- Arizona: Form 140 (AZ resident return) and any AZ schedules as required

6) Records to keep / documentation
- W‑2 and any 1099s you received.
- 1099‑INTs and bank statements for interest.
- 1098‑E from MOHELA for student loan interest; receipts if interest was paid.
- Receipts/acknowledgements for the $1,400 charitable gift.
- Records supporting consulting income (invoices, bank deposits) and business expenses (the $950 and any other expenses).
- Lease/rent records and utility bills for calculating the home office percentage.

7) Quick example rough math (illustrative)
Using the actual home office rent portion:
- Consulting income: $12,500
- Home office (rent pro-rata): ≈ $5,112
- Other business expenses: $950 (if business-related)
- Net self‑employment profit ≈ $12,500 − $5,112 − $950 = $6,438
- SE tax on that net (rough approximation): net × 0.9235 × 15.3% ≈ ≈ $910 (you would deduct half ≈ $455 on Schedule 1)
- Taxable interest: $1,400
- W‑2 wages: $79,400
- Total income before adjustments ≈ $87,238; after SE adjustment and student-loan interest adjustment, AGI will change. With a standard deduction (~$29,200) your taxable income will be significantly reduced. This is a rough illustration — I can run a more precise estimate if you want.

8) Next steps / questions so I can be more precise
- Are the $950 unreimbursed expenses related to your consulting business (i.e., business expenses)? Or are they unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job?
- Was the $3,600 student loan interest paid in the tax year you are filing for (2024)? Did MOHELA send you a Form 1098‑E for that year?
- Do you have significant state or local taxes or mortgage interest that would increase itemized deductions?
- Do you want me to run a quick, approximate tax calculation (estimate federal tax owed/refund) using the numbers you gave and assuming either the simplified or actual home office method?

If you want, I can:
