Key points and treatment
1. Where each item goes
- W-2 wages: Report on Form 1040 (W-2).  
- Consulting income: Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). If you had no business expenses besides those you mentioned, report gross receipts $12,500 and list business expenses on Schedule C.  
- Bank interest: Report on Form 1040 (from 1099‑INT).  
- Home office: If the home office is used for your consulting business and is used regularly and exclusively, you can deduct a portion of rent/utilities as a business expense on Schedule C (actual method or simplified method). It does NOT go on Schedule A. It only applies to the self‑employment activity, not your W‑2 job.  
- Student loan interest: Reported as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (if the interest was paid in the tax year and you are eligible). Max allowed is $2,500 (subject to income phaseouts).  
- Charitable gift: Cash gift deductible on Schedule A (itemized deductions) — only if you itemize.  
- “Unreimbursed expenses”: If they are business expenses for your consulting, deduct on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job, they’re generally NOT deductible for 2018–2025.

2. Home-office calculation (actual-method example)
- Home office share = 140 ÷ 690 = 20.29%.  
- Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200. Rent portion allocable to home office ≈ $25,200 × 0.2029 ≈ $5,112.  
- You can also use the simplified method ($5 × 140 sq ft = $700) — choose the method that gives the larger deduction. In your case the actual allocation of rent (~$5,112) will be far more favorable. That amount is a Schedule C business expense (reduces net self-employment profit).

3. Self-employment tax and related adjustments (approximate)
- Example (if the $950 is a business expense):  
  - Business income 12,500 − home-office 5,112 − other business expenses 950 = net profit ≈ $6,438.  
  - Net earnings subject to SE (≈ 92.35% of profit) ≈ $5,943.  
  - Self-employment tax ≈ 15.3% × $5,943 ≈ $909.  
  - Half of SE tax (≈ $454) is an adjustment to income (Schedule 1), lowering AGI.  
  - Note: these are rounded estimates — exact numbers from your Schedule C will determine SE tax.

4. Student loan interest
- The student loan interest deduction is limited to $2,500 per year (maximum) and phases out at higher MAGI for married filing jointly. Your reported gross income (as shown above) is likely well under phaseout thresholds, but I need to confirm you actually paid $3,600 in interest in 2024 (and that you’ll receive Form 1098‑E). If you actually paid interest in prior years only (2016–2019), that is not deductible on your 2024 return.

5. Itemize vs standard deduction
- For married filing jointly the standard deduction is large (in recent years roughly in the high $20k range; it’s adjusted annually). Based on what you’ve listed (charitable gifts $1,400 and no mortgage interest, limited state taxes reported), your likely itemized deductions will be far less than the standard deduction. So you will almost certainly take the standard deduction unless you have other significant deductible items (large state income or property taxes, mortgage interest, high unreimbursed medical expenses, etc.). The home-office deduction is not a Schedule A item — it reduces business income instead — so it does not help push you to itemize.

