I’m not a CPA or tax attorney, but here’s a practical rundown of how your 2024 filing will likely work based on what you gave me, plus a short list of follow-up questions so I can be more exact.

Quick summary / recommendations
- Most likely you’ll take the 2024 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly ($29,200) unless you have substantial additional itemizable deductions (state income/property taxes, mortgage interest, large medical bills) you haven’t listed.
- The home-office deduction can only apply to your consulting (self‑employment) activity — not to your W‑2 job at Riverside Hospital. Claim it on Schedule C. Use the actual-expense method if you want to deduct your share of rent (and utilities) — it’s substantially larger here than the simplified method.
- Student loan interest deduction is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (not an itemized deduction) but is capped (see note below).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for W‑2 work are not deductible under current law; if your $950 of unreimbursed expenses were for the consulting work, include them on Schedule C.

Detailed breakdown and calculations

1) Income to report
- W-2 wages: $79,400 (report on Form 1040 using your W-2)
- Consulting (self-employment) income: $12,500 (report on Schedule C; you must report this even if you don’t get 1099s)
- Interest income: $920 (Marcus) + $480 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,400 total interest (report on Form 1040; Schedule B is generally required only if interest > $1,500 or other conditions)
Total gross income ≈ $93,300.

2) Home office (self-employment)
- Space: 140 sq ft exclusive use; home = 690 sq ft → business percentage = 140/690 ≈ 20.29%.
- Rent: $2,100/month → annual rent = $25,200.
- Actual-expense home office share of rent = 25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,115. This would be a Schedule C expense (and if you use actual method, you normally compute the allowable home office deduction with Form 8829).
- Simplified method: 140 sq ft × $5 = $700 (much smaller).
- Recommendation: Use the actual method if your only adjustable home expenses are rent and maybe utilities — actual yields the larger deduction here. Claim it on Schedule C; use Form 8829 for the calculation.

Note: home office deduction reduces net self‑employment income, which in turn reduces your self‑employment tax.

3) Other business expenses
- If the $950 “unreimbursed expenses” were for your consulting activity, include them on Schedule C as ordinary and necessary business expenses. If they’re unreimbursed expenses for your W-2 hospital job, they are not deductible under current law.

4) Self‑employment tax
- You’ll owe self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on your net self‑employment earnings. Example estimate assuming you treat the home office as actual ($5,115) and the $950 are business expenses:
  - Net self-employment income ≈ 12,500 − 5,115 − 950 = 6,435.
  - SE tax ≈ 15.3% × (net income × 0.9235) ≈ 15.3% × 5,943 ≈ $909 (approx).
  - Half of SE tax (~$454) is an adjustment to income (deductible on Schedule 1).

5) Student loan interest
- The student loan interest deduction maximum is $2,500 per year (you mentioned $3,600 — you can only deduct the amount of interest you actually paid in 2024, up to $2,500, and subject to income phaseouts).
- Eligibility phases out at higher AGI; with the incomes you reported, you are likely eligible for the full deduction, but confirm actual 2024 MAGI phaseout thresholds if near the threshold.
- You should receive Form 1098-E from your loan servicer (MOHELA) showing interest paid. If the $3,600 is total interest paid in 2024, you’ll only be able to deduct $2,500 of it (assuming you meet the AGI rules).

6) Charitable contribution
- $1,400 cash to Northwest Harvest — deductible only if you itemize (on Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you can’t deduct it on 1040 (except for specific years when there were special above-the-line rules — those do not apply in general for 2024).

7) Standard deduction vs itemizing
- 2024 standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly) = $29,200.
- Your itemizable items you’ve listed: $1,400 charitable + (any state/local taxes up to $10,000) + mortgage interest (none listed) + medical (only above 7.5% of AGI) etc. Unless you have several thousand dollars of state taxes or mortgage interest you haven’t listed, itemizing is unlikely to exceed $29,200. So standard deduction is most likely better for you.

8) Forms you’ll likely use
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments like half of SE tax and possibly student loan interest (reported as adjustment)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report consulting income and business expenses (including the home office expense)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if using actual-expense method for home office
- Schedule SE — to compute self-employment tax
- Form(s) 1099-INT (interest) and Form 1098-E (student loan interest) — keep these for records and reporting
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize
- Any 1099-NEC/1099-MISC you receive from clients (you still report the income even if you don’t receive 1099s)

Practical next steps / checklist
- Confirm whether the $3,600 student loan interest is the amount paid in 2024 and whether you received Form 1098‑E from MOHELA. If yes, what amount is shown for 2024?
- Confirm whether the $950 unreimbursed expenses are related to your consulting work (if yes, include on Schedule C).
- Confirm whether you paid significant state income tax or property tax in 2024 (these affect the itemize vs. standard decision).
- Decide whether you want to use the actual home-office method (requires Form 8829 and a bit more recordkeeping) or the simplified method ($5/sq ft).
- Keep receipts for charitable gift (acknowledgement), student loan interest statements, rent/utility bills, and receipts for business expenses.

Estimated numbers (based on assumptions)
- Gross income: ≈ $93,300
- Student loan interest deduction (assume max allowed): $2,500
- Half of SE tax (estimate): ~$454
- Estimated AGI (rough): ≈ 90,346 (this is a rough example; depends on confirmed numbers)
- Standard deduction (MFJ): $29,200 → likely best choice unless you tell me about large state taxes, mortgage interest, or big medical expenses.

