Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I’m not a CPA, but here’s a practical, step-by-step summary of how your 2024 return will generally work based on what you told me, plus a few calculations and next actions. If you want exact tax owed or complex planning, I recommend confirming with a tax professional or using tax software.

Quick facts you gave
- Filing status: Married filing jointly (Arizona)
- W-2 (Riverside Hospital): $79,400
- Self‑employment / consulting income: $12,500 (6 clients)
- Interest income: $920 (Goldman Sachs Marcus) + $480 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,400
- Home: renting, 690 sq ft total, home office 140 sq ft exclusive
- Rent: $2,100/month → $25,200/year
- Deductions / payments you listed:
  - Student loan interest (MOHELA): $3,600 (you said 2016–2019 — I’ll explain timing below)
  - Charitable cash gifts: $1,400 (receipt for Northwest Harvest)
  - Unreimbursed expenses: $950 (need to know whether these are employee-related or business-related)

What you can (generally) deduct
- Self-employment income and expenses:
  - Report consulting income on Schedule C (profit/loss from business).
  - You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (e.g., supplies, business-related portion of phone/Internet if used for business, business-related unreimbursed expenses).
  - If the $950 unreimbursed expenses are business-related (for your consulting), include them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible for most taxpayers under current law (post‑TCJA).
- Home office:
  - You appear to qualify if the 140 sq ft is used exclusively and regularly for the consulting business, and it is your principal place of business (or used substantially for admin/management of the business).
  - Two methods: simplified ($5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft) or actual expense method (business percentage of actual housing expenses).
  - Your business percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%.
    - Simplified: 140 * $5 = $700 deduction.
    - Actual: rent portion = $25,200 * 20.29% ≈ $5,112 of deductible rent (plus your share of utilities if you pay those). For a renter, actual method usually gives a larger deduction — so actual method looks preferable here.
  - Home office deduction reduces net Schedule C profit, which reduces income tax and self‑employment tax.
- Self-employment tax:
  - You must pay self‑employment tax on net earnings from self‑employment and file Schedule SE. You also get an “above‑the‑line” deduction for half of the self‑employment tax when computing AGI.
- Interest income:
  - Report the $1,400 on Form 1040’s interest line. Schedule B is generally required only if interest/dividends exceed $1,500 (you’re under that threshold).
- Charitable contributions:
  - $1,400 cash gift is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you cannot deduct it on your federal return.
- Student loan interest:
  - The student loan interest deduction only applies to interest you actually paid in the tax year being filed (2024). The maximum federal deduction is generally up to $2,500 of qualifying student loan interest (subject to income phaseouts). If the $3,600 you mentioned were paid in 2016–2019, those earlier payments do not create a deduction for 2024. If you have a 2024 Form 1098‑E (or records showing interest paid in 2024), you can claim up to the allowed limit subject to MAGI phaseouts.

Itemize vs standard deduction
- You currently list only $1,400 in charitable gifts as itemizable expense, plus any state/local taxes, mortgage interest, medical expenses, etc., you didn’t mention.
- Because the standard deduction for married filing jointly is substantially larger than $1,400, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction unless you have significant other itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, property taxes, large state taxes, large medical expenses above the AGI threshold, etc.).
- Recommendation: Compare itemized total (Schedule A) to the MFJ standard deduction for 2024. With the info given, standard deduction is almost certainly better.

Forms you’ll generally need
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 — main return.
  - Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — if applicable (e.g., adjustments like student loan interest).
  - Schedule C — report consulting income ($12,500) and business expenses (home office, business supplies, business portion of $950 if applicable).
  - Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax.
  - Schedule A — only if you itemize (charitable contributions, state/local tax, mortgage interest, etc.).
  - Form 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC — any you receive from clients (you still report the income even if you don’t receive 1099s).
  - Form 1099‑INT — for interest income (from Goldman and TD Ameritrade).
  - Form 1098‑E — student loan interest statement (if showing interest paid in 2024).
  - Form 1040‑ES — for estimated tax payments (if you need to make quarterly payments).
- Arizona (state):
  - Arizona individual return (Form 140 or current AZ form for residents). Arizona rules differ slightly; check AZ Dept. of Revenue instructions or ask a local preparer about state treatment of student loan interest and home office allocation rules. Some states limit or modify deductions.

Rough numbers (estimates) to illustrate effect of home office
- Using actual-method rent allocation: rent portion ≈ $25,200 * 20.29% ≈ $5,112.
- If the $950 is business-related, total business expenses ≈ $6,062 → Schedule C net profit ≈ $12,500 − $6,062 = $6,438.
- SE tax estimate: net * 0.9235 ≈ $5,944 → SE tax ≈ $5,944 * 15.3% ≈ $909. Half of that (~$454) is an adjustment to income.
- If the $950 is NOT business-related, net profit would be ≈ $7,388 → SE tax and resulting effects are larger but still modest.
- Simplified home-office method would give only $700 instead of ~$5,112, so actual method is likely better for you.

Other important items and next steps
- Documentation: keep rent records, utility bills, receipts for donations, Form 1098‑E for student loan interest paid in 2024 (if any), records/receipts for the $950 expenses and any 1099s you receive.
- Confirm whether the $950 unreimbursed expenses were for the consulting business (deductible on Schedule C) or for your W‑2 job (generally nondeductible).
- Confirm whether you actually paid any student loan interest in 2024 — only interest paid in 2024 can be deducted on your 2024 return.
- Consider estimated tax payments (Form 1040‑ES) if you expect to owe $1,000+ when you file because of self‑employment tax and insufficient withholding.
- If you want, I can:
  - Run a side‑by‑side calculation showing (a) actual home office method vs simplified and (b) itemize vs standard deduction, using precise numbers you confirm (e.g., whether $950 is business-related and any other deductible items).
  - Draft a checklist of documents to gather for filing.

Would you like me to:
- Do a quick scenario calculation (I can use the numbers above and show expected taxable income and approximate federal tax), or
- Draft a documents checklist for filing (W‑2, 1099s, 1098‑E, rent/utility receipts, donation receipts, etc.)?

