Thanks — this is enough to give a clear, practical summary and next steps. I’ll cover (1) what’s likely deductible, (2) whether you should itemize or take the standard deduction, (3) how to treat the home office and self-employment income, (4) what federal (and some state) forms you’ll likely need, and (5) a short list of clarifying questions and next steps.

Quick summary of your numbers (for reference)
- W-2 wages (Riverside Hospital): $79,400
- Consulting (6 clients): $12,500 (self-employment)
- Interest (Goldman Marcus): $920 (likely 1099‑INT)
- TD Ameritrade: $480 (likely dividends/interest or broker 1099)
- Home: 690 sq ft total; 140 sq ft exclusive home office (20.29% of home)
- Rent: $2,100/month → $25,200/year
- Student loan interest (you list $3,600) — see note below
- Charitable cash: $1,400 (you have receipts)
- “Unreimbursed expenses” $950 — need to confirm whether these are for consulting (self-employed) or for your W-2 job

1) What you can deduct (federal) — summary
- Student loan interest: Up to $2,500 of student loan interest paid in the tax year is an adjustment to income (above-the-line) if you qualify. You reported $3,600 — only up to $2,500 is allowed (and the deduction phases out at higher incomes). You need the 1098‑E from MOHELA showing interest paid in the year.
- Charitable contributions: $1,400 cash donations are deductible if you itemize (you have receipts). If you take the standard deduction, you don’t separately deduct them.
- Home office (for your consulting business): Allowed for self-employed taxpayers who use an area of the home regularly and exclusively for business. Because your consulting is self-employed income, you can elect either the simplified method or actual-expense method (details below).
- Business expenses for consulting: Ordinary and necessary expenses related to your consulting (e.g., supplies, business mileage, software, phone portion) are deductible on Schedule C. If the $950 “unreimbursed expenses” are business-related, they belong on Schedule C and are deductible. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job, they are not deductible for most taxpayers (suspended by TCJA).
- Self-employment tax: You must pay self-employment (SE) tax on net self-employment earnings; half of the SE tax is deductible as an adjustment to income.
- Interest/dividends/capital gains from Marcus/TD Ameritrade: Reported via 1099s (1099‑INT, 1099‑DIV, 1099‑B) and taxed on Schedule 1 or Schedule D as applicable.

2) Whether to itemize or take the standard deduction
- For married filing jointly, the 2024 standard deduction is substantially larger than a small set of itemized deductions. Based on the items you listed, your only known itemizable federal deduction is the $1,400 charitable gift (unless you have other itemizable items: mortgage interest, large state/local taxes, large unreimbursed medical expenses >7.5% of AGI, etc.).
- Conclusion (likely): It will almost certainly be better to take the standard deduction on the federal return unless you have significant additional itemizable items (mortgage interest, large state and local taxes, or large medical expenses). The home office and business expenses reduce your self-employment (Schedule C) income and therefore reduce your AGI/taxable income regardless of whether you itemize.
- State (Arizona): Arizona has its own rules. Often the federal standard deduction and state deductions differ; you’ll want to check whether itemizing for state gives an advantage. (Most people still take the federal standard deduction and also take the AZ standard deduction unless they have large state itemized items.)

3) Home office — how to treat it (practical numbers)
- You meet the basic tests for a home office deduction for your consulting (exclusive, regular use, principal place of business).
- Office size share: 140 / 690 = 0.2029 → 20.29% of home.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 per sq ft × 140 sq ft = $700 deduction (easy, no allocation of rent/utilities).
  b) Actual-expense method: allocate a percentage of indirect home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.) to the home office. With rent only:
     - Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200.
     - Rent portion = $25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,115.
     - If you include a share of utilities and renter’s insurance the deduction is larger.
- Effect on business tax: Home office is an ordinary Schedule C expense and reduces net profit from consulting. The home-office deduction cannot exceed the net profit from the business (there are ordering rules); other business expenses are deducted first. Reducing net profit also reduces self-employment tax.
- Practical comparison with your numbers (approximate):
  - If your consulting income is $12,500 and you have $950 other business expenses:
    - Net before home-office = 12,500 − 950 = 11,550.
    - With simplified home-office ($700): net = 10,850.
    - With actual rent allocation (~$5,115): net = 6,435.
    - Self-employment tax and income tax will be materially lower under the actual method because the deduction is larger. (Exact tax change depends on other adjustments and tax brackets.)
- Documentation: keep floor plan/sq-ft records, photos, lease showing rent, receipts for utilities and renter’s insurance, and records showing the expenses allocable to the business.

4) Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main individual return).
- Schedule 1 (additional income/adjustments) — e.g., to report taxable interest, and the student loan interest adjustment.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report consulting income and business expenses, including home office.
- Schedule SE — to calculate self-employment tax.
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction if you qualify; with your small business income you likely qualify for the simple Form 8995 approach (reduces taxable income by up to 20% of qualified business income).
- 1098‑E — student loan interest statement (to substantiate the student loan interest paid and the amount eligible for the $2,500 cap).
- 1099‑INT, 1099‑DIV, 1099‑B — for interest, dividends, broker transactions from Marcus/TD Ameritrade.
- 1099‑NEC / records of payments — if any client paid you $600+ they should have issued a 1099‑NEC; even if they didn’t, you must report the income.
- State return — Arizona Form 140 (or other applicable AZ forms). Check AZ rules for standard vs itemized on the state return.

5) Other tax effects to be aware of
- Self-employment tax: net self-employment earnings are subject to SE tax (~15.3% on net earnings after the 92.35% adjustment). Half of SE tax is deductible on Schedule 1.
- QBI deduction: If you have qualified net profit from the consulting activity, you may get a 20% deduction (Form 8995) subject to rules — with your numbers you’re likely under any income thresholds that complicate the calculation.
- Student loan interest phaseout: The student loan interest deduction phases out at higher incomes; whether you can claim the full $2,500 depends on your modified AGI. Because you’re married filing jointly, the phaseout threshold differs from single filers — check current-year thresholds or ask me to estimate based on spouse income if any.
- Estimated tax payments: If you owe self-employment tax and income tax not covered by withholding, you may need to make estimated tax payments or have underpayment penalties.

6) Documentation to gather (recommended)
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital
- 1099‑INT (Goldman Marcus) and 1099s from TD Ameritrade (and 1099‑B if you sold investments)
- Statements or ledgers showing consulting income (1099‑NEC forms or invoices/receipts)
- Receipts/invoices for business expenses ($950 and any other consulting expenses)
- Lease and rent payments, utility bills, renter’s insurance if using actual home-office method
- 1098‑E for student loan interest paid in the tax year
- Charitable receipts for $1,400 donations
- Any other receipts for deductible items (medical, state/local taxes, mortgage interest if applicable)

7) Clarifying questions I need to give a more precise recommendation or run tax estimates
- Is the $3,600 the amount of student loan interest you actually paid in the tax year (2024)? Or is that an aggregate of earlier years? (Only interest paid in the tax year counts.)
- Are the $950 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses related to the consulting activity, or are they unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job?
- Do you (or your spouse) have any other itemizable items (mortgage interest, big state/local taxes, big medical expenses, large charitable gifts beyond the $1,400) that weren’t listed?
- Does your spouse have income or other deductions we should include when deciding itemize vs standard?
- Do you want me to run estimated federal tax calculations under both home-office methods (simplified vs actual) so you can see the numeric tax effect?

8) Immediate recommendation
- Start by collecting the forms listed above (W-2, 1099s, 1098‑E, receipts).
- Most likely take the federal standard deduction (unless you have large additional itemized expenses). Still, definitely claim your Schedule C business deductions and home-office deduction (actual method probably yields a larger deduction given your rent, but it requires record allocation).
- Consider using tax software (TurboTax, TaxAct, H&R Block) or a CPA, especially if you want to use the actual home-office method and to optimize QBI and student loan phaseouts. If you want, I can walk you through a draft calculation.

