Key conclusions (short)
- Likely better to take the standard deduction (MFJ) rather than itemize Schedule A, because your only clear Schedule A item is $1,400 charity (unless you have significant state/local taxes or mortgage interest not listed).  
- Your consulting income is reported on Schedule C and is subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). You can deduct business expenses on Schedule C — that includes your home office allocation.  
- You can choose simplified or regular method for the home office. With your rent level, the regular method (allocating ~20.29% of rent + utilities) is likely much more beneficial than the $5/sqft simplified method — but you must ensure the office meets the exclusive and principal‑place tests.  
- Student loan interest: you paid $3,600, but the maximum federal student loan interest deduction is $2,500 per year (and it phases out at higher incomes). You’ll need to check MAGI phaseout; given your income, you may be able to claim at least part (or all) of the $2,500.  
- The $950 “unreimbursed expenses” are deductible if they are business expenses for your consulting (Schedule C). If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible under current law (2018–2025 TCJA suspension). Clarify this.

Detailed breakdown and estimates

1) Home office: two methods
- Home office percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%.

A. Simplified method
- Deduction = $5 × 140 sq ft = $700.
- If $950 business expenses (for consulting) are deductible on Schedule C too, total business deductions = $700 + $950 = $1,650.
- Net self‑employment income = $12,500 − $1,650 = $10,850.
- Self‑employment (SE) tax ≈ 15.3% on 92.35% of net → SE tax ≈ $1,533 (approx).
- Deductible half of SE tax (above‑the‑line) ≈ $766.

B. Regular method (likely larger here)
- Rent portion allocable to business = $25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,113.
- Add other allocable costs (a share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.) if applicable. If we just add the $950 business expenses you mentioned, total business deductions ≈ $5,113 + $950 = $6,063.
- Net self‑employment income = $12,500 − $6,063 ≈ $6,437.
- SE tax ≈ $909; half deductible ≈ $455.

Compare: regular method gives a much bigger Schedule C deduction (because of high rent), lowering both income tax and SE tax. Important limits: the home‑office deduction cannot create a business loss from the consulting activity by itself (if the deduction exceeds your consulting gross profit after other business expenses, loss rules and at‑risk/passive activity rules can apply).

2) Student loan interest
- You paid $3,600 but the maximum deductible amount is $2,500 per year (subject to MAGI phaseout). Check your MAGI; with your numbers you’re likely under the phaseout thresholds for complete disallowance, but confirm. The deduction is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (Schedule 1), so it lowers AGI.

