Thanks — I can walk through this and tell you what’s generally deductible, how the home-office rules work, whether you’re likely to itemize, and what forms you’ll need. A couple quick clarifying questions before finalizing numbers:
- The $3,600 in student-loan interest — was that paid in tax year 2024, or were those payments made in 2016–2019 (as written)? The student-loan interest deduction only applies to interest paid in the tax year being filed.
- The $950 “unreimbursed expenses” — were those expenses related to your consulting work (self-employment) or unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job? (Only the former is deductible now.)

Assuming the most likely interpretation (student-loan interest not paid in 2024 unless you confirm otherwise, and the $950 are business expenses for consulting), here’s a practical breakdown and recommendation.

1) Income to report
- W-2 (Riverside Hospital): $79,400 — report on Form 1040 (box wages).
- Self-employment/consulting: $12,500 — report on Schedule C (all income must be reported even if you didn’t get 1099s).
- Interest: $920 (Goldman Marcus) + $480 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,400 — report on Form 1040 (and Schedule B only if interest > $1,500).

2) Self-employment tax and related adjustments
- You must pay self-employment tax on net earnings from the consulting (use Schedule SE). SE tax is about 15.3% on net earnings (after the 0.9235 adjustment), and half of the SE tax is an above-the-line adjustment to income on Form 1040/Schedule 1.

3) Home-office deduction (key points)
- You said the home office is 140 sq ft exclusive in a 690 sq ft home → business-use percentage = 140/690 ≈ 20.3%.
- Because you have self-employment income, you can take a home-office deduction for the consulting activity (it is not available for a W-2 job).
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot, up to 300 sq ft → 140 sq ft × $5 = $700.
  b) Actual-expense method: allocate a percentage (20.3%) of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.) to the business. Example with the figures you gave:
     - Rent $2,100/month = $25,200/year × 20.3% ≈ $5,100 deductible as business expense (plus the share of utilities if you have them).
- Practical comparison: based on rent alone, the actual-expense method yields a much larger deduction (~$5,100) than the simplified ($700). If you use the actual method, you’ll compute the deduction on Form 8829 and carry it to Schedule C.
- Important limitations:
  - The home-office deduction is only for the portion of your home used exclusively and regularly for the business.
  - The business deductions (including home office) can’t create a net business loss that offsets other non-business income in certain cases; if deduction would exceed business income, the excess may be limited and carried forward.
  - The home-office deduction reduces net profit, which lowers both income tax and self-employment tax.

4) Charitable contribution
- $1,400 to Northwest Harvest: deductible if you itemize (keep the receipt). If you take the standard deduction, you cannot separately deduct this gift on your federal return (unless special above-the-line rules apply in the tax year).

5) Student-loan interest
- Up to $2,500 of qualified student-loan interest is an above-the-line deduction (reported on Schedule 1) if you meet income limits and the interest was paid in the tax year. If the $3,600 you listed is interest paid in prior years (2016–2019), it is not deductible for 2024. If you did pay $3,600 in 2024, you can deduct up to $2,500 (subject to income phaseouts). I’ll need to know the year(s) of payment to be certain.

6) Unreimbursed employee expenses
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended under current law) for most employees. If the $950 are business expenses for your consulting, treat them as Schedule C expenses (deductible).

7) Itemize vs standard deduction
- For married filing jointly the standard deduction is typically large (recent years around $27k–$28k). Your likely itemizable federal deductions from the info provided are only $1,400 charitable + any state income taxes/property taxes (you didn’t list any). Because of that, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction rather than itemizing.
- The home-office deduction is taken on Schedule C (reduces business income) and does not force you to itemize—so you can still claim the home-office deduction and take the standard deduction for personal deductions.

8) Likely tax forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 — adjustments to income (e.g., half of self-employment tax, student-loan interest if eligible).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report your consulting income and business expenses (including allocated home-office expenses).
- Form 8829 — if you use the actual expense method to calculate the home-office deduction (allocates rent, utilities, etc.). If you use the simplified method, you don’t use Form 8829; claim the simplified amount on Schedule C.
- Schedule SE — to calculate self-employment tax (Social Security / Medicare).
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (to claim the $1,400 charity and any other itemizable deductions).
- Arizona state return (AZ Form 140) — you’ll need to file Arizona state tax return and follow AZ rules for deductions/credits.

9) Recordkeeping and other practical items
- Keep receipts for the charity (written receipt), documentation of rent payments and utility bills for home-office allocation, records for the $950 business expenses, and any 1099-NEC/1099-INT/1099-DIV and Form W-2 and 1098‑E (student loan interest) you receive.
- If you expect to owe additional tax because of self-employment, consider making estimated quarterly payments or increasing W-2 withholding to avoid penalties.

10) Quick back-of-envelope example (illustrative)
- Total income before business expenses: 79,400 (W-2) + 12,500 (consulting) + 1,400 (interest) = 93,300.
- If consulting expenses = $950 and you use actual home-office rent allocation ≈ $5,109, then net business income ≈ 12,500 − 950 − 5,109 = ≈ $6,441.
- That reduces overall taxable income and self-employment tax. (I can run a more precise tax estimate if you want.)

Next steps I can help with
- If you confirm whether the $3,600 was paid in 2024 and whether the $950 are consulting expenses, I’ll:
  - Recalculate with precise numbers and show estimated federal tax and self-employment tax.
  - Prepare a checklist of exactly which lines/forms to fill and a worksheet you can give to a tax preparer.
  - Draft a summary email or a printable worksheet listing documents to gather for filing.

Would you like me to run the numerical estimate and prepare a one-page worksheet? And please confirm the two clarifying items (student-loan years and nature of the $950).

