I’m not a CPA or tax preparer, but here’s a clear, practical run-through of how your 2024 filing will work, what’s deductible, and which forms you’ll likely need. I also list a few questions at the end so we can refine the advice.

Summary answer (short)
- Most likely you will take the standard deduction (married filing jointly) unless you have substantial additional itemizable items (mortgage interest, large property tax, big state tax withholding, large medical expenses, etc.). With the information you gave, your likely itemized total is well below the standard deduction.
- Your consulting income is self-employment income — report it on Schedule C and pay self-employment tax (Schedule SE). The home office deduction is available only for that self-employment activity (not for your W-2 job).
- Student loan interest is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (up to $2,500) if it was paid in tax year 2024 and you meet the income limits. If the $3,600 was paid in 2016–2019 (not in 2024), it is not deductible on your 2024 return.
- Charitable cash gifts are deductible only if you itemize; if you take the standard deduction you do not separately deduct the $1,400.
- You’ll need Form 1040 plus: Schedule 1, Schedule C, Schedule SE, and possibly Form 8829 (or use the simplified home office method). Also file Arizona state return (AZ Form 140).

Detailed breakdown and calculations

Income
- W-2 wages: $79,400
- Consulting (self-employed): $12,500
