I can walk through this and give practical, general guidance — but I’m not a CPA or attorney. For definitive tax filing decisions, please confirm with a tax professional. Quick summary first, then details and next steps.

Quick summary
- Likely better to take the 2024 standard deduction for married filing jointly (IRS standard deduction for 2024: $29,200) — you’ll almost certainly exceed your itemized deductions with that single number.
- You can deduct qualified student loan interest up to $2,500 (subject to income phaseouts) as an above-the-line adjustment — not an itemized deduction.
- Your consulting income ($12,500) is self-employment income. Report it on Schedule C; pay self-employment tax (Schedule SE). You can deduct ordinary business expenses (including the $950 if they’re business expenses) on Schedule C.
- Home office: because you’re self-employed and you have an exclusive 140 sq ft office in a 690 sq ft rental, you can claim a home-office deduction. The actual-expenses method (apportioning rent/utilities) will likely give a larger deduction than the simplified method.
- Interest from Marcus and TD Ameritrade (total $1,400) is taxable interest — report on Form 1040 (Schedule B only if interest > $1,500).
- Forms you’ll likely need: Form 1040, Schedule 1 (adjustments including student loan interest and half SE tax), Schedule C, Schedule SE, and Form 8829 (if you use actual-expense method for home office). Possibly Form 8995 (QBI) depending on taxable income and whether the consulting income qualifies.

Details and calculations (estimates)
1) Standard vs. itemize
- Standard deduction (MFJ, 2024): $29,200.
- Your known potential itemized deductions: charitable cash $1,400. You didn’t list mortgage interest or property taxes; because you pay rent, you likely have little/no mortgage-interest or property-tax deductions. That makes itemizing unlikely to exceed the standard deduction. Conclusion: probably take the standard deduction.

2) Self-employment / consulting income ($12,500)
- Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Deduct ordinary & necessary business expenses on Schedule C. If the $950 are business expenses for the consulting work, they are deductible on Schedule C. If those $950 are unreimbursed employee expenses (you were an employee, not self-employed), they generally are NOT deductible for tax years since TCJA suspended miscellaneous itemized employee unreimbursed expenses.
- Pay self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) via Schedule SE. You may owe roughly 15.3% on net self-employment earnings (after adjustments — see below). You can deduct half of your SE tax on Schedule 1 (above-the-line).

3) Home office (140 sq ft exclusive space; home = 690 sq ft; rent $2,100/mo)
- Business-use percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5/sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 140 * $5 = $700 deduction.
  - Actual-expense method: allocate the business percentage of allowable home expenses. Example annual rent = $2,100 * 12 = $25,200. Business portion ≈ $25,200 * 20.29% ≈ $5,114 (plus business-share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.). Note: renters cannot depreciate the structure; homeowners can (but you did not indicate owning).
- Because you rent and the business portion of rent alone (~$5,100) is much larger than the simplified $700, the actual method will likely be more valuable — if you meet the “exclusive and regular” use test (you said it’s exclusive).
- To claim actual-expense home-office deductions as a self-employed taxpayer you generally use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home). If you choose simplified, you do not use Form 8829.

4) Student loan interest ($3,600 reported)
- The maximum student loan interest deduction is $2,500 per year (above-the-line on Schedule 1), assuming you meet the AGI limits. The extra $1,100 is not deductible as student loan interest.
- Income (MAGI) phaseouts apply: for married filing jointly the deduction phases out above certain MAGI thresholds. You’ll need to check your MAGI; if your MAGI exceeds the threshold you may get a reduced or zero deduction. Your tax preparer can compute this precisely.
- Lenders typically send Form 1098-E showing interest paid — gather that.

5) Bank interest ($920 + $480 = $1,400)
- Report as interest income on Form 1040. Schedule B only required if total taxable interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500 or other conditions (foreign accounts, etc.). At $1,400, Schedule B is probably not required.

6) Charitable giving ($1,400)
- Deductible only if you itemize (or under special provisions — but generally attached to itemizing). Since your standard deduction will likely be larger, your charitable cash contribution probably won’t reduce tax unless you have other itemizable amounts that you haven’t listed.

7) Other potential considerations
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: you may be eligible for a 20% deduction on qualified business income from the consulting (Form 8995 or 8995-A). Eligibility depends on taxable income and business type; even with relatively small consulting income you might qualify, but calculate after all adjustments.
- Estimated tax payments: if your consulting income had no withholding, you may need to have made quarterly estimated tax payments to avoid underpayment penalties. If not, you may owe estimated taxes when you file.
- Information returns: make sure you receive 1099-NEC (or 1099-MISC) from clients if issued. Banks will send 1099-INT for interest. Loan servicers send 1098-E for student loan interest.

Forms you’re likely to need
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income) — for student loan interest deduction (and half of SE tax)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business)
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the actual-expense method for home office
- Possibly Form 8995 or 8995-A (QBI deduction) depending on taxable income
- Possibly Schedule B (interest/dividends) if interest + dividends > $1,500

What to gather (documents & info)
