Thanks — this is enough to give clear, practical guidance. Short version up front, then details and next steps.

Short summary / bottom line
- Your consulting income (12,500) should be treated as self-employment income and reported on Schedule C. That triggers self-employment tax (Schedule SE) and allows business deductions (including a home-office deduction).
- Home-office deduction: you can choose the simplified method ($5 × 140 sq ft = $700) or the regular method (business % × actual rent/expenses). Regular method will likely give a larger deduction because 140/690 ≈ 20.3% (20.29% × $25,200 annual rent ≈ $5,113), but the deduction is limited by business net income rules — I explain below.
- Student loan interest ($3,600 paid) is potentially deductible as an above-the-line adjustment (up to $2,500 maximum) subject to MAGI phaseouts and filing status limits.
- Charitable gift ($1,400) is deductible only if you itemize. With the information you’ve given, you’ll very likely be better off taking the standard deduction unless you have other itemizable items (large state/local taxes, mortgage interest, bigger charitable giving, big medical expenses).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (the $950) are generally NOT deductible for employees under current law. If those $950 are business expenses for your consulting work, include them on Schedule C instead.
- Forms you will likely need: Form 1040, Schedule 1 (adjustments), Schedule C (profit/loss from business), Schedule SE (self-employment tax), Form 8829 (if you use the regular home-office method), and Schedule A only if you itemize. You may also need Schedule B if interest/dividends exceed the reporting threshold. See details below.

Detailed treatment and steps

1) Income
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital ($79,400): report on Form 1040 (W-2 wages).
- Consulting $12,500 (6 clients): treat as self-employment (Schedule C). Whether you received 1099-NEC or not, you report this income on Schedule C.
- Interest from Goldman Sachs Marcus ($920) and TD Ameritrade ($480): report as interest on Form 1040 (and on Schedule B only if total interest/dividends exceed the Schedule B threshold — generally $1,500; your total interest shown is $1,400 so Schedule B likely not required). If TD Ameritrade amounts include dividends or brokerage sales, include those on the correct 1040 lines/1099-B reporting as applicable.

2) Business (consulting) expenses
- Include ordinary and necessary expenses on Schedule C (e.g., supplies, software, phone, the $950 if those are business-related).
- Self-employment tax: you’ll pay SE tax on net earnings from self-employment (Schedule SE). You may deduct half of the self-employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

3) Home-office deduction (self-employed only)
- Eligibility: must use the space exclusively and regularly for business. You said 140 sq ft exclusive — that sounds to qualify.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5 × 140 sq ft = $700 deduction (no Form 8829).
  - Regular: prorate actual home expenses by business percentage. Your business percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%. Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200. Business portion of rent alone ≈ $5,113. You can also prorate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. The regular method typically gives the larger deduction here.
- Limitation: the regular home-office deduction cannot exceed net profit from the business (i.e., you can’t create a Schedule C loss solely from home-office deductions that exceed your self-employment gross profit once other business deductions are taken). There can be carryover rules for certain home-office expense excesses; if your total business deductions would create a loss, the excess may carry forward (Form 8829 handles computation). The simplified method doesn’t create depreciation complications and is simpler.
- Form to use: Form 8829 to compute the regular home-office deduction and attach to Schedule C. If you use the simplified method, you just enter the simplified deduction on Schedule C.

4) Student loan interest ($3,600)
- The student loan interest deduction is an above-the-line deduction (reported on Schedule 1) up to the statutory limit (commonly $2,500 maximum). The actual allowable amount may be reduced or phased out depending on your modified adjusted gross income (MAGI) and filing status (phaseout rules apply for married filing jointly over certain MAGI thresholds). With your combined income (W-2 + other items), you may be partially or fully phased out — check the MAGI threshold or ask a preparer to confirm. Keep your MOHELA statements/1098-E (if available) showing interest paid.

5) Charitable donation ($1,400)
- Only deductible if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction (most likely with only $1,400 charitable and no mortgage interest), you will not deduct this on federal return. Keep the receipt for records.

6) Unreimbursed employee expenses ($950)
- If these are unreimbursed expenses as an employee of Riverside Hospital, under current tax law those are not deductible for most taxpayers (the miscellaneous itemized deduction for unreimbursed employee expenses was suspended). If these are business expenses for your consulting work, include them on Schedule C.

7) Other considerations
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: you may be eligible for a 20% QBI deduction on qualified business income from your consulting (subject to taxable income thresholds and other rules). This is computed on Form 8995 or 8995-A. If your taxable income is well under the high thresholds, you may be eligible for the full QBI deduction. It’s worth checking.
- State taxes (Arizona): AZ rules differ in details. Standard vs itemize choices and some adjustments vary. If you have significant state tax withholding or paid state income tax, that affects SALT deduction up to the federal $10,000 cap when itemizing (if you itemize).
- Recordkeeping: keep W-2, all 1099s, 1098-E (student loan interest), receipts for charitable gifts, documentation for home-office (floor plan, measurement, photos), and records for business expenses.

8) Forms you will likely file
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income — e.g., student loan interest, half SE tax)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $12,500 and business expenses (including home-office deduction from Form 8829 if using regular method)
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if using the regular home-office method
- Form 8995 or 8995-A (QBI) — if claiming the qualified business income deduction
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (to claim the $1,400 charity and any other itemized deductions)
- Schedule B — only if interest/dividends exceed threshold or if you have certain foreign accounts
- Arizona state return forms

Estimate example (illustrative only)
- Rough illustration to show the mechanics (these are approximate; I can run precise math if you want): using regular home-office allocation on rent only:
  - Gross income = 79,400 (W-2) + 12,500 (consulting) + 1,400 (interest) = 93,300
  - If consulting business expenses include home-office ≈ $5,113 + $950 other = $6,063 → net consulting = 12,500 − 6,063 = 6,437
  - Self-employment tax on net consulting ≈ ~$900 (you’d report exact on Schedule SE) and you deduct half (~$450) above-the-line
  - Student loan interest deduction likely capped at $2,500 (check MAGI)
  - With these numbers, you’ll almost certainly take the standard deduction at the federal level unless you have additional itemizable items.

Recommendations / next steps
1. Decide home-office method: if you want, I can calculate the exact comparison (simplified vs regular) using your full home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc.) and show whether regular yields a bigger deduction and whether it’s limited by business net income.
2. If you want an estimated tax bill or refund amount, I can run a full rough calculation showing AGI → taxable income → federal tax → self-employment tax and estimate Arizona tax.
3. If you want, I can draft a short message/email to a CPA with the facts and request guidance/confirmation (I can draft it and you can send it).

Important legal note
I’m not a CPA or tax attorney. This is general guidance based on current federal rules as of my knowledge cutoff. Because of MAGI phaseouts, state specifics, and QBI complexity, I recommend you confirm with a tax professional or use tax-preparation software to compute exact amounts and ensure correct filing.

