Thanks — I can walk through this and give practical next steps. I’ll summarize what’s deductible, show how to treat the home office, estimate the self‑employment tax effect, explain itemizing vs the standard deduction, list the federal forms you’ll likely need, and flag items I need you to confirm so I can run exact numbers.

High‑level summary
- Your W-2 wages: $79,400.
- Self‑employment consulting: $12,500 → report on Schedule C (unless this income is reported elsewhere).
- Interest: $920 + $480 = $1,400 → report as interest income (1099‑INT).
- Home office: 140 sq ft exclusive, home = 690 sq ft, rent $2,100/month → you can take a home‑office deduction on Schedule C (regular or simplified method).
- Charitable gift $1,400 → deductible only if you itemize (not an above‑the‑line deduction).
- Student loan interest $3,600 — only interest paid in the tax year is potentially deductible, and the student loan interest deduction has a legislated annual limit and income phaseouts (see below).
- “Unreimbursed expenses” ($950) — whether deductible depends on what they are: unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job are generally not deductible through 2025; if these are business expenses for your consulting work, they are deductible on Schedule C.

Home office: how to treat it (and a quick calculation)
- You’re self‑employed with an exclusive 140 sq ft home office out of 690 sq ft. Office percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%.
- Two methods:
  1. Regular method (usually better here): allocate actual home expenses to the business. For rent: $2,100 × 12 = $25,200 annual rent × 20.29% ≈ $5,113 deductible on Schedule C as a business expense. You can also allocate utilities and renter’s insurance the same way if you pay them.
  2. Simplified method: $5.00 × 140 sq ft = $700 maximum deduction (easier, no Form 8829).  
- If you use the regular method you complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and transfer the result to Schedule C. If simplified, skip 8829 and report the simplified amount on Schedule C.

Self‑employment income, SE tax, and QBI
- Net Schedule C profit = consulting revenue ($12,500) minus allowable business expenses (home office allocation and any other Schedule C expenses). If your $950 is actually business expenses, include it; if it’s unreimbursed W‑2 expenses, don’t include it.
- Example (if $950 is business expense): net profit ≈ 12,500 − 5,113 − 950 = 6,437.
- Self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) is ~15.3% applied to 92.35% of net profit. With net profit ≈ $6,437:
  - SE tax ≈ 15.3% × 0.9235 × 6,437 ≈ $900–$950 (rough estimate).
  - You get an above‑the‑line deduction for one‑half of SE tax (≈ $450) when computing AGI.
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: you may be eligible for a 20% deduction on qualified business income from the Schedule C (subject to limitations). With a small QBI (e.g., ~$6.4k) you'd likely get ~20% × QBI ≈ $1,200 reduction (use Form 8995 or 8995‑A).

Student loan interest ($3,600)
- Only interest you actually paid in the tax year is potentially deductible, and the usual maximum deduction is $2,500 per year (subject to MAGI phaseouts). If your $3,600 is interest paid across many years (2016–2019), that does not make it deductible now — only 2024 payments matter for the 2024 return.
- The deduction is an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1), not an itemized deduction. If your 2024 MAGI is below the phaseout range for the student loan deduction, you can generally take up to $2,500.

Charitable donation ($1,400)
- Charitable cash donations are deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. If you take the standard deduction you can’t deduct this $1,400 on the federal return.
- For most taxpayers with limited other itemizable items, the standard deduction is larger than $1,400, so you’ll usually take the standard deduction. See the “Itemize vs standard” section below.

Unreimbursed employee expenses ($950)
- If these are unreimbursed expenses related to a W‑2 employer job, they are not deductible under current law (TCJA, through 2025). If they are ordinary and necessary expenses of your self‑employment business, include them on Schedule C.

Itemize vs standard deduction
