- If the $12,500 is self-employment/consulting (reported on 1099-NEC or as miscellaneous income), report it on Schedule C. Business expenses (home office share, supplies, travel, phone portion, and any bona fide business expenses) reduce net profit on Schedule C.
- The $950 you called “unreimbursed expenses”: if these are expenses you paid in the course of your consulting work, they are deductible on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses (i.e., expenses for a job where you were an employee), those are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law (with very limited exceptions).
