Summary (high level)
- Most likely outcome: take the standard deduction (married filing jointly) rather than itemizing, based on the items you listed.
- You should report the $12,500 consulting as self‑employment income on Schedule C and can claim a home‑office deduction for the 140 sq ft exclusive office (either simplified or actual method).
- Student loan interest may be an above‑the‑line deduction (subject to the usual $2,500 limit and income phaseouts) — confirm the $3,600 is interest paid in the tax year you’re filing.
- The $950 labeled “unreimbursed expenses” can only be deducted if they are business expenses related to your consulting (report on Schedule C). Unreimbursed employee expenses for W‑2 work are not deductible for tax years after 2017.
- You’ll likely owe self‑employment tax on the net consulting profit (Schedule SE), and you may get a deduction for half of that SE tax.

Detailed breakdown and numbers (estimate using the amounts you gave)
1) Income to report
- W‑2 wages: $79,400
- Consulting (self‑employed): $12,500 (report on Schedule C)
- Interest: $920 + $480 = $1,400 (report as interest income on Form 1040; Schedule B only needed if interest > $1,500)

Total reported income ≈ $79,400 + $12,500 + $1,400 = $93,300 Gross; after Schedule C expenses (below) AGI will be lower.

2) Student loan interest
- The student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line deduction (reduces AGI), maximum $2,500 per year (subject to MAGI phaseouts). You reported $3,600 — if that $3,600 is interest paid in the tax year, you can generally deduct up to $2,500 (if you’re under the income phaseout limits). You need Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer (MOHELA) showing interest paid in the tax year.

3) Home office
- Because your consulting is self‑employment, you can take the home office deduction if the space is used regularly and exclusively for the business.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → for 140 sq ft that’s $700.
  b) Actual: prorate actual home expenses by business use percentage. Your business percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.3%. If rent = $2,100/month → annual rent $25,200 → rent share ≈ $5,112 (plus a share of utilities if applicable). The actual method generally gives a larger deduction here.
- The home office deduction is taken on Schedule C. If you use the actual method and have depreciation, you’ll use Form 8829 to compute the allowable deduction. Note: the allowable home‑office deduction cannot exceed your net profit from that business (there are special carryover rules for excess depreciation); with your numbers you should still have positive business income.

4) Other business expenses
- The $950 you listed as “unreimbursed expenses” — if these are business expenses related to your consulting (supplies, phone, internet portion, mileage, etc.) they belong on Schedule C and reduce your business net income. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job, they are not deductible under current law.

5) Self‑employment tax and other adjustments
- Your net Schedule C profit (example with actual home office + $950 expenses):  
  Consulting 12,500 − (home office ≈ 5,112 + business expenses 950) ≈ net profit ≈ $6,438.
- Self‑employment tax (approx): SE tax ≈ 15.3% on ~92.35% of net profit → roughly $900–$1,000 (estimate). Half of SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction (reduces AGI).
- You can also deduct qualified retirement plan contributions (SEP, Solo 401(k), IRA) and half of SE tax — these further lower taxable income if you make them.

6) Itemize vs standard deduction
- Based on what you listed, your deductible itemizable expenses look small (charitable $1,400; maybe some state taxes withheld, but you didn’t mention mortgage interest, large medical expenses, or large SALT). The 2024 standard deduction for married filing jointly is about $29k (inflation‑adjusted). It’s almost certain the standard deduction will exceed your likely itemized total, so standard deduction is probably the better choice.
- If you have additional deductible items you didn’t list (large mortgage interest, big state/local taxes, large unreimbursed medical > 7.5% of AGI, larger charitable gifts), re‑check itemizing.

7) Forms you’ll likely need
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 (individual return)
  - Schedule 1 (for adjustments like student loan interest and the deductible half of SE tax) — in some years adjustments are captured directly; the current 1040 packaging may vary but conceptually these adjustments are reported.
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $12,500 and business expenses (home office, $950, etc.)
  - Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
  - Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you choose the actual expense method for the home office
  - Form 8995 or 8995‑A (Qualified Business Income deduction) if you want to claim the QBI 20% deduction for qualified self‑employment income (you may qualify; small business rules apply)
  - Schedule A only if you itemize (probably you won’t)
  - 1099‑NEC(s) or records of payments from consulting clients — you must report income even if you didn’t receive 1099s
  - Form 1098‑E from MOHELA for student loan interest (to support deduction)
  - 1099‑INTs from Goldman Sachs and TD Ameritrade for the interest income
- State:
  - Arizona resident return (Arizona Form 140 or whatever the current AZ resident form is for the year) — you must file state return for AZ.

Practical next steps / checklist
1. Gather documents: W‑2, 1099‑INTs, any 1099‑NEC or records of consulting payments, Form 1098‑E from MOHELA for student loan interest, receipts for charitable gifts (Northwest Harvest receipt), receipts for the $950 business expenses, rent statements, records showing square footage.
2. Decide home office method: simplified (easier, small deduction) vs actual (more work, likely larger deduction here).
3. Prepare Schedule C with consulting income and expenses (including home office). Compute SE tax on Schedule SE, deduct half SE tax on Schedule 1, and apply student loan interest deduction (if eligible) on Schedule 1.
4. Compare total itemized deductions vs the standard deduction; choose the larger (likely standard).
5. File federal 1040 and Arizona return. Consider estimated tax/withholdings for next year if you’ll continue consulting (to avoid underpayment penalties).

A few clarifying questions
- Is the $3,600 student‑loan interest the amount you paid during the tax year you’re filing (e.g., 2024) and do you have Form 1098‑E for that year? (You can only deduct interest actually paid during the filing year.)
- Are the $950 expenses business expenses for your consulting (receipts, supplies, mileage, etc.), or were they unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job?
