Thanks — I can walk you through this. Below is a concise, practical breakdown of what’s deductible, whether you should itemize, how to treat the home office, and which federal/state forms you’ll likely need. I also list a few clarifying questions so I can be more precise.

Summary / quick answer
- Likely take the standard deduction (married filing jointly) because the itemizable amounts you reported ($1,400 charitable) are well below the 2024 MFJ standard deduction.  
- Your consulting income should be reported as self‑employment income on Schedule C; that makes you eligible for a home‑office deduction (and subject to self‑employment tax).  
- Student‑loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction (up to $2,500, subject to income limits) — not an itemized deduction.  
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (the $950) generally are NOT deductible on the federal return for tax years 2018–2025. If those $950 are business expenses for your consulting work, they should be on Schedule C and would be deductible.  
- Use the actual‑expense method for the home office if you want to allocate a share of rent/utilities (likely better here than the simplified method given your rent level), but the home‑office deduction is limited to your net business profit.

Details

1) Standard deduction vs. itemize
- For 2024 the standard deduction for married filing jointly (MFJ) is substantially higher than typical small itemized amounts. Based on the amounts you gave (charitable = $1,400), you are almost certainly better off taking the standard deduction rather than itemizing on Schedule A.  
- Note: student‑loan interest is an adjustment to income (Schedule 1) and doesn’t affect the itemize vs standard calculation.

2) What of your listed items are deductible and where
- W‑2 income from Riverside Hospital ($79,400): report on Form 1040 (W‑2).  
- Consulting income ($12,500): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). If you received 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, include them; even if you didn’t receive a 1099, you must report the income.  
- Interest from Marcus ($920) and TD Ameritrade ($480): report as interest/dividend income on Form 1040. If total interest/dividends > $1,500 or other criteria apply, use Schedule B. (Your combined $1,400 is below the $1,500 Schedule B threshold.)  
- Student loan interest ($3,600 reported): the student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line deduction on Schedule 1 (Form 1040) up to $2,500 per year, subject to MAGI phaseouts. If you truly paid $3,600 in 2024, you can only deduct up to $2,500 (subject to income limits). If the $3,600 refers to interest paid in prior years, it only matters for those years. Confirm the tax year the payments were made.  
- Charitable gifts ($1,400 with receipt): deductible if you itemize on Schedule A. If you take the standard deduction, you cannot separately deduct these on federal return (no 2024 universal above‑the‑line charity deduction).  
- Unreimbursed expenses ($950): if these are employee unreimbursed expenses from an employer, they are not deductible federally under current law. If they are business expenses for your consulting (self‑employed) work, include them on Schedule C.

3) Home‑office treatment
- You can take a home‑office deduction only if the space is used regularly and exclusively for business. You said 140 sq ft exclusive; that qualifies if it is your principal place of business for the consulting.  
- Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5.00 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 140 sq ft × $5 = $700.
  - Actual (recommended likely for you): allocate actual home expenses by percentage of home used for business. Your percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%. Example with rent: $2,100/mo × 12 = $25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,112 deductible portion of rent. You may also allocate a share of utilities, renters insurance, etc. Use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the actual method and file Schedule C.  
- The home office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit. The deduction cannot create or increase a Schedule C loss for tax‑limitation reasons; excess home‑office expense carryforward rules may apply if your business has a loss.

4) Self‑employment tax and other effects
- Your net consulting profit (Schedule C) is subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE) — roughly 15.3% on net earnings (with an adjustment for one‑half of SE tax on Schedule 1).  
- You may be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Form 8995 or 8995‑A) which can shelter up to 20% of qualified business income — eligibility and amount depend on taxable income and type of business. Given your W‑2 of $79k plus consulting income, you may still qualify, but it’s worth modeling.

5) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual return)  
- Schedule 1 (additional income & adjustments) — for student loan interest adjustment, etc.  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report consulting income and business expenses  
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax (and related deduction on Schedule 1)  
- Form 8829 — if you use actual expenses for the home office (filed with Schedule C)  
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — for QBI deduction if applicable  
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (unlikely given current numbers)  
- Schedule B — only if your interest/dividend situations require it (usually if > $1,500 total or other special circumstances)  
- Arizona state return (AZ Form 140 or the current AZ individual return form for the year) — state rules differ; some disallowed federal adjustments may be treated differently by Arizona.

6) Practical examples & quick math (illustrative)
- Home office allocation: 140/690 = 20.29%. Rent portion deductible (actual method) = $25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,112. That exceeds the simplified $700, so actual method would likely give you a larger deduction — assuming you have no other limitations and the expenses are attributable to the business.  
- Rough net consulting profit if you use actual method and include $950 business expenses: 12,500 − 5,112 − 950 ≈ $6,438 net profit (subject to SE tax). If you used simplified method: 12,500 − 700 − 950 = $10,850 net.

7) Action items / questions for you (so I can be more exact)
- Does the $3,600 student loan interest reflect payments made in tax year 2024 (the year you’re filing for)? Or is that a total from earlier years?  
- Are the $950 unreimbursed expenses related to your consulting work (business expenses) or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from an employer?  
- Do you have any state income tax or property tax paid in 2024 (from W‑2 withholding or other payments)? That could increase itemizable deductions if sizable.  
- Did you receive any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC/1099‑INT/1099‑DIV/1099‑B? (If so, gather them.)  
- Do you want me to run a quick estimated calculation of taxable income / self‑employment tax / compare standard vs. itemized using any other numbers you have (state tax paid, mortgage interest, medical expenses, etc.)?

8) Caveats / recommendation
- This is general guidance. Tax law has income phaseouts and limits (particularly for the student‑loan interest deduction and QBI deduction) that can materially change results. For precise calculations (tax due, exact QBI eligibility, and state specifics for Arizona), I recommend either using tax software that walks through the forms or consulting a CPA/tax preparer, especially since you have both W‑2 and self‑employment income.

If you want, I can:
- run a quick worksheet showing estimated taxable income, SE tax, and whether actual or simplified home‑office is better using the numbers you provided; or
- draft a checklist of documents to gather (W‑2, 1099s, mortgage/rent statements, utility bills, receipts) before filing or meeting a preparer.

Which would you prefer?

