Thanks — that’s enough to give a practical overview. I’ll cover (1) what is and isn’t deductible here, (2) whether you’ll likely itemize or take the standard deduction, (3) how to treat the home-office and other self-employment items, (4) forms you’ll use, and (5) recommended next steps and questions I need to finalize numbers.

1) What you can (likely) deduct
- Consulting (self-employment) expenses: Any ordinary and necessary business expenses for your consulting work are deductible on Schedule C. That includes supplies, phone/Internet (business portion), mileage, professional fees, etc. You listed $950 — if those relate to consulting, they’re deductible on Schedule C.
- Home-office deduction: You said the office is exclusive and regular (140 sq ft out of 690 sq ft). Because you have self-employment income from consulting, you can take a home-office deduction on Schedule C. Two options:
  - Actual-expense method: allocate a percentage of your rent and allowable home expenses to the office. Allocation = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.3%. With rent $2,100/month → $25,200/year → 20.3% of rent ≈ $5,110. You can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. This generally gives a larger deduction than the simplified method.
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × square footage = $700 (140 × $5).
  Note: the home-office deduction cannot exceed your net profit from that business (excess may carry over per Form 8829 rules).
- Self-employment tax / SE adjustment: You must pay self-employment tax on net self-employment earnings (Schedule SE). You may deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income (above-the-line).
- Student loan interest: Up to $2,500 of student loan interest is an above-the-line deduction (reported on Schedule 1), subject to MAGI phaseouts. If your MAGI is above the phaseout range you may not get the full or any deduction. (You mentioned $3,600 — the maximum deductible in a year is $2,500; only interest actually paid in the tax year counts.)
- Charitable contribution: Your $1,400 gift to Northwest Harvest is deductible on Schedule A (itemized deduction) if it’s to a qualified charity and you itemize.
- Bank/broker income: $920 (Marcus) and $480 (TD Ameritrade) are interest/dividend income (reported on 1099-INT/1099-DIV). This is taxable income and included on your return.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: For W-2 work, miscellaneous unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible under current law (suspended through 2025) unless you are in a specific category of employee (very limited). If the $950 are business expenses for your consulting work, report them on Schedule C instead.

