- Is the $480 from TD Ameritrade interest, dividends, or something else (they’ll show on 1099 you receive)?
- Does your spouse have any income or tax documents to add? (You said married filing jointly.)
- Any mortgage interest, property tax, state income tax paid, or other charitable gifts not listed?

What you can deduct (summary)
- W-2 wages: not a deduction, but report on Form 1040 (Box amounts from your W-2).
- Self-employment (consulting) income: report gross receipts on Schedule C. You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (including a home‑office deduction if you qualify). If you received 1099-NEC/1099-MISC from clients, include them — but you must report all income even if you didn’t get a 1099.
- Interest from savings/brokerage (Goldman Sachs $920 + TD Ameritrade $480): taxable interest/dividends — report on Form 1040. If total interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500 for the year you’d use Schedule B; otherwise you can report directly on Form 1040.
- Student loan interest: deductible as an adjustment to income (above-the-line) up to $2,500 (subject to MAGI phaseouts and eligibility rules). You cannot deduct interest paid in earlier years — only interest paid in the tax year. Married filing separately generally disallows this deduction.
- Charitable contributions: $1,400 cash to a qualified charity — deductible as an itemized deduction on Schedule A (if you itemize). Keep the receipt.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses ($950): generally NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025 because miscellaneous itemized deductions subject to the 2% floor were suspended by the TCJA. If those $950 are actually self-employed business expenses, they belong on Schedule C and are deductible there.
- Self-employment tax: you’ll owe self-employment tax on net profit; half of the self-employment tax is an above-the-line deduction on Schedule 1.

Itemize vs. standard deduction
- Standard deduction (married filing jointly) is usually quite large and will often exceed small itemized totals. Because the only itemized amount you listed is $1,400 (charitable) and (likely nondeductible) unreimbursed expenses, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction unless you have significant other deductible items (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes up to the $10,000 SALT cap, large medical expenses, etc.).
- Important: student loan interest is an adjustment to income (Schedule 1) and does not affect the itemize-vs-standard decision.

Home office treatment (eligibility and numbers)
- You can claim a home office deduction only if a part of your home is used regularly and exclusively for business and it is your principal place of business (or used to meet clients, etc.). From what you wrote (140 sq ft exclusive), it sounds like you meet the exclusive/regular use test for your consulting.
- Two methods:
  1. Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For 140 sq ft → 140 × $5 = $700 (easy, no Form 8829).
  2. Regular method: pro‑rata share of actual home expenses. You gave rent $2,100/month → $25,200/year. Business-use % = 140 / 690 = 20.29% ≈ 20.3%. Home-office portion of rent = 25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,112. Under the regular method you can also allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., to the business share, which can increase the deduction.
- Limitations:
  - The home-office deduction (regular method) cannot exceed your net business income from that activity (there are rules for carrying forward disallowed amounts). If your Schedule C net profit (before the home-office deduction) is small or zero, some or all of the deduction may be limited or carried forward.
  - The simplified method has no such income limitation (but the total is usually smaller).
- Practical note with your numbers: If you take the regular method (~$5,100), that could significantly reduce your Schedule C net income (12,500 gross − ~5,100 = ~7,400 before other expenses), lowering both income tax and self-employment tax. But if you have other business expenses, and if the home-office deduction would create a loss, parts may be limited and carried over. If you prefer simplicity and have relatively little other business activity, simplified method might be easier though smaller.

Self-employment tax and other effects
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). You then deduct one-half of self-employment tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1.
- You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A) of up to 20% of qualified business income — this is calculated on Form 8995 or 8995‑A and depends on taxable income and other factors.

Forms you’ll likely need
