I can walk through this and give a clear, practical recommendation — but I’m not a CPA or tax preparer, so consider this guidance educational. If you want firm tax filing advice, share these numbers with a tax pro or I can help draft a message to one.

Summary of your numbers (as you gave them)
- W-2 (Riverside Hospital): $79,400
- Consulting income (6 clients): $12,500 — likely self-employment (Schedule C) unless these were W-2 payments
- Interest: Goldman Sachs Marcus $920 + TD Ameritrade $480 = $1,400
- Home office: 140 sq ft exclusive space; home is 690 sq ft; rent $2,100/month ($25,200/year)
- Charitable cash donation: $1,400 (with receipt)
- Student loan interest: $3,600 paid to MOHELA (you wrote 2016–2019 — please confirm whether any of that was paid in tax year 2024)
- "Unreimbursed expenses" $950 — need to confirm whether these are business expenses for your consulting or unreimbursed employee expenses from an employer

What is deductible (federal) — quick rules
- Self-employment/business expenses: If your consulting income is self-employment (1099 or independent contractor), ordinary and necessary business expenses are deductible on Schedule C. That includes supplies, phone portion, mileage, and the home-office deduction (see below). If those $950 are business expenses for consulting, they are deductible on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses (for a W-2 job), they are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law (with limited exceptions).
- Home-office deduction: Available only if you’re self-employed (or a gig worker) who uses part of your home exclusively and regularly for business. Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5.00 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → for 140 sq ft that is $700.
  - Actual (pro rata) method: allocate your rent and eligible home expenses by the office fraction (140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%). On rent alone: $25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,110. (You can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., which would increase the deduction.)
  - Note: the home-office deduction is taken on Schedule C and effectively reduces net self-employment income. There are rules if the deduction would create a loss — we can check that after seeing business expenses and gross receipts.
- Self-employment tax: If you’re self-employed you’ll owe SE tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net earnings. You pay Schedule SE; half of the SE tax is an above-the-line deduction on Form 1040 (reported on Schedule 1).
- Student loan interest: The maximum student loan interest deduction is $2,500 for the year (an adjustment to income), subject to income phaseouts. It is only for interest paid in the tax year (e.g., 2024 payments are deductible on your 2024 return). If your $3,600 were paid in earlier years (2016–2019) they do not create a 2024 deduction — clarify the year(s) of payment.
- Charitable donations: Cash donations are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). For 2024, you can’t deduct them unless you itemize (there is no universal above-the-line cash charity deduction for 2024).

Itemize vs. standard deduction
- Standard deduction (2024 tax year, married filing jointly): $29,200.
- Your visible potential itemized deduction right now:
  - Charitable: $1,400
  - State/local taxes (SALT): you can deduct up to $10,000, but you need to know how much AZ state income tax or property tax you paid in 2024
  - Mortgage interest / medical expenses: none provided
- With only $1,400 in clear charitable deductions (and no large mortgage interest or big state tax payments given), you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction of $29,200. The home-office and business expenses reduce your adjusted gross income via Schedule C (not via itemizing) — so they don’t change the itemize vs standard comparison directly.

Estimated quick calculation examples
(These are illustrative — I used your numbers and two home-office methods; I assumed the $950 are business expenses and ignored other potential deductions/withholding):

A) If you choose the simplified home-office method ($700)
- Self-employment net profit = 12,500 − 950 − 700 = 10,850
- SE tax ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% × 10,850 ≈ $1,532
- Half SE tax deductible ≈ $766
- AGI ≈ 79,400 (W-2) + 1,400 (interest) + 10,850 (net SE) − 766 (half SE tax) = ≈ $90,884
- Less standard deduction 29,200 → taxable income ≈ $61,684

B) If you use actual home-office allocation (rent portion ≈ $5,110)
- Self-employment net profit = 12,500 − 950 − 5,110 = 6,440
- SE tax ≈ 0.153 × 0.9235 × 6,440 ≈ $910
- Half SE tax deductible ≈ $455
- AGI ≈ 79,400 + 1,400 + 6,440 − 455 = ≈ $86,785
- Less standard deduction 29,200 → taxable income ≈ $57,585

Takeaway: using the actual method (allocating rent) may give you a materially larger deduction than the simplified method in your case, because rent is high relative to square footage used for office. But actual method requires documenting rent allocation and any utilities/other home expenses you use in allocation.

Other notes
- QBI (Qualified Business Income) deduction: your net self-employment income may qualify for a 20% QBI deduction (Form 8995). That reduces taxable income further. Whether it applies and how much depends on the business type and your taxable income; we can calculate if you’d like.
- 1099s and withholding: ensure you gather any 1099-NEC/1099-MISC for consulting, Form 1098-E (student loan interest) for 2024 if applicable, and Form 1099-INT for interest. If you didn’t receive 1099s but earned the income, it’s still taxable and reportable.
- State return: you’ll also file Arizona state return (Form 140 for full-year AZ residents). AZ rules follow federal with some differences; state taxes paid may affect itemizing (SALT cap applies federally).

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main individual return)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report consulting income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for above-the-line adjustments (e.g., half of SE tax, student loan interest)
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — if you claim the QBI deduction (20% of qualified business income)
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (charitable donations, SALT up to $10k, mortgage interest, etc.)
- Arizona Form 140 (state resident return) — plus any state schedules required

What I need from you to be definitive
1. Were the $12,500 consulting payments as an independent contractor (1099) / self-employed? Or were they reported on W-2s? (If self-employed, I’ll treat them as Schedule C income.)
2. Are the $950 unreimbursed expenses business expenses for the consulting work? If yes, give a quick breakdown (office supplies, mileage, software, etc.)
3. Did you actually pay $3,600 in student loan interest in 2024? Or were those payments earlier (2016–2019)? If 2024, do you have Form 1098-E from MOHELA showing the interest paid in 2024?
4. Do you have notable state tax withholding or property tax payments in 2024 you want to count toward itemizing? (SALT cap applies.)
5. Do you want me to run a more precise tax estimate with these numbers (including federal tax, SE tax, and an estimate of whether you owe estimated taxes)?

If you confirm the above, I can:
- Run a precise taxable-income and estimated tax calculation and show federal tax owed/refund
- Calculate whether actual vs simplified home-office method is better for you (including utilities if you provide them)
- Draft a neat summary you can give to your CPA, or draft an email to request clarification from MOHELA / your clients / a CPA

Which would you like next?

