- “Unreimbursed expenses”: $950 — see clarification

What’s deductible (high level)
- Consulting income (self‑employment) is reported on Schedule C. Ordinary and necessary business expenses for that work are deductible on Schedule C (including a home‑office share, office supplies, business mileage, phone/internet portion, etc.).
- Home office: deductible for self‑employed taxpayers only. Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft.
  - Actual: allocate a percentage of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance portion, etc.) equal to office sq ft / total sq ft and deduct that share.
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll owe Social Security/Medicare on net self‑employment income; you calculate it on Schedule SE. You can deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income (above‑the‑line).
- Interest from Marcus and TD Ameritrade is taxable (report on 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV).
- Charitable cash gift ($1,400) is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A).
- Student loan interest: deductible as an adjustment to income up to $2,500 for qualifying interest paid during the tax year (phaseouts apply by MAGI). You can only deduct interest actually paid during the year for the loan — not past years’ payments. You should get a Form 1098‑E from MOHELA for interest paid in the tax year.
- “Unreimbursed employee expenses” for W‑2 jobs are NOT deductible for tax years after 2017 (unless you’re in a very limited category of employees). If the $950 is business‑related to your consulting (self‑employment), include it on Schedule C; if it’s for the W‑2 job, it’s generally not deductible.

