- Allocated rent = $25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,112 (you could also allocate a portion of utilities, internet, renter’s insurance if applicable which increases the deduction)
- Other business expenses = $950
- Total business expenses ≈ 6,062 → Net self‑employment income = 12,500 − 6,062 = 6,438
- Net subject to SE tax ≈ 6,438 × 0.9235 ≈ 5,945
- Self‑employment tax ≈ 5,945 × 15.3% ≈ $909 → half deductible ≈ $454

Effect on taxable income (high‑level)
- Using actual method gives a much larger business deduction (mainly because of rent allocation), which lowers AGI and therefore taxable income. Based on the rough math above, actual method reduces your AGI by a few thousand more than the simplified method, so it’s generally preferable here (provided you have documentation and/or additional allocable expenses like utilities).
- Either way, self‑employment expenses are above‑the‑line reductions (reported on Schedule C and Schedule SE), so they help even if you take the standard deduction.

Itemize vs standard deduction
